window.CATTO_INDICE = [{"t":"Downloads","d":"Page","u":"/en/descargas","x":"Downloads Documents and files to download. Click an item to download it. 📄 Curriculum Vitae — Catto PDF · 2 pages · in Spanish ~75 KB ↓ ↓ … 📖 Aprenda a Hablar (sin hacer preguntas) PDF · in Spanish · “Learn to Speak (without asking questions)” · written four-handed by Catto and Claude Code 🤖 ~190 KB ↓ ↓ … Donations empuja al chancho a las estrellas 🐷⭐ Pesos catto.ar.mp copy Mercado Pago (Argentina) · account dedicated to donations. push the pig to the stars 🐷⭐ US dollars venus.abstraido.inca copy Thanks for supporting the project! 🚀"},{"t":"Electronics, from technician to engineer","d":"Page","u":"/en","x":"Electronics, from technician to engineer Two complete curricula developed topic by topic: the Electronics Technician program (years 4–7 of technical secondary school) and the Electronic Engineering degree at the UTN (Argentina's National Technological University). Theory, animated diagrams, worked examples, lab practice and self-assessment. 🔍 × Technician 4 years 17 subjects 95 topics 88 pages Engineering 6 levels 37 subjects 321 core topics 321 pages \"Omnes sumus in canale, pauci autem ad astra vident\" ↩ Pick up where you left off — — Back to the topic → Q&A forum (in Spanish) Stuck on a topi"},{"t":"Operational Amplifiers","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/amplificadores-operacionales","x":"The operational amplifier configurations one by one, with their schematic, equation and derivation: inverting, non-inverting, follower, summing, subtracting, integrator, differentiator and instrumentation amplifier."},{"t":"⚡ Phasor Study of Alternating Current","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/ca-vectorial","x":"From the rotating vector to the sine wave A vector of length \\( V_p \\) rotates at constant speed. Its shadow on the vertical axis draws the sine; its shadow on the horizontal axis, the cosine. The sine wave is no accident: it is what you see when something rotates. sine · vertical projection cosine · horizontal projection rotating vector (phasor) \\[ v (t) = V_p \\operatorname{sin} (\\omega t + \\varphi) \\] ω is the angular velocity in rad/s, t is time in seconds and φ is the initial phase: where the vector was when the stopwatch started. \\( \\omega = 2 \\pi f \\) · \\( T = \\frac{1}{f} \\) · \\( \\text{o"},{"t":"Resistor Calculator","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/calculadora-resistores","x":"Resistor color code calculator for 4- and 5-band resistors: choose the colors and it gives you the value, the tolerance and the real range the resistance can fall in."},{"t":"🧭 The Smith Chart","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/carta-smith","x":"The Γ plane with an impedance grid on top The chart is the radius-1 circle of the reflection coefficient Γ. Every point inside is a possible load. On top of it is drawn the grid that tells you which impedance corresponds to that Γ: constant-resistance circles and constant-reactance arcs. Move the cursor over the chart: the circle and the arc that cross at that point are highlighted. Click to pin it. constant resistance r inductive reactance (+jx) capacitive reactance (−jx) The three equations behind the chart \\( z = \\frac{Z}{Z_0} = r + j x \\) \\( \\Gamma = \\frac{z -1}{z + 1} \\) \\( z = \\frac{1 +"},{"t":"SI Converter and Symbols","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/conversor-si","x":"Converter for International System (SI) prefixes, from yotta to yocto, plus the three-digit capacitor code and technical symbols ready to copy."},{"t":"✍️ Cursive Handwriting","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/escritura-cursiva","x":"▶ Watch how it is written ↺ Erase my stroke Next stroke › ‹ Previous Next › Letter ☆☆☆ Tap Watch how it is written and then trace over it. Tip — Guide Full Dotted No guide Paper Notebook Chalkboard Practice first with the full guide, then dotted, and finally with no guide. By then it comes naturally. Challenge Six random items, with no extra dotted guide: collect as many stars as you can. Start challenge ⭐ 0 Large line Medium Small Notebook Chalkboard ↺ Undo 🧽 Clear all ⬇ Save image Free ruled paper, with the four-line guide and the slant lines. Ideal for writing your name, copying a sentence"},{"t":"LM317 Regulated Power Supply","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/fuente-lm317","x":"A regulated power supply built around the LM317, explained stage by stage: transformer, bridge rectifier, filter capacitor and regulator, with the signal waveform at each point of the circuit."},{"t":"Digital Logic","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/logica-digital","x":"Digital logic step by step: binary numbering, base converter, binary arithmetic, logic gates, truth tables, Karnaugh maps, 40XX-family ICs and sequential logic."},{"t":"🔌 Topic Map · Secondary Technical Program in Electronics by Catto","d":"Map","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica","x":"← catto.ar 🧠 Mind map 🔲 Area matrix 🔎 Explore an area Each column is a year. Click a subject to see its topics. Topics with the label Full page › have their complete page with theory, diagrams, worked examples, lab practice and self-assessment. The colored chips are cross-cutting areas: click one to see in which other subjects it appears (it jumps to the explorer). Rows = cross-cutting areas · Columns = subjects by year. The number/color indicates the depth with which each subject covers the area. Read a row from left to right to see the repetition and progression of an area throughout the pro"},{"t":"Lighting installations","d":"Topic · Industrial Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/alumbrado","x":"Industrial Installations · 168 h · Topic 5 of 7 Lighting installations Good lighting is not about putting up lots of lamps: it is about putting up the ones you need, of the right type and in the right place, so that every task can be done comfortably and without fatigue. And that is something you calculate. Lighting engineering Lumen method Switching Emergency 01 The quantities Quantity Unit What it measures Luminous flux Φ lumen (lm) How much light the source emits in total, in all directions. Luminous intensity I candela (cd) How much light it emits in a given direction. It depends on the lu"},{"t":"Power amplifiers","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/amplificadores-potencia","x":"Analog Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 4 of 8 Power amplifiers The last stage, the one that drives the loudspeaker. Here voltage gain no longer matters; what matters are the watts delivered, the efficiency and the heat: a badly designed power amplifier does not sound bad, it burns out. Amplifiers Class A / B / AB Push-pull Efficiency Heat sinks 01 How this differs from the small signal In a small-signal amplifier, the swing is millivolts and the transistor always works on a tiny linear stretch. In power amplifiers, the signal travels along the whole load line and three new problems appear: Disto"},{"t":"Small-signal amplifiers","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/amplificadores-senal-debil","x":"Analog Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 3 of 8 Small-signal amplifiers A microphone delivers 2 mV. A loudspeaker needs several volts. In between there is a chain of amplifiers, and the first link is this one: the small-signal stage, where the signal is so small that the transistor behaves linearly. Amplifiers Transistors Common emitter Common collector Darlington 01 DC and AC at the same time A small-signal amplifier does two things at once, and the secret to understanding it is to separate them: In DC, the circuit biases the transistor and leaves it at a stable Q point, in the middle of the acti"},{"t":"Antennas and radiating systems","d":"Topic · Telecommunications I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/antenas","x":"Telecommunications I · 144 h · Topic 4 of 9 Antennas and radiating systems The antenna is the transducer between the cable and free space. It is also the component that pays off the most when it is well chosen and well installed: in a link, doubling the antenna gain is worth more than doubling the power, and it is much cheaper. Antennas Gain SWR Installation 01 The parameters that define an antenna Parameter What it means Gain How much it concentrates energy in one direction, compared with a reference. In dBi relative to the ideal isotropic radiator and in dBd relative to a dipole:. Radiation"},{"t":"Applications with logic gates","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/aplicaciones-compuertas","x":"Digital Electronics I · 96 h · Topic 3 of 3 Applications with logic gates This is where everything comes together: a problem stated in plain words becomes a truth table, is simplified with Karnaugh, gets a schematic, is built on a breadboard and ends up as a soldered circuit board. It is the topic where the subject turns into shop work. Digital logic CAD and PCB Karnaugh Design Circuit boards 01 The digital design method Every problem in this unit is solved with the same sequence. It pays to always go through all of it, even when the circuit looks obvious: that is what keeps you from remaking"},{"t":"Control applications","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics IV","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/aplicaciones-control","x":"Digital Electronics IV · 120 h · Topic 4 of 6 Control applications Three applications that come up again and again in industry, and that force you to bring together everything covered so far: weighing and dosing, elevator control and automatic verification and test systems. In all three, the microcontroller is the easy part; the hard part is the real problem. Load cell Dosing Elevators Test bench Where we start from Temperature and lighting control is covered in control with devices, sensors and actuators in sensors and actuators, and the internal peripherals in microcontroller peripherals. He"},{"t":"Basic programming applications","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/aplicaciones-plc","x":"Industrial Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 5 of 8 Basic programming applications With half a dozen blocks—timers, counters, flags, edges and registers—the vast majority of industrial automation tasks can be solved. What sets a good program apart is not using exotic blocks, but using the usual ones well. Timers Counters Edges Shift register 01 Timers Type Behavior Example use TON The output turns on after the set time, if the input holds. If the input drops earlier, the count resets. Waiting for the pressure to stabilize before enabling the process. TOF The output turns on with the input and tur"},{"t":"Microprocessor and microcontroller architecture","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/arquitectura-microprocesadores","x":"Digital Electronics II · 96 h · Topic 4 of 4 Microprocessor and microcontroller architecture Until now, changing what a digital circuit does meant changing the wires. A processor turns that around: the circuit stays fixed and what changes is the contents of a memory. That is the whole idea, and the last fifty years of electronics come out of it. Microcontrollers CPU Buses Memories Instruction cycle 01 From hardwired circuit to program A ten-step sequencer built with counters and gates works perfectly. The problem shows up when the customer asks for step 4 to last twice as long, or for the sequ"},{"t":"Automation and programmable logic controllers","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/automatismo-plc","x":"Industrial Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 8 Automation and programmable logic controllers To automate is to make a process run by itself, in a repeatable and verifiable way. The programmable logic controller (PLC) is the tool that turned this from a relay panel impossible to modify into a program. Closed loop Hardwired logic PLC Scan cycle 01 What an automatic system is Open loop The action is applied without checking the result: a timer that opens a valve for thirty seconds, a motor that runs as long as there is a run command. It is simple and cheap, and it works when the process is very"},{"t":"Serial communication buses","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics IV","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/buses-serie","x":"Digital Electronics IV · 120 h · Topic 2 of 6 Serial communication buses The USART connects two devices. For a microcontroller to talk to twenty ICs on the same board, or to nodes spread around a plant, there are buses: a few shared wires, with clear rules about who talks and when. I²C SPI RS-485 CAN Where we start from Asynchronous serial transmission, the USART, the baud rate and the framing of each character are covered in the serial communication module. Here we add the synchronous and multipoint buses, which are the ones that show up as soon as there are more than two ICs. 01 The buses, c"},{"t":"Three-phase circuits","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/circuitos-trifasicos","x":"Electrical Technology II · 144 h · Topic 3 of 6 Three-phase circuits Nearly all of the world’s electrical energy is generated, transported and distributed as three-phase. It is not a gratuitous complication: with three phases the power arrives steady instead of pulsating, less copper is needed for the same job, and the rotating field that drives motors appears for free. Alternating current Wye and delta Square root of 3 Neutral 01 Why three and not one Constant power In single-phase, the instantaneous power pulsates at 100 Hz and passes through zero a hundred times per second. With three balan"},{"t":"Power components","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/componentes-potencia","x":"Industrial Electronics I · 144 h · Topic 1 of 10 Power components In signal electronics, what matters is gain. In power electronics, what matters is how much heat the component generates and how that heat is removed. Everything else —choosing the device, sizing the heatsink, protecting the switching— follows from that. Power Thyristors IGBT Heat dissipation 01 The complete family Some of them already appeared in special semiconductors. Here they are arranged by what really decides the choice: voltage, current and switching frequency. Component Turns on Turns off Frequency Home turf SCR Gate pu"},{"t":"Satellite communication","d":"Topic · Telecommunications I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/comunicacion-satelital","x":"Telecommunications I · 144 h · Topic 7 of 9 Satellite communication A communications satellite is, in essence, a very tall repeater: it receives on one frequency, amplifies and retransmits on another. What changes everything is the altitude, and with it the coverage, the delay and the losses. Orbits Transponder C/Ku/Ka bands Pointing 01 The orbits Orbit Altitude Period Round-trip delay Use LEO 500 to 2000 km 95 to 127 min 7 to 27 ms Earth observation, internet constellations, space station. MEO 2000 to 35,000 km 2 to 12 h ≈ 267 ms at 20,000 km Navigation: GPS at 20,200 km, Galileo, GLONASS. GE"},{"t":"Communication module","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/comunicacion-serie","x":"Digital Electronics III · 144 h · Topic 2 of 7 Communication module A microcontroller on its own is good for very little: almost always it has to talk to a sensor, a display, another micro or a computer. All of that is solved with two or three wires and a peripheral that is configured in five lines. Microcontrollers USART SPI I²C Baud rate 01 Why serial and not parallel Sending a byte over eight wires at once seems faster, and it is over short distances. But as soon as you have to leave the circuit board, serial communication wins by a landslide. Parallel Serial Wires 8, 16 or more, plus groun"},{"t":"Analog communications","d":"Topic · Telecommunications I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/comunicaciones-analogicas","x":"Telecommunications I · 144 h · Topic 1 of 9 Analog communications All long-distance communication uses the same scarce resource: the electromagnetic spectrum. The frequency you choose determines how far the signal reaches, what interrupts it, what antenna is needed and how much information fits. Modulation Spectrum Propagation Decibels 01 The communication system It is always the same blocks: an information source, a transmitter that adapts it to the channel, the channel —which adds noise and attenuation— and a receiver that recovers it. Why modulation is needed Antenna size. An efficient ante"},{"t":"Data communications","d":"Topic · Telecommunications II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/comunicaciones-datos","x":"Telecommunications II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 6 Data communications For any two devices to exchange information you need much more than a cable: you need rules. Protocols organize those rules in layers, and that organization is what allowed networks of completely different technologies to connect with one another. Layers Packets CRC TCP/IP 01 The layer model OSI layer What it deals with Example 7 Application The service the user sees. HTTP, SMTP, Modbus, MQTT 6 Presentation Format, encoding and encryption. TLS, JSON, compression 5 Session Establishing and maintaining the dialogue. session opening"},{"t":"Industrial communications","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/comunicaciones-industriales","x":"Industrial Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 7 of 8 Industrial communications Replacing hundreds of point-to-point cables with a twisted pair that runs through the plant is not just a saving in copper: it is what allows each device to report its status, diagnostics to be done remotely and production to be measured. Fieldbus Modbus AS-i Industrial Ethernet Where we start from The physical media—RS-485, CAN, differential pair, termination, isolation—are covered in serial communication buses. Here we work on what goes on top: industrial protocols and the architecture of a plant network. 01 The autom"},{"t":"Programmable counters","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/contadores-programables","x":"Digital Electronics II · 96 h · Topic 1 of 4 Programmable counters A counter is a frequency divider and a state register at the same time. What makes it programmable is being able to decide, by hardware —or by software, later on— what number it starts at, where it stops and which way it counts. All digital timing comes from here. Sequential logic Counter ICs Frequency division Cascading 01 Where we come from: counting is dividing In sequential logic we built counters from discrete flip-flops. Now we use counter integrated circuits, which have everything worked out inside and add the pins that"},{"t":"PID controllers","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/control-pid","x":"Industrial Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 8 of 8 PID controllers Three actions that look at the error in three different ways: the proportional action looks at how much error there is right now, the integral at how much error has built up, and the derivative at where it is heading. These three control the vast majority of industrial processes. P I D Tuning Ziegler-Nichols Anti wind-up 01 The three actions e is the error, the difference between the setpoint and the measurement. T i is the integral time and T d the derivative time, both in seconds. Action What it does If overdone P Corrects in p"},{"t":"Automatic control systems","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/control-potencia","x":"Industrial Electronics I · 144 h · Topic 4 of 10 Automatic control systems Controlling means measuring, comparing and correcting, over and over. In industrial electronics what gets corrected is almost always power: how much energy is delivered to a furnace, a motor or a heating line, and by what method it is delivered. Control Power Closed loop Phase-angle control 01 Open loop and closed loop Open loop An action is applied and it is trusted to produce the expected effect. A knob dimmer, an oven with its heating element at 50 % on a timer: nobody measures the result. It is simple and cheap. It"},{"t":"Control with devices","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/control-temperatura-iluminacion","x":"Digital Electronics III · 144 h · Topic 4 of 7 Control with devices This is where everything comes together: a sensor delivers a voltage, the converter turns it into a number, the program decides, and an isolated output drives mains power. The two cases in the program —temperature and lighting— are the most common and the ones that best show the complete chain. Control Microcontrollers Sensors Hysteresis Isolation 01 The control chain Every automatic control system has the same five links, and each one contributes its own error and delay: 02 Measuring temperature Sensor Output Range What makes"},{"t":"A/D and D/A converters","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/conversores-ad-da","x":"Digital Electronics II · 96 h · Topic 2 of 4 A/D and D/A converters The world is analog and processing is digital. All modern electronics lives on the border between the two, and that border is made of two circuits: the one that translates a voltage into a number and the one that translates a number into a voltage. Conversion Sampling R-2R Successive approximation RS485 01 The border between the two worlds A microphone delivers millivolts that change continuously. A microcontroller only understands numbers. Between the two there is always the same chain, and it is worth having the whole of it"},{"t":"Electric current","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/corriente-electrica","x":"Electrical Technology I · 120 h · Topic 2 of 5 Electric current From charge in motion to solving a complete circuit. This is where Kirchhoff's laws appear: the tool used to analyze any network, however complicated, without needing intuition. Circuits and electrical fundamentals Kirchhoff Resistivity Wheatstone Joule's law 01 What current is Electric current is charge in orderly motion. Its magnitude is the charge that crosses a cross-section of the conductor per unit of time. Conventional direction and actual direction In metals, the charges that move are the electrons, from negative to positi"},{"t":"Alternating currents","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/corrientes-alternas","x":"Electrical Technology I · 120 h · Topic 5 of 5 Alternating currents In AC, Ohm’s law still holds, but it has to be extended: inductors and capacitors oppose the flow of current without dissipating energy and shift the current in phase relative to the voltage. From this come impedance, resonance and the power factor. Alternating current Reactance Phasors Resonance Power factor 01 Where the sine wave comes from A loop rotating at constant speed inside a uniform magnetic field generates, by Faraday’s law, an EMF that follows exactly the sine function. It is not a design choice: it is a geometric"},{"t":"Fault diagnosis and detection","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/diagnostico-fallas","x":"Industrial Electronics I · 144 h · Topic 6 of 10 Fault diagnosis and detection Repairing is not swapping parts until it works. It is reducing uncertainty with measurements that rule out half of the problem at a time. A technician with a method and a multimeter finds more faults than one without a method and the best instruments. Measurement Method Repair Safety 01 The method The six steps Listen to the whole symptom and write it down: what it does, what it doesn't do, since when, what changed beforehand. Half of all faults get narrowed down in that conversation. Reproduce the fault. What can't"},{"t":"Technical drawing and computer-aided design","d":"Topic · Computing for Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/dibujo-tecnico-cad","x":"Computing for Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 5 Technical drawing and computer-aided design A technical drawing is not an illustration: it is a document. It has to be readable by another person, in another workshop, ten years from now, and the part must come out exactly the same. All standardization exists to eliminate interpretation. CAD and design IRAM standards Projections Dimensioning 01 A language, not a drawing A freehand sketch is for thinking. A drawing is for manufacturing, and that is why everything in it is standardized: the sheet formats, the thickness of each line, where each"},{"t":"Smart displays","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics IV","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/displays","x":"Digital Electronics IV · 120 h · Topic 5 of 6 Smart displays The display is the only part of the equipment the user sees. An excellent instrument with a confusing interface is perceived as a bad instrument, and a poorly handled display is also one of the main consumers of processor time and battery current. Multiplexing HD44780 Graphics Interface 01 Seven segments and multiplexing With N multiplexed digits, each one is lit only of the time: to keep the brightness the peak current has to be raised in the same proportion, within what the display can take. Worked example · Four digits We want a b"},{"t":"Electrodynamics and induction","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrodinamica-induccion","x":"Electrical Technology I · 120 h · Topic 4 of 5 Electrodynamics and induction Faraday discovered that a changing magnetic field generates voltage. That one sentence contains the whole electrical industry: the generators in power plants, the transformers on the grid, the inductors in filters, and the spark that burns out transistors when a relay is switched off. Circuits and fundamentals Transformers Faraday and Lenz Inductance Hysteresis 01 Faraday’s law In 1831 Michael Faraday ran the decisive experiment: he brought a magnet close to a coil connected to a galvanometer. While the magnet was mov"},{"t":"Electrostatics","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/electrostatica","x":"Electrical Technology I · 120 h · Topic 1 of 5 Electrostatics Electricity at rest: charges, the forces between them, field and potential. This is where the capacitor comes from, the component found in every power supply, every filter and every oscillator in the rest of the program. Circuits and fundamentals Coulomb's law Electric field Capacitors Dielectrics 01 Electric charge Charge is a property of matter, like mass. It comes in two signs and obeys three principles: There are two kinds. Positive (proton) and negative (electron). Like charges repel, unlike charges attract. Charge is conserved"},{"t":"Microwave links","d":"Topic · Telecommunications I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/enlaces-microondas","x":"Telecommunications I · 144 h · Topic 5 of 9 Microwave links A radio link joins two fixed points with antennas aimed at each other. Calculating it means adding gains and subtracting losses to see whether enough signal reaches the receiver, and checking that the path is really clear. Antennas Modulation Fresnel Link budget 01 Where a radio link is used Replacing cable Joining two buildings, a plant with its office, a town with the fiber node. It is installed in days and does not depend on right-of-way permits. Backhaul Carrying the traffic of a cell tower to the core network, or connecting radio"},{"t":"C and C++ development environments","d":"Topic · Computing for Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/entornos-c-cpp","x":"Computing for Electronics I · 72 h · Topic 2 of 3 C and C++ development environments The leap from “programming” to “programming hardware”. Pointers, structures and direct access to registers are the tools with which a program stops running on top of an abstraction and starts driving real pins, timers and converters. Programming Microcontrollers Pointers Interrupts Embedded systems 01 The toolchain Programming a microcontroller has one central difference from programming a PC: the program is written and compiled on one machine and run on another. This is called cross-compilation. Component Wha"},{"t":"Schematic circuit diagrams","d":"Topic · Computing for Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/esquematicos","x":"Computing for Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 5 Schematic circuit diagrams The schematic is the parent document of an electronics project: the board, the bill of materials, the cost estimate and the repair instructions all come from it. A clear schematic saves weeks; a confusing one costs them. CAD and design Netlist Libraries ERC 01 What it represents and what it does not Connectivity, not geometry A schematic says what is connected to what. It does not say where each component goes, how long a wire is, or how big the circuit board is. Two schematics drawn in very different ways can descr"},{"t":"Structure of a programmable logic controller","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/estructura-plc","x":"Industrial Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 8 Structure of a programmable logic controller On the outside it is a box with terminal blocks and lights. On the inside it is an industrial microcontroller surrounded by everything needed so that real-world signals—24 V, bouncing contacts, motor noise—can come in and go out without destroying anything. CPU and memory Process image Digital I/O Isolation 01 External structure Block Function Power supply Powers the internal logic and, in many units, also delivers the 24 V for the sensors. Its capacity limits how many modules can be mounted. CPU Runs"},{"t":"The microcontroller family","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/familia-microcontroladores","x":"Digital Electronics III · 144 h · Topic 1 of 7 The microcontroller family In Year 5 the microcontroller was a concept. Here it becomes a concrete chip with numbered pins, named registers and a four-hundred-page datasheet. Learning to read that datasheet is, to a large extent, what this year is about. Microcontrollers Registers I/O ports Memories Datasheet 01 Where we are coming from In microprocessor and microcontroller architecture we established what is inside: a CPU with an ALU and registers, memories, buses and peripherals, all in one chip. Now the point is to use one, and that means answe"},{"t":"Fiber optics","d":"Topic · Telecommunications I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fibra-optica","x":"Telecommunications I · 144 h · Topic 8 of 9 Fiber optics A strand of glass thinner than a hair now carries all the internet traffic between cities and continents. The reason is simple: since the carrier is light —hundreds of terahertz—, the available bandwidth is enormous, and communications-grade glass is one of the most transparent materials there is. Total reflection Single-mode Attenuation Splicing and OTDR 01 How the light gets trapped A fiber is a glass core surrounded by a cladding with a slightly lower refractive index. When light reaches the boundary at a sufficiently shallow angle, i"},{"t":"Filters","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/filtros","x":"Analog Electronics II · 144 h · Topic 6 of 6 Filters A filter separates signals by their frequency: it lets some through and blocks others. It is everywhere—in the power supply that feeds the equipment, ahead of every converter, in the tweeter, in the tuner of a radio—and it almost always solves a problem that no other circuit can solve. Filters and oscillators Cutoff frequency Decibels Sallen-Key Q factor 01 The idea: frequency as the criterion An amplifier makes the signal bigger. A rectifier turns it into DC. A filter does something different: it chooses. And it can choose because capacitor"},{"t":"Power supplies","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/fuentes-conmutadas","x":"Industrial Electronics I · 144 h · Topic 5 of 10 Power supplies The linear supply regulates by burning off whatever is left over. The switching supply doesn't regulate: it chops and averages, and that is why it reaches 90 % efficiency at a tenth of the size. All modern equipment lives off that difference. Power Switching Rectifiers Inverters 01 Linear versus switching The linear supply was already covered with the LM317 regulator: mains transformer, rectifier, filter and a regulator that dissipates the difference between input and output. Example · What a linear supply wastes A regulator deliv"},{"t":"Tool handling","d":"Topic · Industrial Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/herramientas","x":"Industrial Installations · 168 h · Topic 3 of 7 Tool handling The difference between a neat job and one that has to be redone almost never lies in knowledge: it lies in using the right tool, in good condition, in the right way. It is the easiest thing to learn and the most expensive to ignore. Hand tools Insulated 1000 V Power tools Machines 01 Hand tools Tool What it is for and what it is not for Combination pliers Gripping, bending and cutting thick conductor. It is not a wrench: tightening nuts with it rounds off the flats. Needle-nose pliers Fine work, forming eyelets, reaching narrow spac"},{"t":"Occupational health and safety","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/higiene-seguridad","x":"Industrial Electronics I · 144 h · Topic 10 of 10 Occupational health and safety This topic is not an administrative formality: it is the one that decides whether someone gets home safe. Electricity gives no warning, cannot be seen and does not forgive improvisation, and most serious accidents happen during routine tasks carried out by experienced people. Health and safety Electrical hazard Five golden rules Regulations 01 What current does to the body What does the damage is not the voltage but the current, how long it flows and where. Voltage matters because it determines that current agains"},{"t":"Electrical installations","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-electricas","x":"Electrical Technology II · 144 h · Topic 4 of 6 Electrical installations Here electrical technology stops being a calculation and becomes a responsibility. Each component of an installation protects something different, and mixing up what protects what is the origin of most electrical accidents. Installations Conductors Thermal-magnetic breaker RCD Contactors 01 What protects what This is the idea that organizes the whole topic, and it is worth fixing in mind before any calculation: Device Protects Against Thermal-magnetic breaker The conductor Overload and short circuit. Keeps the cable from"},{"t":"Residential electrical design","d":"Topic · Industrial Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instalaciones-vivienda","x":"Industrial Installations · 168 h · Topic 1 of 7 Residential electrical design A home installation is not improvised: it is designed. The regulations define how many circuits are needed, what can hang from each one and how it is protected, and that design is what is later drawn, built and tested. Electrification grades Circuits Max. simultaneous demand Distribution boards What is taken for granted Conductor sizing, overcurrent protection, the residual-current device (RCD) and grounding are developed in electrical installations, and the protection devices are revisited in depth in electrical pro"},{"t":"Analog measuring instruments","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-analogicos","x":"Analog Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 6 of 8 Analog measuring instruments How a current becomes the movement of a pointer, and how this one mechanism is used to build an ammeter, a voltmeter and an ohmmeter. Understanding the instrument from the inside is what lets you know when it is lying. Measurement and instrumentation Moving coil Shunt Sensitivity Ohmmeter 01 Why study them when everything is digital today Digital multimeters are more accurate, cheaper, and do not break if you connect them backwards. And yet the analog instrument remains in the curricula and in the workshop, for four speci"},{"t":"Digital measuring instruments","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/instrumentos-digitales","x":"Digital Electronics II · 96 h · Topic 3 of 4 Digital measuring instruments A digital instrument is not an analog instrument with a number display. Inside there is a different chain, with different errors, and a way of lying all its own: showing five solid digits of a value that is not exact in any of them. Measurement Multimeter Dual slope Frequency counter Accuracy 01 What changed compared with the pointer instrument In analog instruments the measurement was a physical phenomenon: a current produced a torque on a coil and the pointer stayed where that torque balanced against the spring. Here"},{"t":"Interrupts","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics IV","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/interrupciones","x":"Digital Electronics IV · 120 h · Topic 3 of 6 Interrupts A program that keeps asking “did anything happen?” wastes the processor and arrives late. An interrupt reverses the relationship: the hardware gives notice, the program drops what it was doing, handles the event and returns exactly where it was. Vector Latency Priorities volatile 01 What exactly happens Step What happens 1 The peripheral detects the event and sets its request flag. 2 If that interrupt is enabled and the global interrupts are too, the core finishes the current instruction. 3 It saves the return address and the processor s"},{"t":"Basic elements of the C language","d":"Topic · Computing for Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-c","x":"Computing for Electronics I · 72 h · Topic 1 of 3 Basic elements of the C language C is the language of electronics. It is used to program the microcontrollers of Years 6 and 7, industrial embedded systems and the kernel of almost every operating system. It is demanding, but it gives total control over the machine. Programming Data types Flow control Functions Preprocessor 01 Why C in an electronics technician program There are more comfortable languages, but none combines these four things at once: Direct access to the hardware. It lets you write a value into a microcontroller register or rea"},{"t":"The C++ language","d":"Topic · Computing for Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/lenguaje-cpp","x":"Computing for Electronics I · 72 h · Topic 3 of 3 The C++ language C++ adds to C the ability to model objects: an LED, a sensor or a motor stop being a loose collection of variables and become entities that keep their data and their operations together. It is the language of Arduino and of a good part of the modern embedded industry. Programming Classes and objects Inheritance Polymorphism Drivers 01 What C++ adds to C C++ is, almost entirely, a superset of C: a C program compiles as C++. What it adds is a different way of organizing large programs. Feature What it is for Does it cost resource"},{"t":"Combinational logic","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/logica-combinacional","x":"Digital Electronics I · 96 h · Topic 1 of 3 Combinational logic Circuits whose output depends only on what is on their inputs at that instant. It is the gateway to all of digital electronics: this is where the adders in a calculator, the decoders of a display and the arithmetic unit of a microcontroller come from. Digital logic Gates Boolean algebra TTL / CMOS Adders Decoders 01 What a combinational circuit is A digital circuit makes decisions with only two values: 0 and 1. These two values represent everything — a switch open or closed, a sensor that detects or does not detect, one bit of a n"},{"t":"Sequential logic","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/logica-secuencial","x":"Digital Electronics I · 96 h · Topic 2 of 3 Sequential logic When a digital circuit remembers. Flip-flops store a bit, counters keep count and registers move data. Without this part there would be no memories, digital clocks or microcontrollers. Digital logic Flip-flops Counters Registers Clock 01 Why memory is needed A combinational circuit always responds the same way to the same input. That is enough to decide, but not to count, nor to sequence, nor to remember that a button was pressed a while ago. Think of the classroom bell pushbutton: it rings while you hold it down and stops when you r"},{"t":"Magnetism and electromagnetism","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/magnetismo-electromagnetismo","x":"Electrical Technology I · 120 h · Topic 3 of 5 Magnetism and electromagnetism Every current creates a magnetic field, and every magnetic field can exert a force on a current. From this back-and-forth come the motor, the relay, the speaker and — in the next topic — the transformer and the generator. Circuits and electrical fundamentals Magnetic field Solenoid Reluctance Electromagnet 01 The magnetic field A magnet has two poles, north and south. Like poles repel, unlike poles attract. Between them there is a field represented by closed lines of force: they leave the north pole, run through the"},{"t":"Fundamental electrical quantities","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/magnitudes-electricas","x":"Analog Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 5 of 8 Fundamental electrical quantities Voltage, current and resistance: the three quantities used to describe any circuit. Together with Ohm's law and Joule's law they form the basic language of the whole field, and they are the first things you need to learn to measure safely. Circuits and fundamentals Measurement Ohm's law Power Multimeter 01 The quantities and their units Quantity Symbol Unit What it represents Electric charge Q coulomb (C) Amount of electricity. One coulomb is 6.25 × 10 18 electrons. Current I ampere (A) Charge passing through a condu"},{"t":"Control and signaling units","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/mando-senalizacion","x":"Industrial Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 8 Control and signaling units Everything that goes into and out of the PLC passes through here: the pushbutton panel that a person operates, the sensors that watch the process, the signal conditioners that make the signal readable and the actuators that carry out the commands. It is the part of the automation that gets touched, and where most failures occur. Pushbutton panel NO and NC 4-20 mA Wiring The starting point The operating principles of each type of sensor and actuator—inductive, capacitive, photoelectric, encoders, contactors, solenoid v"},{"t":"Maintenance of electrical components","d":"Topic · Industrial Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/mantenimiento-electrico","x":"Industrial Installations · 168 h · Topic 6 of 7 Maintenance of electrical components In a factory, one hour of downtime costs more than a whole year of maintenance. The craft lies in detecting the problem before it stops production, and that is done with method, with instruments and with records. Preventive Predictive Thermal relay Thermography 01 Three ways to maintain Corrective Repairs are made when something fails. It is the only option for cheap, non-critical components, and the worst one for everything else: the failure happens at the worst moment and usually drags other damage along wit"},{"t":"PC operation and maintenance","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/mantenimiento-pc","x":"Digital Electronics III · 144 h · Topic 6 of 7 PC operation and maintenance A computer is just another piece of electronic equipment: it has a switching power supply, buses, connectors and heat dissipation. The difference is that its failures show up as “it runs slow” or “it won't boot,” and you have to translate that into a concrete measurement. Measurement Interfaces Networking Diagnosis 01 The parts and how they fail Part Typical failure How to check Switching power supply Electrolytic capacitors with high ESR: won't start, reboots, shuts down under load Measure the voltages under load and"},{"t":"Equipment installation and maintenance","d":"Topic · Telecommunications II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/mantenimiento-telecomunicaciones","x":"Telecommunications II · 120 h · Topic 6 of 6 Equipment installation and maintenance A telecommunications site has to work all the time. That does not depend on the radio equipment but on everything around it: the power supply, the ground, the temperature, the record of what was done and the discipline with which it is worked on. Equipment room Backup Grounding Safety Where we start from Radio-frequency instruments and measurements on transmitters and receivers are covered in measurements and installation in RF, and the general framework for prevention in health and safety. Here we work on the"},{"t":"Electrical materials","d":"Topic · Industrial Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/materiales-electricos","x":"Industrial Installations · 168 h · Topic 2 of 7 Electrical materials An installation is, materially, a selection of materials: copper, insulation, sheet metal, plastic and iron. Choosing any of them badly is not noticed on the first day; it is noticed two years later, when something heats up, cracks or fills with water. Conductors Insulation IP rating Ferromagnetic materials 01 Conductors Material Resistivity at 20 °C When it is used Copper 0.0172 Ω·mm²/m Practically everything: installations, boards, motors. The best common conductor, ductile and easy to connect. Aluminum 0.0282 Ω·mm²/m Overh"},{"t":"AC measurements","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/mediciones-ca","x":"Analog Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 8 of 8 AC measurements In DC, a single number is enough. In AC the voltage changes from instant to instant, so you have to define what you are measuring: the amplitude, the average value or the RMS value. Mixing them up is the most common source of error in the whole lab. Measurement and instrumentation AC RMS value Function generator Frequency counter 01 The parameters of an AC signal Parameter Symbol Definition Amplitude or peak value V p Maximum value the signal reaches relative to zero. Peak-to-peak value V pp From minimum to maximum. In a symmetrical w"},{"t":"Standard circuit measurements","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/mediciones-impedancia","x":"Analog Electronics II · 144 h · Topic 4 of 6 Standard circuit measurements Measuring a resistance means reading one number. Measuring an impedance means reading two —magnitude and phase— and also asking at what frequency, because the answer changes. A 100 nF capacitor is not worth the same at 1 kHz as at 10 MHz: at some point it stops behaving like a capacitor at all. Measurement Impedance Bridges Q factor ESR 01 What it means to measure an impedance In DC, Ohm’s law is enough. In AC the current can lead or lag the voltage, and a complex number is needed to describe the whole picture: the impe"},{"t":"Measurements and installation in RF","d":"Topic · Telecommunications I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/mediciones-rf","x":"Telecommunications I · 144 h · Topic 9 of 9 Measurements and installation in RF In radio frequency you cannot trust your eyes or the multimeter: everything is measured with specific instruments, and a good part of the job consists of knowing what to measure, with which instrument, and without destroying anything in the attempt. Spectrum analyzer VSWR Sensitivity Safety 01 The instruments Instrument What it measures Spectrum analyzer Amplitude as a function of frequency. It is the central instrument: it shows the carrier, the sidebands, the harmonics, the spurious emissions and the noise floor."},{"t":"Programmable systems and memories","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/memorias-dispositivos-programables","x":"Digital Electronics III · 144 h · Topic 5 of 7 Programmable systems and memories Two topics that look different and are really the same: storing bits in one place and reading them back later. A memory stores data; a programmable logic device stores the function the circuit is going to perform. Microcontrollers Memories Decoding FPGA 01 The map of memories Type Volatile Write Cycles Where you find it SRAM Yes Immediate Unlimited Microcontroller variables, processor cache. Fast and expensive: six transistors per bit. DRAM Yes Immediate Unlimited Main memory of a PC. One transistor and one capaci"},{"t":"Digital modulation","d":"Topic · Telecommunications II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/modulacion-digital","x":"Telecommunications II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 6 Digital modulation Turning voice into bits is half the problem; the other half is fitting those bits onto a radio carrier without using more spectrum than necessary. Digital modulations solve exactly that trade-off between speed, bandwidth and robustness. PCM Quantization QAM Constellation Where we start from Sampling and its conditions are covered in sampling theorem, and analog modulations and their comparison in modulation systems. Here the chain is completed: quantize, encode and modulate. 01 PCM: from sample to number n is the number of bits of"},{"t":"Single-phase and three-phase AC motors","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/motores-ca","x":"Electrical Technology II · 144 h · Topic 6 of 6 Single-phase and three-phase AC motors The three-phase asynchronous motor is the workhorse of all industry: a stator with three windings, a cast-aluminum rotor and nothing else. No brushes, no commutator, no electronics. Understanding why it turns means understanding the rotating field. Electrical machines Rotating field Slip Wye-delta Control 01 The rotating field Three windings placed 120° apart in the stator, fed by the three voltages of a three-phase system, produce a magnetic field whose direction rotates at constant speed, even though nothi"},{"t":"Stepper motors","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/motores-paso-a-paso","x":"Industrial Electronics I · 144 h · Topic 9 of 10 Stepper motors An ordinary motor turns as long as voltage is applied. A stepper motor advances an exact angle for each pulse and stays there. That makes it possible to position without sensors, and it is what sits inside a 3D printer, a homemade CNC machine or a dosing machine. Electrical machines Sensors Microstepping Drivers 01 Positioning without feedback The stator has several coils and the rotor is a toothed permanent magnet. By energizing the coils in a certain order, the rotor jumps from one stable position to the next. By counting pulses"},{"t":"Pneumatics and hydraulics","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/neumatica-hidraulica","x":"Industrial Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 6 of 8 Pneumatics and hydraulics When something has to be pushed, clamped or moved with force, the energy almost never travels in electrical form all the way to the end: it is converted into compressed air or pressurized oil. The electrical automation system merely opens and closes the valves. Compressed air Cylinders Valves Hydraulics 01 Compressed air Stage What it does Compressor Takes air from the atmosphere and compresses it, typically to 8 bar for distribution and use at 6 bar. Aftercooler and receiver Lowers the temperature, condenses a good par"},{"t":"Oscillators","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/osciladores","x":"Analog Electronics II · 144 h · Topic 5 of 6 Oscillators An amplifier with positive feedback and a network that picks a frequency. Nothing more than that, and yet from it come a microcontroller’s clock, a transmitter’s carrier and the tone of an electronic instrument. An oscillator is a circuit that is never satisfied. Filters and oscillators Barkhausen LC tank Wien Crystal 01 The oscillation condition In an amplifier with negative feedback, the signal that returns is subtracted and the circuit settles. If the signal that returns adds and arrives with enough amplitude, the circuit sustains its"},{"t":"The oscilloscope","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/osciloscopio","x":"Analog Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 7 of 8 The oscilloscope The instrument that shows the shape of a signal, not just its value. A multimeter says “5 V”; the oscilloscope tells you whether those 5 V are clean DC, a sine wave, a square wave with overshoot, or DC with 200 mV of hum riding on top. Measurement and instrumentation Timebase Trigger Probes Waveforms 01 What the screen shows An oscilloscope draws a graph of voltage versus time. The vertical axis is voltage and the horizontal axis is time; the screen is divided into a grid of divisions (typically 8 vertical × 10 horizontal), and two k"},{"t":"Other microcontroller families","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics IV","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/otros-microcontroladores","x":"Digital Electronics IV · 120 h · Topic 1 of 6 Other microcontroller families Learning one microcontroller is useful; understanding what all of them have in common is far more useful. This topic moves beyond the family you started with and compares architectures, resources and tools, so that you can choose the right chip for each problem and migrate without starting from scratch. Architectures ARM Cortex-M Selection Migration Where we start from The basic architecture, the control registers, memory and I/O ports are covered in the microcontroller family, and the internal peripherals in microcon"},{"t":"Printed circuit boards","d":"Topic · Computing for Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/pcb","x":"Computing for Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 5 Printed circuit boards The board is where the circuit stops being an idea and acquires resistance, capacitance, inductance and temperature. Two boards with the same schematic can work perfectly in one case and be a disaster in the other: the difference is in how they were laid out. CAD and design Routing Trace width Gerber SMD 01 What a board is made of An insulating substrate with copper bonded on top. The most common is FR-4: fiberglass with epoxy resin, 1.6 mm thick, with a copper foil of 35 µm (what the industry calls “one ounce”) on one"},{"t":"Peripherals","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/perifericos-microcontrolador","x":"Digital Electronics III · 144 h · Topic 3 of 7 Peripherals Peripherals are hardware that works on its own, in parallel with the CPU. A timer counts while the program does something else; a PWM module holds an output without anyone tending to it. Understanding them changes the way you write programs: you stop waiting. Microcontrollers Timers Interrupts PWM A/D converter 01 Stop waiting Everyone’s first program blinks an LED with a delay. The problem appears with the second requirement: while it waits, the program can’t do anything else. Not read a pushbutton, not service the serial port, not me"},{"t":"Power in alternating current","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/potencia-ca","x":"Electrical Technology II · 144 h · Topic 2 of 6 Power in alternating current In DC, power is the product of voltage and current, and that is the end of the story. In AC there are three powers, and only one of them does work. The other two explain why a workshop pays more than it consumes and why cables are sized thicker than would seem necessary. Alternating current Power factor Power triangle Compensation 01 Instantaneous power The power at each instant is always. What changes in AC is that v and i may be out of phase: there are moments when one is positive and the other negative, and then th"},{"t":"Professional internship (workplace practice)","d":"Topic · Year 7","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/practica-profesionalizante","x":"Workplace Training · 240 h · Single topic Professional internship (workplace practice) This is the stage where the work stops being an exercise: someone is waiting for a result, there is a deadline that does not move and there are real consequences if something goes wrong. At 240 hours, it is the longest course component of the seventh year, and the one that most resembles what comes afterwards. Professional profile Agreement Logbook Responsibility 01 What sets it apart In the school workshop In the professional internship The problem is scoped by the teacher. The problem comes as it comes, wi"},{"t":"PLC programming","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/programacion-plc","x":"Industrial Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 8 PLC programming The IEC 61131-3 standard defines five languages. They do not compete with one another: each is convenient for a class of problem, and in a real project they usually coexist. What does not change is the method: first you understand the process, then you write the program. Ladder Grafcet Seal-in IEC 61131-3 01 The five languages Language Type What it is good for Ladder diagram (LD) graphical Combinational and control logic. Any electrician can read it. By far the most widely used. Function block diagram (FBD) graphical Processes bu"},{"t":"Electrical protection","d":"Topic · Industrial Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/proteccion-electrica","x":"Industrial Installations · 168 h · Topic 4 of 7 Electrical protection There are two different things to protect, and confusing them is the costliest conceptual mistake in the trade: the installation, which is protected against overloads and short circuits, and people, who are protected against contact with current. Each has its own device, and neither replaces the other. Indirect contact TT / TN systems Trip curves Selectivity Where we start from The principle of the thermal-magnetic breaker, the residual-current device (RCD) and grounding is developed in electrical installations. Here we go d"},{"t":"Installation project","d":"Topic · Industrial Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/proyecto-instalacion","x":"Industrial Installations · 168 h · Topic 7 of 7 Installation project This topic brings together everything before it into a single job: surveying, calculating, drawing, budgeting, executing and verifying a complete installation. It is the first time the work looks like a real professional commission, with a client, a deadline and a budget. Methodology Quantity takeoff Documentation Commissioning 01 The stages of the project Stage What is done What is delivered 1 Survey and starting data: what is needed, what already exists, what the regulations and the utility company require. Descriptive repo"},{"t":"Capstone project","d":"Topic · Year 7","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/proyecto-integrador","x":"Capstone Project · 144 h · Single topic Capstone project This is the work where everything learned over seven years has to function together and at the same time. It does not assess a topic: it assesses the ability to take a real problem from the statement through to a solution that works, is documented and can be defended. Method CAD Schedule Defense 01 What is expected of a capstone project Expected Not expected A real problem, with someone it is useful to. A made-up exercise to justify using a component. Verifiable requirements defined before starting. “It should work well,” with no accepta"},{"t":"Microcontroller projects","d":"Topic · Digital Electronics IV","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/proyectos-microcontrolador","x":"Digital Electronics IV · 120 h · Topic 6 of 6 Microcontroller projects A project is not just a longer program: it is a way of working. What separates a prototype that runs on the bench from a device that runs for a whole year in a plant is not the code, it is the method. Method State machine Non-blocking Robustness 01 From the idea to the device Stage What is defined What is delivered 1 Requirements: what it must do, with what accuracy, under what conditions, for whom. List of verifiable requirements. 2 Architecture: blocks, the signals between them, choice of the microcontroller and the senso"},{"t":"Semiconductor materials theory","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-diodos","x":"Analog Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 1 of 8 Semiconductor materials theory Where silicon comes from, what it means to dope a crystal, and why a PN junction lets current flow in only one direction. From this come the diode, the power supply of any piece of equipment and, later on, the transistor and all integrated circuits. Semiconductors Power electronics Diodes Rectification Zener 01 Conductors, insulators and semiconductors What determines whether a material conducts or not is how free its valence electrons are — those in the outermost shell of the atom. Conductor 1 to 3 valence electrons, p"},{"t":"Special semiconductors","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/semiconductores-especiales","x":"Analog Electronics II · 144 h · Topic 1 of 6 Special semiconductors The bipolar transistor is controlled by current. The components of this topic are controlled by voltage (the FETs), by a pulse that gets latched (the thyristors) or by light (the photoelectric devices). Three different principles and three different fields of application. Semiconductors FET and MOSFET SCR and triac Optoelectronics 01 Three families, three ways of controlling Field effect An electric field narrows or widens a channel through which the current flows. The gate draws no current: it behaves like a capacitor. They a"},{"t":"Signal generation and processing","d":"Topic · Telecommunications I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/senales-fourier","x":"Telecommunications I · 144 h · Topic 2 of 9 Signal generation and processing A signal can be looked at in two ways: how it changes in time —what the oscilloscope shows— or what frequencies it is made of. Both describe exactly the same thing, and learning to go from one to the other is the central tool of communications. Modulation Fourier Bandwidth Spectrum 01 The two domains Time domain Frequency domain What it shows Amplitude moment by moment How much amplitude there is at each frequency Instrument Oscilloscope Spectrum analyzer Used to see Waveform, timing, overshoot, synchronization Harmon"},{"t":"Sensors, actuators and transducers","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sensores-actuadores","x":"Industrial Electronics I · 144 h · Topic 8 of 10 Sensors, actuators and transducers An automatic machine needs to know —where the part is, at what temperature, how much pressure there is— and it needs to act. Sensors are its senses and actuators its muscles; the control, which was covered earlier, is what sits in between. Sensors Control 4-20 mA Encoders 01 Vocabulary and characteristics Term What it means Transducer Converts one physical quantity into another, usually electrical. Sensor The transducer plus its signal conditioning: it delivers a usable signal. Detector Sensor with an on/off ou"},{"t":"Electronic component symbols","d":"Topic · Computing for Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simbologia-componentes","x":"Computing for Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 5 Electronic component symbols A symbol does not draw the component: it draws what it does. That is why a carbon-composition resistor and a 50 W wirewound one are represented the same way, and why the transistor symbol shows which way the current flows instead of showing a package. CAD and design IEC 60617 Designators Standardization 01 Two standards living side by side There are two families of symbols and you run into both every day: European schematics and North American manuals. You do not have to pick one: you have to recognize both, becau"},{"t":"Circuit simulation and virtual measurements","d":"Topic · Computing for Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/simulacion-circuitos","x":"Computing for Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 5 of 5 Circuit simulation and virtual measurements A simulator solves, for each instant, the Kirchhoff equations of the circuit. That is all it does, and it is a great deal: it lets you try twenty variants in one afternoon. But what it returns is not reality, but reality according to the models that were loaded into it. CAD and design SPICE Analysis Error margins 01 What a simulator does inside All circuit simulators descend from SPICE, written at Berkeley in 1973. The procedure has not changed: a system of equations is set up with Kirchhoff’s laws,"},{"t":"Modulation systems","d":"Topic · Telecommunications I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/sistemas-modulacion","x":"Telecommunications I · 144 h · Topic 3 of 9 Modulation systems Modulating means writing information onto a carrier by changing one of its three parameters: amplitude, frequency or phase. That choice determines the bandwidth occupied, the noise resistance and the complexity of the equipment. Modulation AM FM Bandwidth 01 Three parameters, three families A sinusoidal carrier is written. There are exactly three things that can be modified in it, and every modulation that exists comes from them. Amplitude · AM The carrier envelope follows the signal. Simple to generate and to detect; noise adds di"},{"t":"Telephony and cellular telephony","d":"Topic · Telecommunications I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/telefonia-celular","x":"Telecommunications I · 144 h · Topic 6 of 9 Telephony and cellular telephony For a century the telephone network was one copper pair per subscriber running to an exchange. Cellular telephony solved an apparently impossible problem —serving millions of users with only a few radio channels— with a simple idea: divide the territory into cells and reuse the frequencies. Cells Multiple access Handover 1G to 5G 01 The fixed telephone network Element Function Subscriber pair Two copper wires from the house to the exchange. It carries the voice and the power: the exchange supplies 48 V, which is why t"},{"t":"Analog and digital television","d":"Topic · Telecommunications II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/television","x":"Telecommunications II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 6 Analog and digital television Transmitting moving pictures by radio meant solving a formidable problem: turning something two-dimensional that changes over time into a single signal that travels along a cable or through the air. The solution —scanning the image line by line— is still in use eighty years later. Scanning Composite video ISDB-Tb COFDM 01 Scanning and interlacing Parameter 625-line system 525-line system Lines per frame 625 525 Frames per second 25 30 Fields per second 50 60 Line frequency 15,625 Hz 15,734 Hz Line duration 64 µs 63.5 µs"},{"t":"Sampling theorem","d":"Topic · Telecommunications II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/teorema-muestreo","x":"Telecommunications II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 6 Sampling theorem All digital communication rests on a surprising claim: a continuous signal can be reconstructed exactly from isolated samples, as long as they are taken often enough. That “enough” has a precise value. Nyquist Aliasing Antialias TDM Where we start from Spectral analysis and the idea of frequency content are developed in signals and Fourier analysis, and the conversion itself in A/D and D/A converters. Here we work on the criterion that decides how fast you have to sample and what happens if it is not respected. 01 The statement The"},{"t":"Transformers","d":"Topic · Electrical Technology II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transformadores","x":"Electrical Technology II · 144 h · Topic 5 of 6 Transformers It is the simplest electrical machine—it has not a single moving part—and the one that made power distribution as we know it possible. It steps the voltage up for transmission and down for use, with efficiencies that no other machine can match. Electrical machines Mutual induction Turns ratio Efficiency 01 The principle Everything follows from what we saw in electrodynamics and induction: a varying current produces a varying magnetic flux, and that flux, passing through another winding, induces a voltage in it. The iron core is there"},{"t":"Bipolar transistors","d":"Topic · Analog Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/transistores-bipolares","x":"Analog Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 2 of 8 Bipolar transistors The component that changed the 20th century: a small current in the base controls a large current between collector and emitter. With that you amplify, switch and regulate. Everything that comes later in the program builds on this. Transistors Power electronics Q-point Biasing Switching 🎛️ Interactive course on bipolar transistors Eleven topics with characteristic curves that move, the Q-point shifting along the load line live, the transistor as a switch driving an LED, the drift of the operating point when β triples, and the comm"},{"t":"Variable-speed drives","d":"Topic · Industrial Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-electronica/temas/variadores-velocidad","x":"Industrial Electronics I · 144 h · Topic 7 of 10 Variable-speed drives An induction motor turns at the speed imposed by the mains frequency. If the frequency is changed, the speed changes. The whole drive exists to generate that frequency at will, and to do so without destroying the motor. Power Control Volts per hertz Motors 01 Why vary the frequency In AC motors it was established that the synchronous speed is. With 50 Hz and four poles, that is 1500 rpm, and there is no way to change it: the mains is in charge. Method Range Problem Valve or damper Whole flow range The motor keeps drawing th"},{"t":"🎓 Topic Map · Electronic Engineering UTN","d":"Map","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria","x":"← catto.ar 🧠 By level 🔲 Areas by level All blocks Basic Engineering Sciences Complementary Sciences and Technologies Applied Technologies Basic Technologies All areas Social Sciences Electronics Physics Engineering Management Language Mathematics Chemistry Communication Systems Control Systems Circuit Theory Digital Techniques Each column is a level of the program. Click a subject to see its minimum topics. The colored dot indicates the area. No topic matches that search. Rows = areas · Columns = levels. The number is the count of minimum topics that each area contributes at that level. All th"},{"t":"Signal conditioning and sensors","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/acondicionamiento-sensores","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 8 of 11 Signal conditioning and sensors A Pt100 connected with two wires and 10 m of thin cable reads 4.5 °C too high. The sensor is accurate; the cables introduce the error. Pt100 Thermocouple Strain gauges Instrumentation amplifier Four-wire 01 From the phenomenon to the number To measure a temperature, a force or a pressure, a sensor converts the quantity into an electrical signal: a resistance that changes, a voltage of microvolts, a current. That signal can almost never be connected directly to the converter. Signal conditioning is everything betw"},{"t":"Expert witness work and arbitration","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/actividad-pericial","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 11 of 13 Expert witness work and arbitration The judge knows law, not electronics. When a lawsuit depends on whether a device failed because of its design or because of misuse, it is largely decided by an expert's report. Court-appointed expert Technical consultant Expert report Arbitration Amiable compositeur 01 The expert witness, the judge’s technical eyes Many lawsuits depend on a technical question: whether a fire started in a poorly protected switchboard, whether a medical device failed because of its design or because of misuse, whether an installation meets w"},{"t":"Impedance matching","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/adaptacion-impedancias","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 4 of 10 Impedance matching Matching 5 Ω to 50 Ω with an L network forces a Q of 3, and the match lasts about 23 MHz around 100. The transformation ratio decides the bandwidth. Conjugate load L network Quality factor π and T networks Return loss 01 Why match An RF power transistor presents a few ohms at its output; an antenna, 50 Ω; a low-noise amplifier delivers its best noise figure with a particular source impedance. Connecting them directly wastes power and, on lines, produces the standing waves of transmission lines. A lossless matching network trans"},{"t":"Project planning and management","d":"Topic · Final Project","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/administracion-proyectos","x":"Final Project · 96 h · Topic 4 of 4 Project planning and management If the purchase of components is delayed three weeks beyond the forecast, the probability of delivering the project in 20 weeks drops from 74 % to 37 %: purchasing is on the critical path, and every day it slips, everything slips. Work breakdown Gantt chart Critical path PERT Slack 01 Scope and work breakdown Planning starts by defining the scope: what the project delivers and what is left out. Then it is divided into ever smaller parts, down to tasks that one person can estimate and control: this is the work breakdown structu"},{"t":"Problem solving and algorithm representation","d":"Topic · Computer Science I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/algoritmos","x":"Computer Science I · 120 h · Topic 3 of 6 Problem solving and algorithm representation Programming is, above all, solving problems. The language comes later: first you have to understand what is being asked, plan the solution and be able to explain it without ambiguity. Algorithm Pseudocode Flowchart Desk check Efficiency 01 What an algorithm is A cooking recipe, the instructions for assembling a piece of furniture and the procedure for measuring a resistance with a multimeter have something in common: they are steps that, followed to the letter, lead to a result. An algorithm is exactly that,"},{"t":"Aliasing","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aliasing","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 31 of 34 Aliasing A high frequency disguised as a low one. The problem is not that it gets distorted: it is that afterward there is no way to tell, because the samples are exactly the same. Aliasing Folding Antialiasing Oversampling Sigma-delta 01 One frequency disguised as another When the sampling frequency is not high enough, the replicas of the spectrum get into the useful band and a high signal appears in the place of a low one. This is called an alias, and the frequency at which it shows up comes from a simple calculation: \\[ f_{\\text{alias}} = \\big|\\,"},{"t":"The differential amplifier","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificador-diferencial","x":"Applied Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 5 of 7 The differential amplifier The differential pair does not saturate abruptly: it splits a fixed current between two sides. Being symmetric, it generates no second harmonic, and having a current tail, it ignores whatever arrives equally at both inputs. Differential pair Common mode CMRR Third harmonic Offset 01 The differential pair Two identical transistors with their emitters joined and fed by a current source \\( I_{EE} \\) form the most important circuit in analog electronics. The source fixes the sum of the two currents; all the input can do is spl"},{"t":"Multistage amplifiers","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-multietapa","x":"Applied Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 6 of 7 Multistage amplifiers Three identical 1 MHz stages do not make a 1 MHz amplifier: the cascade cuts off at 510 kHz. Each stage adds gain and eats up bandwidth. Cascade Loading effect Cascode Miller effect Bandwidth 01 Why more than one stage A single stage rarely meets everything that is asked of an amplifier: high gain, high input resistance so as not to load the source, and low output resistance to drive the load. Each configuration is good at one thing, so they are combined in cascade, each doing its own job: Stage Function Typical configuration I"},{"t":"Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-operacionales-ing","x":"Applied Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 8 Operational amplifiers: linear and nonlinear applications A comparator receiving a sine wave with 50 mV of noise changes state 30 times where it should change 3. With 0.1 V of hysteresis, it changes 3 times again. Virtual short circuit Instrumentation amplifier Offset Comparator Schmitt trigger 01 The op amp as a feedback amplifier An operational amplifier is a differential amplifier with enormous gain, from \\( 10^5 \\) to \\( 10^7 \\), with a high-impedance input and a low-impedance output. It only makes sense inside a feedback loop: the loop gain is"},{"t":"Low-frequency power amplifiers","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-ing","x":"Applied Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 6 of 8 Low-frequency power amplifiers A class B amplifier dissipates more heat at two thirds of its maximum amplitude than at full power. And with 12 mA of quiescent current its small-signal distortion drops from 20 % to less than 1 %. Amplifier classes Efficiency Crossover distortion Class AB Thermal design 01 What changes with power In a signal amplifier, gain and noise matter. In a power amplifier, which drives a loudspeaker or a motor, efficiency comes to the front, because whatever does not reach the load is dissipated as heat in the transistors, alo"},{"t":"RF power amplifiers","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-potencia-rf","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 6 of 10 RF power amplifiers Narrowing the conduction angle to 90° raises the efficiency of an RF amplifier to 94 %, but takes away 2 dB of power and all the amplitude linearity. Efficiency Conduction angle Class C Class E Predistortion 01 Power, efficiency and linearity The power amplifier is the stage that consumes the most in a transmitter, and what it does not deliver to the antenna it turns into heat. Three figures describe it: the output power, the drain efficiency (or collector efficiency), \\( \\eta = P_{out}/P_{DC} \\), and the linearity, which deci"},{"t":"Feedback amplifiers","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-realimentados","x":"Applied Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 8 Feedback amplifiers An amplifier that distorts 7.7 % drops to 1.2 % with a loop gain of 10. Feedback trades surplus gain for linearity. Loop gain Desensitivity Topologies Impedances Distortion 01 The loop gain A feedback amplifier takes a sample of its output, multiplies it by a factor \\( \\beta \\) and subtracts it from the input. If the amplifier without feedback has gain \\( A \\), the closed-loop gain is: \\[ A_f = \\frac{A}{1 + A\\beta} = \\frac{A}{1 + T} \\] \\( T = A\\beta \\) is the loop gain: how much a signal is amplified as it travels around the com"},{"t":"Low-level and tuned amplifiers","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/amplificadores-sintonizados","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 5 of 10 Low-level and tuned amplifiers The internal feedback of an RF transistor is small, but it decides whether it oscillates. Going from |S12| = 0.05 to 0.08 leaves the same transistor potentially unstable. S parameters Rollett factor Maximum gain Neutralization Low-noise amplifier 01 Describing an RF amplifier: the S parameters At radio frequency, voltages and currents cannot be measured with open or short circuits: transistors oscillate and cables have lengths comparable to the wavelength. That is why devices are described with scattering parameters"},{"t":"Signals and systems in communications","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-senales-comunicaciones","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 2 of 11 Signals and systems in communications A rectangular pulse leaves 5 % of its energy outside twice its null-to-null width. A raised-cosine pulse of the same duration leaves 100 times less: the pulse shape decides how much it invades its neighbors. Bandwidth Parseval Linear phase Equalizer Intermodulation 01 Thinking in frequency In communications almost everything is reasoned in the frequency domain: what part of the spectrum a signal occupies, what a channel lets through, where an interference falls. The tools are those of Signals and Systems —the se"},{"t":"Analysis of work and processes","d":"Topic · Industrial Organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analisis-trabajo","x":"Industrial Organization · 48 h · Topic 3 of 7 Analysis of work and processes For 450 units per shift, a line of ten tasks needs a 60 s cycle and five stations, with 92 % efficiency. For 540, the largest-time rule uses seven stations even though the theoretical minimum is six. Methods study Standard time Product life cycle Process types Line balancing 01 Methods study and work measurement Work study has two parts. Methods study records how a task is done, examines it critically —what is done, where, when, by whom and how— and proposes a simpler, safer and more productive way. It relies on chart"},{"t":"Frequency-domain measurements","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/analizador-espectro","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 10 Frequency-domain measurements Lowering the resolution bandwidth of a spectrum analyzer by a factor of ten lowers its noise floor by 10 dB and makes the sweep a hundred times slower. Superheterodyne RBW Sweep time Noise floor Real time 01 The superheterodyne spectrum analyzer The oscilloscope shows the signal in time; the spectrum analyzer, its power as a function of frequency. The classic analyzer is a self-tuning superheterodyne receiver: a local oscillator sweeps a range of frequencies, a mixer translates each input component to a fixed inte"},{"t":"Antennas and radio links","d":"Topic · Transmission Media","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/antenas-enlace","x":"Transmission Media · 96 h · Topic 8 of 8 Antennas and radio links For a 30 km link over flat terrain it is not enough for the antennas to see each other: at the midpoint, the Earth rises 13 m and the Fresnel zone has a radius of 14 m. The towers need about 22 m. Gain Effective area Friis equation Fresnel zone Fade margin 01 What defines an antenna An antenna is the transducer between a transmission line and space. The radiation unit covered radiation resistance, the pattern and directivity; to design a link you also need: Parameter What it tells you Gain \\( G = e \\cdot D \\) The directivity aft"},{"t":"The engineering preliminary design","d":"Topic · Final Project","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/anteproyecto","x":"Final Project · 96 h · Topic 3 of 4 The engineering preliminary design A sensor that wakes up for a tenth of a second per minute at 20 mA and sleeps at 5 μA lasts almost six years on two AA batteries. If it draws 50 μA when asleep, it lasts less than three: the sleep mode decides the battery life. Descriptive report Block diagram Energy budget Test plan Component selection 01 What a preliminary design contains The preliminary design is the document that shows, before building, that the proposed solution will meet the specification. It is not the detailed design —the complete schematics and the"},{"t":"Applications of the derivative","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-derivada","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 5 of 10 Applications of the derivative The derivative finds maxima and minima, justifies linear approximations and solves the optimization problems that appear in every design. Extrema Concavity Mean value theorem L’Hôpital Taylor 01 Increase, decrease and extrema The sign of the derivative tells you where the function is heading, and the points where it vanishes are the candidates for maximum or minimum: If on an interval… then f… f′(x) > 0 increases f′(x) < 0 decreases f′(c) = 0 or does not exist has a critical point at c: it has to be analyzed The first-derivative"},{"t":"Applications of the definite integral","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aplicaciones-integral","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 9 of 10 Applications of the definite integral Definite integrals give areas, volumes, work, accumulated charge and the energy a signal delivers to a load. Areas Volumes Work Energy RMS value 01 Areas between curves The area enclosed between two curves is the integral of the difference, the upper one minus the lower one, between the points where they intersect: \\[ A = \\int_a^b \\left[ f (x) -g (x) \\right] d x \\] The limits a and b come from solving f(x) = g(x). If the curves cross inside the interval, you have to split it and swap the order of the subtraction on each p"},{"t":"Processor architecture and organization","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/arquitectura-procesadores","x":"Digital Techniques II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 6 Processor architecture and organization Summing a matrix by traversing it by columns instead of by rows can drop the cache hit rate from 87 % to 0 %. The algorithm is the same; what changes is the order. RISC and CISC CPI Pipeline Cache Locality 01 Architecture and organization There are two ways of looking at a processor. The architecture is the contract with the software: the instruction set, the visible registers, the addressing modes, the memory model. The organization (or microarchitecture) is how that contract is fulfilled in silicon: how many"},{"t":"Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects","d":"Topic · Final Project","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/aspectos-proyecto","x":"Final Project · 96 h · Topic 2 of 4 Technical, legal, economic, social and environmental aspects A project with a net present value of 238 thousand stops being worthwhile if the selling price falls by barely 7 %, but it withstands 40 % more investment. Sensitivity analysis tells you where it pays to refine the estimates. Technical risk Intellectual property Sensitivity Social impact Life cycle 01 The technical aspect The technical analysis confirms that the product can be made with the available technology, within the deadline and with the project team. It reviews the architecture alternatives"},{"t":"Attenuators and compensators","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/atenuadores-compensadores","x":"Circuit Theory II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 8 Attenuators and compensators A 10 dB attenuator for 50 Ω needs 25.97 and 35.14 Ω. With the nearest commercial resistors it gives 10.07 dB: the rounding hardly matters, but you have to calculate it. T attenuator π attenuator E24 and E96 series Lead network Equalizer 01 Lowering the level without causing a mismatch An attenuator reduces the amplitude of a signal by a fixed number of decibels, and also presents the correct impedance on both sides. A simple resistive divider attenuates, but it changes the impedance seen by the generator and the load: in a 5"},{"t":"Advanced measurement automation systems","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-avanzada","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 10 of 10 Advanced measurement automation systems With a Cpk of 1, three out of every thousand power supplies fall out of specification. With 1.33, thirty per million. The test system not only decides which unit passes: it measures how good the process is. PXI and LXI IVI drivers Test sequencer Cp and Cpk Yield 01 From the bench instrument to the test system The measurement automation of Measurements I connected bench instruments to a computer with SCPI commands. In production, where thousands of devices are tested per day, that is not enough: compact,"},{"t":"Measurement automation","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/automatizacion-mediciones","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 11 of 11 Measurement automation A badly written command does not stop a measurement program: the instrument only stores an error in its queue, and if nobody reads it, the data look good. GPIB VISA SCPI PyVISA Data acquisition 01 Why automate a measurement Sweeping the frequency response of a filter at a hundred points, recording a temperature every minute for a week or testing a thousand boards at the end of a production line cannot be done by hand. An automatic measurement system controls the instruments from a computer: it configures them, triggers t"},{"t":"Eigenvalues and eigenvectors","d":"Topic · Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/autovalores-autovectores","x":"Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry · 120 h · Topic 9 of 9 Eigenvalues and eigenvectors In every transformation there are privileged directions that do not turn, they only stretch. Finding them means finding the natural modes of a physical system. Characteristic polynomial Diagonalization Natural modes Stability Resonance 01 Directions that do not turn A linear transformation, in general, moves every vector and changes its direction. But there are almost always a few privileged directions along which the vector does not turn: it is only stretched, shortened or flipped. Those directions are th"},{"t":"Using the language for low-level applications","d":"Topic · Computer Science I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bajo-nivel","x":"Computer Science I · 120 h · Topic 6 of 6 Using the language for low-level applications The same C used to write a desktop program drives the pins of a microcontroller: all it takes is the bit operators, a pointer to a fixed address and knowing why volatile is needed. Bit operators Masks volatile stdint.h Interrupts 01 What “low level” means A low-level program is one that talks directly to the hardware: it lights an LED by writing a bit into a register, reads a sensor by waiting for a status bit to change, builds a frame bit by bit to send it over a wire. In the stack of layers from the first"},{"t":"The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-amplificador","x":"Applied Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 2 of 7 The bipolar transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal A common emitter without degeneration is linear only for a few millivolts: with 10 mV at the base it already produces almost 10 % of second harmonic. The scale of the bipolar transistor is 25.85 mV. Common emitter Emitter degeneration Harmonic distortion Clipping Small and large signal 01 Small signal and large signal The bipolar transistor is an exponential device: the collector current follows the base-emitter voltage as \\( i_C = I_S\\, e^{v_{BE}/V_T} \\). No exponential is a straight li"},{"t":"Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching","d":"Topic · Electronic Devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/bjt-dispositivo","x":"Electronic Devices · 120 h · Topic 3 of 9 Bipolar transistor: DC, small signal and switching All the bipolar transistors in the world have the same transconductance at 1 mA: 38.7 mS. Their β, on the other hand, varies from 100 to 300 between two samples of the same type. Ebers-Moll Early effect Hybrid-π Saturation Stored charge 01 Two junctions very close together An NPN bipolar transistor is two PN junctions back to back, but it is not the same as two diodes: its trick is that the middle base is much thinner than the diffusion length of the electrons. With the base-emitter junction forward bi"},{"t":"Differential calculus of several variables","d":"Topic · Calculus II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-diferencial-varias","x":"Calculus II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 8 Differential calculus of several variables With several variables, the derivative becomes a vector: the gradient, which points in the direction in which the function grows fastest and is perpendicular to the level curves. Partial derivatives Gradient Tangent plane Extrema Lagrange 01 Partial derivatives: moving one variable at a time With several variables, “the” derivative is not enough: you have to say with respect to which one. The partial derivative is computed by holding the others fixed, as if they were constants: \\[ \\frac{\\partial f}{\\partial x} = \\lim"},{"t":"Integral calculus","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-integral","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 6 of 10 Integral calculus Integration is the inverse operation of differentiation: starting from how something changes, you reconstruct how much of it there is. Antiderivatives Substitution By parts Partial fractions Table 01 Undoing the derivative Differentiating is easy and mechanical: there are rules for everything. The inverse operation —given the derivative, find the function— is called finding an antiderivative, and it is not mechanical: it is a craft. \\[ F (x) \\; \\text{is an antiderivative of }\\; f \\; \\iff \\; F' (x) = f (x) \\quad \\int f (x) d x = F (x) + C \\]"},{"t":"Applied numerical computation","d":"Topic · Computer Science II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calculo-numerico","x":"Computer Science II · 120 h · Topic 6 of 8 Applied numerical computation The computer does not calculate with real numbers but with approximations, and that difference has consequences. Numerical methods solve what has no analytical solution, always with an error that you must know how to bound. Floating point Bisection Newton Least squares Error 01 The computer does not calculate with real numbers A double stores 64 bits: one sign bit, 11 of exponent and 52 of mantissa. With that it represents about 16 significant decimal digits and nothing more. Everything else is rounded. \\[ x = (-1)^s \\cdo"},{"t":"Power quality","d":"Topic · Electrical Machines and Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-energia","x":"Electrical Machines and Installations · 96 h · Topic 5 of 5 Power quality In a building full of computers, the neutral can carry more current than each phase. The third harmonics of the three phases do not cancel: they add. Voltage sags Harmonics THD Neutral current Active correction 01 When power is “good quality” The ideal grid delivers a perfect sinusoidal voltage, of constant amplitude and frequency and balanced three-phase. Power quality measures how far the real one departs from that ideal, and how those deviations affect equipment. The responsibility is shared: the utility answers for t"},{"t":"Quality control and total quality","d":"Topic · Industrial Organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calidad-total","x":"Industrial Organization · 48 h · Topic 6 of 7 Quality control and total quality A plan that inspects 50 units and accepts the lot with one defect rejects 9 % of good lots and accepts 28 % of bad ones. With 200 units and up to 5 defects, those risks drop to 1.6 % and 6.2 %. Acceptance sampling Operating characteristic curve Statistical control Total quality ISO 9001 01 What quality is For Juran, quality is fitness for use; for Crosby, conformance to requirements. The two views complement each other: a product has to meet its specifications and, in addition, the specifications have to reflect wh"},{"t":"Heat and temperature","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/calor-temperatura","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 15 Heat and temperature Measuring temperature means converting it into something electrical, and there are four ways to do it. None of them is the best: each one wins in one range and loses in another, and choosing the wrong sensor ruins the measurement before it starts. Temperature Specific heat Thermal expansion Thermocouple Pt100 and NTC 01 Temperature is not heat They are two different things, and they get confused all the time. Temperature measures the mean kinetic energy of the particles: it is a property of the state. Heat is energy in transit between two"},{"t":"Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice","d":"Topic · Transmission Media","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-maxwell","x":"Transmission Media · 96 h · Topic 1 of 8 Electromagnetic fields for links: Maxwell in practice At 1 GHz the current in a copper wire flows in the first 2 microns of its surface, and the energy does not travel through the copper but through the insulator around it. Phasor form Boundary conditions Skin effect Surface resistance Poynting vector 01 From physics to the link Maxwell's equations already appeared in Physics II, on the page on Maxwell's equations, as the synthesis of all electricity and magnetism. In Transmission Media they are used for a concrete purpose: carrying a signal from one po"},{"t":"Vector fields, curl and divergence","d":"Topic · Calculus II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/campos-vectoriales","x":"Calculus II · 120 h · Topic 6 of 8 Vector fields, curl and divergence A field assigns a vector to every point in space. Divergence measures whether there are sources and curl measures whether there is circulation: the two central questions of electromagnetism. Field lines Divergence Curl Conservative Potential 01 A vector at every point A vector field assigns a vector to every point in space. It is neither a curve nor a surface: it is a distribution. The wind on a map, the velocity of water in a river, the electric field around a charge and the magnetic field inside a transformer are vector fi"},{"t":"Notation and amount of substance","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cantidad-sustancia","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 2 of 12 Notation and amount of substance Counting atoms seems impossible, yet it is done every day: a doping of 10¹⁶ atoms per cubic centimeter is two parts per million, and the resistivity of a wafer depends on that number. Mole Avogadro Molar mass Doping Parts per million 01 The mole: a very large dozen Atoms are so small that counting them one by one is useless. Chemistry uses a package of fixed size, just as the dozen is used for eggs, only vastly larger: \\[ 1\\ \\text{mol} = 6.022\\times10^{23}\\ \\text{entities} \\qquad (\\text{Avogadro's number},\\ N_A) \\] Sinc"},{"t":"Capacitance and capacitors","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/capacitores","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 5 of 15 Capacitance and capacitors A 10 µF X7R ceramic can end up below 3 µF when 10 V is applied to it, and that does not appear in the tolerance printed on it. It is the cause of power supplies that oscillate and filters that do not filter. Capacitance Dielectrics Dielectric strength ESR RC transient 01 What capacitance is Two conductors separated by an insulator store charge when a potential difference is applied to them. Capacitance measures how much charge they store per volt, and it depends only on the geometry and on the material in between: not on the voltage"},{"t":"The Cauchy-Riemann equations","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cauchy-riemann","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 4 of 34 The Cauchy-Riemann equations Two partial differential equations tie the real part to the imaginary part: in a differentiable function they cannot be chosen separately. Harmonic functions follow from them and, with those, the calculation of fields. Cauchy-Riemann Partial derivatives Harmonic Laplace Complex potential 01 Two paths, one condition In the previous topic it became clear that the complex derivative requires the difference quotient to give the same result from every direction. It is enough to impose that two directions agree to obtain a cond"},{"t":"Causality","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/causalidad","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 18 of 34 Causality Today’s output cannot depend on tomorrow’s input. That single requirement is why no real filter has a vertical cutoff, and why any digital audio processing has latency. Causality Memory Linear phase Paley-Wiener Latency 01 No guessing the future A system is causal if its output at an instant depends only on what happened up to that instant, never on what is going to happen afterward: \\[ y(t_0) \\text{ depends on } x(t) \\text{ for } t \\le t_0 \\text{, and on nothing else} \\] They are also called non-anticipative. Every system that works in re"},{"t":"Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-dinamica-rigido","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 4 of 10 Kinematics and dynamics of the rigid body An extended body does not only translate: it also rotates. Every quantity of translation has its twin in rotation, and mass becomes moment of inertia. Rotation Moment of inertia Torque Rolling Angular momentum 01 Every quantity has its twin A rigid body is a model: a solid whose particles always keep the same distance from one another. It does not deform. With that idealization, any motion breaks down into translation of the center of mass plus rotation about it. And the remarkable thing is that rotation repeats, term"},{"t":"Kinematics of a particle","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinematica-punto","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 1 of 10 Kinematics of a particle Kinematics describes motion without asking about its causes. It consists of three functions of time —position, velocity and acceleration— linked by a derivative, and that same structure reappears later in the charging of a capacitor. Position Velocity Acceleration Projectile motion Circular motion 01 Describing a motion Before asking why something moves, you have to be able to say how it moves. That takes three prior decisions, which are almost always made without noticing: A frame of reference. There is no absolute motion: a seated pa"},{"t":"Chemical kinetics","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cinetica","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 8 of 12 Chemical kinetics That the life of an electrolytic capacitor is halved for every 10 °C is not an isolated empirical rule: it is the Arrhenius equation with an activation energy of 0.83 electronvolts. Rate Reaction order Arrhenius Activation energy Service life 01 How fast, not whether it happens Thermodynamics says whether a reaction can occur; kinetics says how long it takes. They are independent questions, and confusing them leads to absurd conclusions. The oxidation of silicon in air is spontaneous and highly exothermic, and yet a wafer does not fal"},{"t":"Inductively coupled circuits","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-acoplados","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 11 of 13 Inductively coupled circuits Two coils with no wire between them transfer energy, isolate a circuit from the mains and turn an 8 Ω loudspeaker into a 5 kΩ load. It all comes from a single number: the mutual inductance. Mutual inductance Coupling Ideal transformer Reflected impedance Leakage 01 Mutual inductance When the magnetic flux of one coil passes through another, a varying current in the first induces a voltage in the second with no electrical connection between them. This relationship is measured by the mutual inductance \\( M \\), and it adds a t"},{"t":"Polyphase circuits","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/circuitos-polifasicos","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 12 of 13 Polyphase circuits When the neutral of a building is cut, some apartments receive more than 300 V and their appliances burn out. The phasor diagram of an unbalanced wye explains exactly why. Three-phase Wye and delta Neutral Millman Three-phase power 01 Three voltages 120° apart A three-phase generator has three windings spaced 120° apart in space, and produces three voltages of equal amplitude 120° apart in time. The entire electrical grid of the world is generated, transmitted and distributed this way, for three reasons that appear in this unit: with"},{"t":"Combinational logic: minimization and hazards","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/combinacional-minimizacion","x":"Digital Techniques I · 96 h · Topic 1 of 4 Combinational logic: minimization and hazards The shortest expression is not always the best: it can let the output drop for a few nanoseconds when it should not. Minimizing well means finding the minimal expression and knowing when to add a term to it. Karnaugh Quine-McCluskey Don’t-cares Hazards Critical path 01 From the truth table to the minimal expression Gates, Boolean algebra and the step from a truth table to a circuit are covered in the page on combinational logic in the Electronics Technician program. This unit starts from there and asks the"},{"t":"Passive components: frequency and time","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/componentes-pasivos","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 3 of 13 Passive components: frequency and time The same inductor is almost a short circuit at 50 Hz and almost an open circuit at 1 MHz. Complex impedance explains why, and the RC time constant explains everything that happens at the instant a switch is closed. Phasors Impedance Time constant Steady state RC transient 01 The voltage-current relationship of each component Three passive elements are enough to build almost any linear circuit. Each one relates voltage and current in a different way, and that difference is what makes this whole course interesting. C"},{"t":"Software-defined radio and digital communications","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/comunicaciones-digitales-rf","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 10 of 10 Software-defined radio and digital communications A 20 MHz band centered at 70 MHz can be digitized with a 56 MS/s converter, less than its highest frequency. The converter, well chosen, acts as a mixer. Software-defined radio Bandpass sampling Nyquist zones OFDM MIMO 01 Software-defined radio In a classic receiver, each function is a circuit: the channel filter, the demodulator, the carrier recovery. In a software-defined radio (SDR), the analog front end is reduced to amplifying, sometimes translating, and digitizing; everything else is done b"},{"t":"Standards, failures and reliability","d":"Topic · Electronic Technology","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/confiabilidad","x":"Electronic Technology · 120 h · Topic 1 of 9 Standards, failures and reliability A 200-component device that at 40 °C has a 92 % probability of working five years without failing drops to 66 % if it runs at 60 °C. Temperature is the first enemy of reliability. Failure rate MTBF Bathtub curve Redundancy Arrhenius 01 Standards and specifications An electronic product is designed against a specification: what it has to do, under what conditions and for how long. A good part of it is set by standards, which make it possible to compare products and meet legal requirements: Body Examples of standard"},{"t":"Scientific and technological knowledge","d":"Topic · Engineering and Society","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conocimiento-cientifico-tecnologico","x":"Engineering and Society · 48 h · Topic 1 of 5 Scientific and technological knowledge Science, craft and technology are not synonyms. Telling them apart changes what can be demanded of each one, and explains why an engineer has to decide even without having all the information. Science and craft Falsifiability Paradigms Technological knowledge TRL 01 Science, craft, technology and engineering The words are used as synonyms and they are not. Telling them apart is not an academic whim: it changes what you can demand of each one and which questions it answers. Activity What it seeks How it is vali"},{"t":"Conservation laws in mechanics","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conservacion-mecanica","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 3 of 10 Conservation laws in mechanics Some quantities do not change even when everything else does. Those constants make it possible to solve in two lines problems that would take a full page of differential equations using Newton. Work Mechanical energy Momentum Impulse Collisions 01 Why it pays to conserve something Solving a problem with Newton’s laws requires knowing the forces at every instant. Often they are not known —what exact force do two cars exert on each other during a crash?— and yet the problem can be answered, because certain quantities have the same"},{"t":"The National Constitution","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/constitucion-nacional","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 3 of 13 The National Constitution The right to practice a profession, to own an invention or to enjoy a healthy environment is not granted by a statute: it is in the Constitution, and no statute can override it. Supremacy Federalism Rights and guarantees Article 14 bis Amparo 01 The supreme law The Constitución Nacional (National Constitution) is the rule that organizes the State and sets out the rights of the inhabitants. It was enacted in 1853 and amended in 1860, 1866, 1898, 1957 and 1994. The 1994 reform was the most extensive: it added the rights to a healthy en"},{"t":"Software build tools","d":"Topic · Computer Science II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/construccion-software","x":"Computer Science II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 8 Software build tools Between the text file and the running program there are four stages, and each one throws its own errors. Knowing which one failed saves half of the debugging time. Preprocessor Compiler Linker make git 01 The four stages Typing gcc main.c -o program looks like a single step, but it is four programs chained together. Knowing which one failed is half of debugging: Stage Input Output Typical error Preprocessor.c.i Cannot find a header Compiler.i.s (assembly) Syntax or type error Assembler.s.o (object) Rarely fails Linker.o and librar"},{"t":"Contracts","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/contratos","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 6 of 13 Contracts A quotation accepted by email is already a contract. What was not put in writing is filled in by the law, and not always the way one expected. Consent Good faith Work contract Service contract Confidentiality 01 What a contract is Article 957 of the Civil and Commercial Code defines a contract as the legal act by which two or more parties express their consent to create, regulate, modify, transfer or extinguish property-related legal relationships. Three principles govern it: Freedom of contract (Article 958): the parties decide whether to contract,"},{"t":"Introduction to control systems","d":"Topic · Control Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/control-introduccion","x":"Control Systems · 96 h · Topic 1 of 8 Introduction to control systems A proportional control that is too gentle leaves the oven seven degrees below the setpoint. One that is too strong, with a twenty-second delay, makes it oscillate like a thermostat. Closed loop On-off Hysteresis Proportional control PLC and SCADA 01 Open loop and closed loop A control system makes a physical quantity, the controlled variable, follow a desired value, the reference (or setpoint), by acting on a plant. In open loop, the controller applies an action calculated in advance, like a toaster that heats for a fixed ti"},{"t":"Control of electric machines","d":"Topic · Power Electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/control-maquinas","x":"Power Electronics · 96 h · Topic 6 of 8 Control of electric machines With the voltage proportional to the frequency, at 10 Hz the maximum torque of a motor falls from 191 to 86 N·m, because the stator drop is no longer negligible. A 15 V boost brings it back to 122 N·m. Variable-speed drive V/f control Voltage boost Vector control Regenerative braking 01 DC motors The speed of a separately excited DC motor is proportional to the armature voltage, less the drop across its resistance, and the torque to the current. It is controlled with a controlled rectifier from the line or with a DC chopper;"},{"t":"Peripheral control and communication","d":"Topic · Computer Science II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/control-perifericos","x":"Computer Science II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 8 Peripheral control and communication A program stops being an exercise when it lights a real LED. At the other end of the cable a microcontroller is waiting for bytes, and you have to agree on what each one means. Serial port Frames Checksum Buffers Protocol 01 From the program to the physical world A program that only prints to the screen does not need to know anything about the hardware. As soon as you have to light an LED or read a sensor, three questions appear: where does the data go out, how fast, and what does each byte mean. Interface Typical"},{"t":"Analog-to-digital and digital-to-analog converters","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/conversores-datos","x":"Digital Techniques II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 6 Analog-to-digital and digital-to-analog converters A 14-bit converter sampling a 49 MHz signal with a clock of 1 ps jitter performs the same as an 11-bit one: the clock, not the converter, sets the limit. Quantization ENOB INL and DNL Jitter SAR and sigma-delta 01 The boundary between analog and digital Every digital system that measures or acts on the world has a converter at each edge: an analog-to-digital converter (ADC) that turns a voltage into a number, and a digital-to-analog converter (DAC) that does the opposite. Between the two there can b"},{"t":"AC-AC converters","d":"Topic · Power Electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/convertidores-ca-ca","x":"Power Electronics · 96 h · Topic 4 of 8 AC-AC converters With the firing angle at 90°, a phase controller delivers half the power to a heater, but its current carries 54 % third harmonic and the power factor drops to 0.71 even though the load is a resistor. Phase control Triac Integral-cycle control Soft starter Cycloconverter 01 AC voltage controllers An AC voltage controller regulates the RMS voltage across a load without changing the frequency. It is built with two thyristors in antiparallel or, at low power, with a triac. There are two ways to drive it: Method How it works Use Phase contro"},{"t":"Converters in power systems","d":"Topic · Power Electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/convertidores-sistemas-energia","x":"Power Electronics · 96 h · Topic 7 of 8 Converters in power systems A perturb-and-observe tracker with 2 V steps quickly finds the maximum power point, but it oscillates and loses almost 2 %. With 0.1 V steps it is accurate, but slow: after 40 iterations it has not arrived yet. Photovoltaic MPPT Grid-connected inverter STATCOM DC transmission 01 Converters in generation Renewable generation reaches the grid through converters. A photovoltaic panel delivers direct current with a current-voltage characteristic that depends on irradiance and temperature; a DC-DC converter makes it work at its max"},{"t":"Convolution and modulation theorems","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/convolucion-modulacion","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 32 of 34 Convolution and modulation theorems Convolving in one domain is multiplying in the other. The first theorem lets you analyze filters; the second lets you move signals in frequency, and all of radio comes from it. Convolution Modulation Sidebands AM Efficiency 01 Two theorems that are the same one They have already appeared separately; it pays to see them together because their symmetry is what organizes half of signal processing: \\[ x(t) * h(t) \\;\\longleftrightarrow\\; X(j\\omega)\\,H(j\\omega) \\] \\[ x(t)\\,h(t) \\;\\longleftrightarrow\\; \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\,X("},{"t":"Convolution","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/convolucion","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 21 of 34 Convolution Flip, shift, multiply and measure the area. With those four steps, and knowing the impulse response, you can compute the output of an LTI system for any input. Convolution Impulse response Cascade Duration Discrete convolution 01 Why the integral looks like this Convolution is not a definition that fell from the sky: it comes from applying linearity and invariance to the decomposition of a signal into impulses. The reasoning was already sketched when discussing LTI systems and it is worth having in full: \\[ x(t) = \\int x(\\tau)\\,\\delta(t-"},{"t":"DC circuits","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/corriente-continua","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 7 of 15 DC circuits The load that receives the maximum power is the one that equals the internal resistance, and right there the efficiency is 50%. That is why in radio frequency you match, and in a power system you never do. Ohm's law Kirchhoff Dividers Thévenin Maximum transfer 01 Current and Ohm's law Current is charge crossing a cross-section per unit of time. Its conventional direction is that of positive charges, even though in a metal what moves are electrons and they go the other way: it is an inherited convention that is better accepted than fought. \\[ I = \\"},{"t":"Costs: the engineer and costs","d":"Topic · Industrial Organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/costos-ingenieria","x":"Industrial Organization · 48 h · Topic 5 of 7 Costs: the engineer and costs With ARS 600,000 of fixed costs per month and ARS 100 of contribution per unit, you must sell 6000 units to avoid a loss. Selling 8000, a 10 % increase in sales raises profit by 40 %: the operating leverage is 4. Fixed and variable costs Contribution margin Break-even point Margin of safety Operating leverage 01 Classifying costs A cost is the value of the resources consumed to produce. According to how they vary with volume, they are fixed —rent, overhead salaries, depreciation, which do not change with the quantity p"},{"t":"Sketching circuits","d":"Topic · Computer-Aided Design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/croquizado-circuitos","x":"Computer-Aided Design · 72 h · Topic 4 of 5 Sketching circuits Before the program there is the pencil. A clear sketch solves half the problems of a design, and it teaches you to think of the circuit in blocks before drawing a single wire. Sketch Proportion Blocks Connections Readability 01 Why sketch before opening the program A sketch is a freehand drawing, proportioned but not to scale, made to think. It does not compete with the CAD drawing: it comes before it. It is faster: you can try three arrangements in the time it takes to place one symbol on the computer. It forces you to decide the"},{"t":"Two-port networks","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/cuadripolos","x":"Circuit Theory II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 8 Two-port networks Two attenuators of 6 and 10 dB in cascade give 16 dB only if each one sees the impedance it was designed for. The transmission matrix tells you what happens with any other load. z and y parameters h parameters ABCD matrix Cascade Reciprocity 01 A box with two pairs of terminals Many circuits are used as a block with one input and one output: an amplifier, a filter, an attenuator, a transformer, a line. A two-port network describes them solely by what happens at their two pairs of terminals, regardless of how they are built inside. It h"},{"t":"Complex data containers","d":"Topic · Computer Science I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/datos-complejos","x":"Computer Science I · 120 h · Topic 5 of 6 Complex data containers Memory is a row of numbered bytes. Arrays, matrices, structures and lists are different ways of arranging data in that row and of knowing where each item ended up. Arrays Pointers Structures Dynamic memory Files 01 Why group data Storing the 1000 samples of an oscilloscope in a thousand separate variables is impossible to manage. Storing a measurement in three separate variables —voltage, current and time instant— forces you to always pass them together and never mix them up. Data containers solve both problems: the array groups"},{"t":"De Broglie and the uncertainty principle","d":"Topic · Electronic Physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/de-broglie","x":"Electronic Physics · 120 h · Topic 5 of 9 De Broglie and the uncertainty principle If a photon can behave as a particle, an electron can behave as a wave. Half of quantum mechanics came out of that symmetry, and so did the reason an atom does not collapse on itself. De Broglie Davisson-Germer Uncertainty Zero-point energy Electron microscope 01 The de Broglie hypothesis The photon has momentum without having mass: \\( p = h/\\lambda \\), the relation that closed the previous unit. In 1924 Louis de Broglie turned the question around: if a wave —light— behaves like a particle, why could a particle"},{"t":"Labor law","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/derecho-laboral","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 7 of 13 Labor law The same task, done by an employed engineer or by one who invoices as a freelancer, is subject to completely different rules. What decides which applies is the reality of the relationship, not what the paper says. Employment Contract Act Employment relationship Severance Collective bargaining agreements Occupational risks 01 Employee or independent contractor An engineer can work in two very different ways as far as the law is concerned. In an employment relationship (relación de dependencia), they make their work available to an employer who organi"},{"t":"Public law and private law","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/derecho-publico-privado","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 2 of 13 Public law and private law A contract with a company and a government tender may ask for the same equipment and be governed by very different rules. The difference lies in who is on the other side. Public law Private law Persons Legal acts Civil and Commercial Code 01 What law is Law is the set of rules that regulate people’s conduct in society and that the State can enforce by force if necessary. That last part distinguishes it from morality, from rules of etiquette or from a technical standard: a legal rule has behind it an apparatus —judges, police, oversi"},{"t":"Legal rights and duties of the professional","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/derechos-deberes-profesional","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 10 of 13 Legal rights and duties of the professional The signature both authorizes and binds: the same act that gives legal value to a drawing makes the signer responsible for what it says. Professional signature Fees Duty to inform Professional secrecy Intellectual property 01 The signature both authorizes and binds The professional license gives the engineer a set of rights that other people do not have, and in return imposes more demanding duties. Both are concentrated in the same act: the signature. A drawing, a certificate or a report signed by a licensed profes"},{"t":"Differentiable functions","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/derivada","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 4 of 10 Differentiable functions The derivative measures how fast something changes. In a circuit it is the current through a capacitor, the voltage across an inductor and the response speed of an amplifier. Difference quotient Tangent line Rules Chain rule Differential 01 The slope at a point The slope of a line is computed from two points. What about the slope of a curve, at a single point? The way out is to take two very close points, compute the slope of the line joining them —the secant — and see what value it approaches as the points come together. That limit i"},{"t":"Technology, industry and sustainable development","d":"Topic · Engineering and Society","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/desarrollo-sostenible","x":"Engineering and Society · 48 h · Topic 2 of 5 Technology, industry and sustainable development In a phone, almost all of the environmental footprint has already happened before it is switched on. Knowing where the impact really lies is what makes it possible to decide well at the drawing board. Brundtland Life cycle WEEE Efficiency Circular economy 01 What “sustainable development” was meant to say The definition that became standard dates from 1987, from the report Our Common Future of the World Commission on Environment and Development, known as the Brundtland Report: The Brundtland definiti"},{"t":"Determinants","d":"Topic · Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/determinantes","x":"Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry · 120 h · Topic 2 of 9 Determinants A single number tells you whether a matrix can be inverted, whether a system has a unique solution and by how much the associated transformation stretches or shrinks areas. Sarrus Cofactors Properties Cramer Geometric interpretation 01 A number that says whether the system holds up Every square matrix can be assigned a number, its determinant, which summarizes an essential property: if it is zero, the rows are linearly dependent —some row contributes no new information—, the matrix has no inverse and the associated system"},{"t":"Fluid dynamics","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/dinamica-fluidos","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 9 of 10 Fluid dynamics The flow that enters is the flow that leaves, and where the fluid speeds up the pressure drops. With those two ideas you can explain the wing of an airplane, the flowmeter and the fan of a heat sink. Flow rate Continuity Bernoulli Viscosity Reynolds 01 Flow rate and continuity The flow rate is the volume that passes through a cross-section per unit time, Q = A·v, in m³/s. If the fluid is incompressible and there are no leaks, what enters at one end leaves at the other: \\[ A_1 v_1 = A_2 v_2 = Q \\] Where the pipe narrows, the fluid speeds up. This"},{"t":"Dynamics of a particle","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/dinamica-punto","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 2 of 10 Dynamics of a particle Newton does not say what a force is: he says what it does. And what it does is change velocity. The whole craft lies in drawing the free-body diagram correctly before writing a single equation. Newton's laws Free-body diagram Friction Inclined plane Systems 01 The three laws, stated precisely First law · principle of inertia A body on which the net force is zero keeps its velocity: if it was at rest it stays at rest, and if it was moving it keeps moving in a straight line at constant speed. What needs explaining is not the motion, but th"},{"t":"Computer-aided design environments","d":"Topic · Computer-Aided Design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/diseno-asistido","x":"Computer-Aided Design · 72 h · Topic 5 of 5 Computer-aided design environments The program does not draw for you: it speeds up what you already know how to do. What CAD changes is that the drawing becomes a model with data, and from it come the bill of materials and the board. CAD EDA Layers Netlist PCB 01 What changes when the drawing becomes a model A paper drawing is a set of lines: the meaning is supplied by whoever reads it. A CAD drawing is a database: the program knows that this rectangle is a 470 Ω resistor, that it is connected to that node and that its package measures so much. All t"},{"t":"Analog integrated circuit design","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/diseno-ci-analogicos","x":"Applied Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 7 of 7 Analog integrated circuit design On a chip, no resistor is worth what the drawing says, but two neighboring resistors are almost identical. All integrated analog design rests on that paradox. Matching Pelgrom's law Common centroid gm/ID Monte Carlo 01 Designing with ratios Integrated analog design starts from a paradox. The absolute values on a chip are poor: a resistor can come out 20 % above or below its value, the threshold voltage of a transistor varies by tens of millivolts from one wafer to another, and no capacitance is exact. But two identic"},{"t":"Design of continuous and discrete controllers","d":"Topic · Control Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/diseno-control","x":"Control Systems · 96 h · Topic 8 of 8 Design of continuous and discrete controllers The same PID that controls a plant well in continuous time makes it oscillate if it is implemented by sampling every half second. The sampling period is part of the design. PID Ziegler–Nichols Digital control Sampling period Integral windup 01 The PID controller Most industrial loops use a three-action controller. The proportional action acts according to the current error; the integral, according to the accumulated error, and eliminates the steady-state error because it raises the type of the system, as seen i"},{"t":"Filter design","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/diseno-filtros","x":"Circuit Theory II · 120 h · Topic 8 of 8 Filter design In an FIR designed with windows, adding coefficients does not improve rejection: with the Hamming window it stays at 53 dB with 21 coefficients or with 101. What changes is the width of the transition. Windows Gibbs phenomenon Parks-McClellan Bilinear transform Prewarping 01 From specification to coefficients Designing a digital filter means following a fixed path: start from the template (bands, attenuations, sampling frequency), choose between FIR or IIR, compute the coefficients with an approximation method, choose the structure, quanti"},{"t":"Power electronic devices","d":"Topic · Power Electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/dispositivos-potencia","x":"Power Electronics · 96 h · Topic 1 of 8 Power electronic devices At 20 A, an IGBT loses less than a MOSFET below about 19 kHz; above that the MOSFET wins, and at 100 kHz the IGBT would dissipate almost twice as much. The switching frequency chooses the device. Thyristor Power MOSFET IGBT Silicon carbide Switching losses 01 The device as a switch Power electronics converts electrical energy —from AC to DC, from DC to AC, from one voltage to another— with efficiencies of 90 to 99 %. To do so, the semiconductors do not work in their linear region, as in an amplifier, but as switches: fully closed"},{"t":"Random variables and distributions","d":"Topic · Probability and Statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/distribuciones","x":"Probability and Statistics · 72 h · Topic 3 of 6 Random variables and distributions Thermal noise is normal, the arrival of photons at a detector is Poisson and the time to failure of a component is exponential. Choosing the wrong distribution gives answers that look reasonable and are not. Random variable Expected value Normal Poisson Exponential 01 From events to numbers A random variable is a function that assigns a number to each outcome of the experiment. That step, which looks bureaucratic, is what makes it possible to calculate averages, variances and everything else: events cannot be a"},{"t":"The circuit in the transformed domain","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/dominio-transformado","x":"Circuit Theory II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 8 The circuit in the transformed domain In the Laplace domain, a charged capacitor is an impedance 1/sC in series with a source v(0)/s. The initial conditions stop being a separate problem and become part of the circuit. Laplace transform Initial conditions Transfer function Poles Damping 01 Why transform A circuit with inductors and capacitors is described by differential equations, and solving them in the time domain requires setting up the homogeneous and particular solutions and fitting constants to the initial conditions. The Laplace transform turns"},{"t":"Digital signal processing and hardware","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/dsp-hardware","x":"Digital Techniques III · 120 h · Topic 1 of 3 Digital signal processing and hardware A filter with a Blackman window attenuates 75 dB in floating point. With 12-bit coefficients, barely 57: the precision of the hardware decides which filter can be implemented. Multiply-accumulate Harvard architecture Q15 fixed point DMA and blocks FFT 01 An operation repeated millions of times Almost all digital signal processing boils down to sums of products. An \\( N \\)-coefficient FIR filter computes each output sample with \\( N \\) multiplications and \\( N \\) additions; so does an FFT, a correlation or an e"},{"t":"Ordinary differential equations","d":"Topic · Calculus II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ecuaciones-diferenciales","x":"Calculus II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 8 Ordinary differential equations A circuit with a capacitor or an inductor is described not by an algebraic equation but by a differential one. Solving it means predicting how the circuit evolves in time. First order Second order Free response Forced RLC 01 Equations where the unknown is a function A differential equation relates a function to its derivatives. Its unknown is not a number: it is the entire function. Differential equations appear as soon as anything changes in time, and in electronics they appear as soon as there is a capacitor or an inductor, b"},{"t":"The professional practice of engineering","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ejercicio-profesional","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 8 of 13 The professional practice of engineering A university degree is not enough to sign: you need the license from the council or association of the jurisdiction where you work. Qualifying degree Reserved activities License Professional councils COPITEC 01 From the degree to the signature Graduating is not enough to sign. Between the university degree and the first signed drawing there are two steps regulated by law: The qualifying degree (título habilitante), issued by a university with an accredited program, which defines what the graduate is qualified to do. Th"},{"t":"Choosing the product and regulatory requirements","d":"Topic · Final Project","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/eleccion-producto","x":"Final Project · 96 h · Topic 1 of 4 Choosing the product and regulatory requirements Among an energy meter, an irrigation controller and a medical monitor, the decision matrix picks the irrigation controller if feasibility and regulations carry weight, and the monitor if only the market and the team’s interest do. The weights are also a decision. Need and user Requirements Specification Decision matrix Regulatory requirements 01 From the idea to the need The final project integrates the degree into a product or system that solves a real problem. Ideas can come from a need observed in a company"},{"t":"Electrochemistry","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/electroquimica","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 11 of 12 Electrochemistry That aluminum wire is not spliced to copper is no electrician’s whim: it is two volts of difference in the table of potentials, and with moisture that is a cell that eats the aluminum. Redox Anode and cathode Nernst Galvanic corrosion Batteries 01 Electrons that change owners All of electrochemistry is an oxidation-reduction reaction in which the electrons are forced to pass through a wire instead of being handed over directly. That is the only difference between a nail that rusts and a battery: the path. Occurs in What happens Electr"},{"t":"Electrostatics","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/electrostatica","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 15 Electrostatics The damage threshold of a MOSFET starts at about 100 V, while the threshold of human perception is near 3000. A component can be destroyed by a discharge that the person did not feel. Coulomb's law Electric field Gauss's law Potential Electrostatic discharge 01 Coulomb's law and the field Two charges at rest attract or repel each other with a force that falls with the square of the distance. The form is identical to that of gravitation, with one decisive difference: charge has two signs, and that is why the electric force can cancel. \\[ F = k\\,"},{"t":"The company, productivity and standards","d":"Topic · Industrial Organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/empresa-productividad","x":"Industrial Organization · 48 h · Topic 2 of 7 The company, productivity and standards A line with 90 % availability, 86 % performance and 97 % good parts seems to be working well, but its overall effectiveness is 76 %: of 450 planned minutes, only 340 produce good parts at the ideal speed. Production system Organizational structure Productivity Standards Overall equipment effectiveness (OEE) 01 The company as a production system An industrial company transforms inputs —materials, energy, labor, capital, information— into products and services that it sells in a market. It can be seen as a syst"},{"t":"Standards-based testing","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ensayos-normas","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 10 of 11 Standards-based testing A power supply that must give 5.00 V ± 1% measures 5.045 V with an uncertainty of 10 mV. It seems to comply, but there is almost a 16% probability that it does not. IEC standards Electromagnetic compatibility Electrical safety Decision rule Guard band 01 A test anyone can repeat A standards-based test is a measurement with a procedure fixed in advance: what is measured, with which instruments, under which environmental conditions, with which setup and by which criterion it is accepted. The goal is that two different lab"},{"t":"The complex envelope","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/envolvente-compleja","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 3 of 11 The complex envelope A 100 MHz radio signal can be simulated entirely with two numbers that change a few thousand times per second. The carrier carries no information: it only moves it. Bandpass signal I and Q components Complex envelope I/Q receiver PAPR 01 A radio signal is a carrier with two pieces of data Almost all communication signals are bandpass: they occupy a narrow band around a carrier of frequency \\( f_c \\) much greater than their bandwidth. An FM radio occupies 200 kHz around 100 MHz; a Wi-Fi channel, 20 MHz around 2.4 GHz. Any such si"},{"t":"Equilibrium in solutions","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio-soluciones","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 10 of 12 Equilibrium in solutions Hydrofluoric acid is a weak acid and the most dangerous reagent in an electronics laboratory. The two facts do not contradict each other: the strength of an acid measures its dissociation equilibrium, not its toxicity. pH Ka and pKa Titration Buffers Solubility product 01 Water ionizes itself Two water molecules exchange a proton, and everything else follows from that equilibrium. Its constant is small but not zero, and that is enough to fix the scale on which acidity is measured: \\[ \\mathrm{2H_2O \\rightleftharpoons H_3O^+ + O"},{"t":"Chemical equilibrium","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/equilibrio","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 9 of 12 Chemical equilibrium Le Chatelier says that ammonia is best made cold, and kinetics says that cold is never how it is made. Almost every industrial process lives in the negotiation between those two answers. Reversibility Constant K Le Chatelier Van 't Hoff Haber-Bosch 01 Reactions that come back Many reactions do not go to completion: they reach a point at which the forward and reverse reactions occur at the same rate and the concentrations stop changing. That is chemical equilibrium, and it is dynamic: underneath everything keeps happening, but the n"},{"t":"Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/errores-incertidumbre","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 1 of 11 Errors, uncertainty and instrument accuracy Measuring 5 V on the 200 V range of a multimeter instead of the 20 V range multiplies the uncertainty by six. The instrument is the same; the decision belongs to whoever is measuring. Systematic error Type A and Type B Expanded uncertainty Specification Traceability 01 No measurement is exact To measure is to compare a quantity with a reference, and no comparison is perfect. The value an instrument gives differs from the true one because of the instrument, the method, the environment and the person me"},{"t":"Vector spaces","d":"Topic · Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espacios-vectoriales","x":"Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry · 120 h · Topic 7 of 9 Vector spaces Signals, polynomials and matrices are added and scaled by the same rules as arrows in the plane. That common structure is what is studied here. Subspaces Linear independence Basis Dimension Change of basis 01 The same structure in very different things Arrows in the plane can be added and multiplied by numbers. So can 2 × 3 matrices. And polynomials of degree at most 3. And voltage signals as functions of time. In all four cases the same rules hold, so it makes sense to study them once and for all: that is a vector space"},{"t":"Spectra","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/espectros","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 25 of 34 Spectra An analyzer cannot observe forever, and that truncation has consequences: a pure tone appears smeared out. Windows correct it at the cost of resolution, and choosing one is choosing what you give up. Spectrum Spectral density Leakage Windows Resolution 01 What is plotted when you plot a spectrum The transform returns one complex number for each frequency, so two plots are needed to show it in full: the magnitude plot and the phase plot. \\[ X(j\\omega) = |X(j\\omega)|\\,e^{\\,j\\angle X(j\\omega)} \\] In practice you almost always look at the magnit"},{"t":"Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-compensacion","x":"Applied Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 5 of 8 Stability, compensation, oscillators and slew rate To stabilize a follower, adding a new pole leaves less than 1 kHz of bandwidth. Lowering the pole that was already there leaves 845 kHz with the same phase margin. Phase margin Dominant pole Miller compensation Slew rate Wien bridge 01 When a feedback amplifier oscillates The denominator \\( 1 + T(s) \\) vanishes if at some frequency the loop gain is exactly −1: magnitude 1 and phase −180°. A signal of that frequency goes around the loop and returns unchanged, with no excitation at all. The Nyquist c"},{"t":"Stability of control systems","d":"Topic · Control Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad-control","x":"Control Systems · 96 h · Topic 5 of 8 Stability of control systems In 1877 Routh found how to tell whether a polynomial has unstable roots without computing them. For a third-order loop, the same table gives the gain at which it starts to oscillate and the frequency. Characteristic equation Routh–Hurwitz Critical gain Gain margin Relative stability 01 What a stable system is A linear system is stable if every bounded input produces a bounded output. For a system described by a rational transfer function, that happens if and only if all its poles have negative real part: each pole contributes t"},{"t":"Stability","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estabilidad","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 19 of 34 Stability Every bounded input has to produce a bounded output, and a single exception is enough to rule out stability. An ideal integrator is the closest example: it does not satisfy it. BIBO Integrability Worst case Poles Integrator 01 Bounded input, bounded output The stability criterion used in signals has a name of its own: BIBO, for bounded input, bounded output. A system is stable if every bounded input produces a bounded output: \\[ |x(t)| \\le M \\lt \\infty \\;\\;\\text{for all } t \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; |y(t)| \\le K \\lt \\infty \\;\\;\\text{for all } t"},{"t":"Descriptive statistics","d":"Topic · Probability and Statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estadistica-descriptiva","x":"Probability and Statistics · 72 h · Topic 1 of 6 Descriptive statistics A measured value without its spread is not a measurement: it is a number. All the descriptive statistics in this unit exist so that you can write the “plus or minus” that follows the value. Mean and median Standard deviation Quartiles Histogram Outliers 01 Why a single number is not enough Measure the same resistance ten times with the same instrument and you do not get the same number ten times. That scatter is not a flaw in the procedure: it is information, and throwing it away amounts to inventing a precision you do not"},{"t":"Steady state and system type","d":"Topic · Control Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estado-permanente","x":"Control Systems · 96 h · Topic 4 of 8 Steady state and system type Each integrator in the loop makes it possible to follow, without error, an input one degree harder. A motor position control follows a step exactly because the motor already integrates. Final value System type Position constant Velocity constant Integrator 01 The error once everything has settled The transient response tells how the system gets there; the steady state, where it ends up. In a loop with unity feedback and loop gain \\( G(s) \\), the error between the reference and the output is \\( E(s) = R(s)/(1 + G(s)) \\), and its"},{"t":"States of matter","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estados-agregacion","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 5 of 12 States of matter Chips are made in vacuum chambers, and the reason is not cleanliness: it is that at 10⁻⁶ torr an evaporated atom travels fifty meters without colliding with anything. Ideal gases Van der Waals Phase diagram Triple point Vacuum 01 Gases, and why they are the easy ones In a gas the molecules are so far apart that they hardly notice one another. That mutual indifference is what makes it possible to describe the gas with a single equation, the same for all gases: \\[ PV = nRT \\qquad R = 0.082\\ \\frac{\\text{atm}\\cdot\\text{L}}{\\text{mol}\\cdot\\"},{"t":"Statics","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estatica","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 5 of 10 Statics That something does not move does not mean there are no forces: it means they cancel. Two vector equations hold up bridges, shelves and the support of an antenna. Equilibrium Moments Center of gravity Supports Trusses 01 The two conditions of equilibrium A body not moving does not mean there are no forces: it means they cancel. And for an extended body it is not enough for the forces to cancel, because a pair of equal and opposite forces applied at different points makes it rotate. Two conditions are needed: \\[ \\sum_{} \\vec{F} = 0 \\quad \\sum_{} \\vec{M}"},{"t":"Stoichiometry and the energy of reactions","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estequiometria","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 6 of 12 Stoichiometry and the energy of reactions When one micron of silicon is oxidized, 2.27 microns of oxide grow. That number does not come from a measurement: it comes from the balanced equation, and it has to be taken into account when designing the gate thickness. Balancing Limiting reactant Yield Enthalpy Hess's law 01 The balanced equation is a recipe A balanced chemical equation does not say what happens: it says in what proportion it happens. The coefficients are the recipe, and they are read in moles, not in grams or in volumes. \\[ \\mathrm{Si} + \\m"},{"t":"Structure of matter","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructura-materia","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 3 of 12 Structure of matter That silicon has four valence electrons and phosphorus has five is not an isolated fact: it is the reason one forms crystals and the other serves to dope them. All of solid-state electronics starts here. Orbitals Quantum numbers Configuration Periodic table Valence 01 Where the electrons are The Bohr model —electrons circling in orbits— is enough to draw a picture, but not to explain. The current model says that an electron has no trajectory but a probability distribution, and that its state is described by four numbers: Number What"},{"t":"Dynamic data structures","d":"Topic · Computer Science II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/estructuras-dinamicas","x":"Computer Science II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 8 Dynamic data structures When you do not know in advance how much data is going to arrive, the array gets in the way. The linked list grows and shrinks by following pointers, and with that mechanism you build queues, stacks and trees. Linked list Nodes Stack Queue Binary tree 01 When the array is not enough An array is fast and simple, but its size is fixed, decided before the program runs. If more data arrives than expected, it overflows; if less arrives, memory is wasted. A dynamic structure grows and shrinks during execution, allocating one node per"},{"t":"Ethics in professional practice","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/etica-profesional","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 9 of 13 Ethics in professional practice Almost all professional ethics violations are legal until someone gets hurt. Codes exist so that the decision is made beforehand. Deontology Code of ethics Conflict of interest Professional secrecy Ethics tribunal 01 Ethics, morality and deontology Morality is the set of convictions of a person or a community about what is right and what is wrong. Ethics is the reflection on those convictions: why something is right, how to decide when two duties collide. Professional deontology is ethics applied to a profession, written into c"},{"t":"Economic evaluation of investment projects","d":"Topic · Economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/evaluacion-proyectos","x":"Economics · 72 h · Topic 4 of 4 Economic evaluation of investment projects Investing one million to receive 300 thousand per year for five years yields an internal rate of return of 15.2 %: it is worthwhile if capital costs 12 %, with a net present value of 81 thousand, and stops being worthwhile if it costs 20 %. Time value of money Cash flow Net present value Internal rate of return Payback period 01 The time value of money A peso today is worth more than a peso a year from now, because it can be invested and because the future is uncertain. To compare amounts from different moments they are"},{"t":"The complex exponential signal","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/exponencial-compleja","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 16 of 34 The complex exponential signal If a complex exponential enters an LTI system, the same exponential comes out multiplied by a number. No other signal does that, and all of frequency analysis comes from it. Eigenfunction Euler s-plane Phasors Negative frequency 01 The most important function in the subject There is a precise reason why all of signal analysis revolves around the complex exponential, and it is not an aesthetic one. It is this: \\[ x(t) = e^{st} \\;\\longrightarrow\\; \\boxed{\\text{LTI system}} \\;\\longrightarrow\\; y(t) = H(s)\\,e^{st} \\] The w"},{"t":"The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fet-amplificador","x":"Applied Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 3 of 7 The field-effect transistor as an amplifier: small and large signal The FET parabola distorts only in the second harmonic, and by an exact amount: for the same input, almost eight times less than a bipolar. The price is amplifying less per milliampere. Common source Transconductance Overdrive Second harmonic Source follower 01 A square law instead of an exponential The field-effect transistor —the MOSFET and the JFET— amplifies with the same idea as the bipolar: an input voltage controls an output current. The difference lies in the shape of that re"},{"t":"Optical fibers and fiber transmission","d":"Topic · Transmission Media","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fibras-opticas","x":"Transmission Media · 96 h · Topic 6 of 8 Optical fibers and fiber transmission At 10 Gb/s, a multimode fiber has power to spare for 2 km, but dispersion cuts the link at 300 m. On a single-mode fiber, the same transmitter reaches tens of kilometers. Numerical aperture Single-mode and multimode Windows Dispersion Link budget 01 Light through a thread of glass An optical fiber is a dielectric waveguide that works at frequencies of about 200 THz. It has a silica glass core surrounded by a cladding of slightly lower refractive index, and the light is trapped in the core. Compared with a copper cab"},{"t":"Active and passive analog filters","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-analogicos","x":"Circuit Theory II · 120 h · Topic 5 of 8 Active and passive analog filters To reject 40 dB at twice the cutoff frequency, a Butterworth needs seven poles and a 0.5 dB Chebyshev, five. The ripple in the passband buys steepness. Template Butterworth Chebyshev LC ladder Sallen-Key 01 The filter template A filter is specified with a template: in the passband, up to \\( \\omega_p \\), the attenuation cannot exceed \\( A_p \\); in the stopband, from \\( \\omega_s \\) on, it has to be at least \\( A_s \\). Between the two lies the transition band. The narrower the transition and the more demanding \\( A_p \\) an"},{"t":"IIR and FIR digital filters","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/filtros-digitales","x":"Circuit Theory II · 120 h · Topic 7 of 8 IIR and FIR digital filters An eight-sample average and an exponential with α = 0.22 reduce noise equally. The first needs eight samples in memory; the second, a single variable. FIR IIR Linear phase Moving average Fixed point 01 Two families of digital filters A digital filter is a difference equation that a processor computes at every sample. If it uses only inputs, its impulse response is finite ( FIR ); if it feeds back previous outputs, it is infinite ( IIR ). The choice between the two is the first design decision: FIR IIR Stability Always stable:"},{"t":"Semiconductor physics","d":"Topic · Electronic Physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fisica-semiconductores","x":"Electronic Physics · 120 h · Topic 9 of 9 Semiconductor physics Every topic in this subject left a piece ready for this one: the photon, tunneling, Fermi-Dirac statistics, the Pauli principle. Here they all come together in the band gap and the Fermi level, which is where a diode comes from. Band gap Effective mass Law of mass action Fermi level Silicon 01 From atomic levels to bands The unit on atomic models ended with a pending idea: when \\( 10^{22} \\) atoms come together in a crystal, Pauli forces the single atomic level of each one to split into as many levels as there are atoms, so close"},{"t":"Fluids at rest","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fluidos-equilibrio","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 8 of 10 Fluids at rest The pressure in a fluid at rest depends only on depth. From this come the hydraulic press, the float and the measurement of pressure with a column of mercury. Pressure Pascal Hydrostatics Archimedes Manometers 01 Pressure A fluid at rest cannot sustain shear stresses: it only exerts forces perpendicular to any surface it touches. That force per unit area is the pressure, and it is a scalar: at a point it has the same value in all directions. \\[ p = \\frac{F}{A} \\quad [ \\mathrm{Pa} ] = [ N / m^2 ] \\] Unit Equivalent to Where it is used 1 Pa 1 N/m²"},{"t":"Quadratic forms and conics","d":"Topic · Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formas-cuadraticas","x":"Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry · 120 h · Topic 6 of 9 Quadratic forms and conics The energy stored in a circuit, the power dissipated and the ellipse traced by a Lissajous figure are all written the same way: as a quadratic form. Associated matrix Conics Principal axes Definiteness Energy 01 Second-degree polynomials, written with a matrix A quadratic form is a homogeneous polynomial of degree two: all its terms have degree two. In two variables, q(x, y) = a x² + b xy + c y². What makes it interesting is that it can be written with a symmetric matrix, splitting the cross term in half: \\["},{"t":"Cauchy’s integral formula","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/formula-cauchy","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 8 of 34 Cauchy’s integral formula The values of an analytic function on a closed curve completely determine its value everywhere inside. There is nothing like it in real calculus, and from that rigidity come Liouville’s theorem and the fundamental theorem of algebra. Cauchy’s formula Mean value Derivatives Liouville Maximum modulus 01 The value inside, read from the boundary The previous theorem says when an integral is zero. This one does something much stronger: it says that the values of an analytic function on a closed curve completely determine what the"},{"t":"The photoelectric effect and duality","d":"Topic · Electronic Physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fotoelectrico","x":"Electronic Physics · 120 h · Topic 4 of 9 The photoelectric effect and duality Einstein won the Nobel Prize for this, not for relativity. A one-line equation explained three facts that wave-like light could not, and in passing gave a name to the particle that a photomultiplier now counts one at a time. Photon Work function Millikan Compton Duality 01 The experiment that waves cannot explain Light shining on a metal plate inside an evacuated tube knocks out electrons, which a collector picks up as current. None of that surprises wave theory: a wave carries energy, and that energy can free charg"},{"t":"Switching power supplies","d":"Topic · Power Electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-conmutadas-ing","x":"Power Electronics · 96 h · Topic 3 of 8 Switching power supplies A buck converter that delivers 12 V at 2.4 A rises to 15.4 V when the load drops to 0.77 A, without touching the duty cycle: it has entered discontinuous conduction. That is why every switching supply needs feedback. Buck Boost Flyback Discontinuous conduction Current-mode control 01 The principle A linear supply regulates by dissipating the difference between input and output, as seen in linear regulated power supplies. A switching supply, by contrast, chops the voltage with a switch that opens and closes at a fixed frequency, a"},{"t":"Current sources and active loads","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-corriente","x":"Applied Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 4 of 7 Current sources and active loads Replacing a collector resistor with a transistor takes the gain of a stage from a few hundred to almost two thousand. It is the trick that makes an operational amplifier possible. Current mirror Widlar source Cascode Output resistance Active load 01 Transistors instead of resistors In an integrated circuit, resistors are expensive —they take up a lot of area—, imprecise —they vary by 20 % from one wafer to another— and cannot be very large. Transistors, on the other hand, are cheap and, most importantly, identical to"},{"t":"Linear regulated power supplies","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-reguladas-lineales","x":"Applied Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 7 of 8 Linear regulated power supplies With a 7.5 V secondary, a 7805 loses regulation a hundred times per second and an LDO regulates with almost 1 V of margin. What decides it is the bottom of the ripple, not the average voltage. Rectifier and filter Ripple Series regulator Dropout voltage Dissipation 01 From the mains to a stable DC voltage A linear power supply has four blocks: a transformer that steps down the mains voltage and isolates, a rectifier, a filter capacitor that converts the pulses into a DC voltage with ripple, and a regulator that elimi"},{"t":"Signal sources","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/fuentes-senales","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 3 of 11 Signal sources A DDS with a 100 MHz clock adjusts the frequency in steps of 0.023 Hz, but at 45 MHz its image is almost at the same level as the signal. That is why nobody uses it beyond 40% of its clock. Function generator DDS Tuning word Images 50 Ω output 01 Half of every measurement To measure the response of an amplifier, a filter or a receiver, it must be excited with a known signal. The signal source is the other half of the measurement bench, and its imperfections —distortion, frequency error, phase noise, amplitude that changes with fr"},{"t":"Functions of a complex variable","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-complejas","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 2 of 34 Functions of a complex variable A complex function is not plotted as a curve: you show what it turns each figure of the plane into. That is how you see where the Smith chart comes from, and why the complex exponential describes everything that oscillates and dies out. Mapping Complex exponential Logarithm Conformal Bilinear 01 A function that cannot be graphed A real function \\( y = f(x) \\) is drawn as a curve: two axes are enough. A complex function \\( w = f(z) \\) takes a point of the plane and returns another point of the plane: it would take four"},{"t":"Continuous functions","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-continuas","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 3 of 10 Continuous functions Continuity is not a technical detail: it is what lets you guarantee that a root exists, that a maximum exists and that the voltage across a capacitor cannot jump. Discontinuities Bolzano Intermediate value Weierstrass Bisection 01 Three conditions, not one A function is continuous at a point a when all three of these hold at once: \\[ 1) \\; \\exists f (a) \\quad 2) \\; \\exists \\lim_{x \\to a} f (x) \\quad 3) \\; \\lim_{x \\to a} f (x) = f (a) \\] The value exists, the limit exists and they coincide. If any one of the three fails, there is a discont"},{"t":"Network functions: analysis and synthesis","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-red","x":"Circuit Theory II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 8 Network functions: analysis and synthesis The same impedance can be built with series LC tank circuits or with a ladder of inductors and capacitors. The two circuits look nothing alike and are indistinguishable from their terminals. Positive real function Foster’s theorem Foster I Cauer I Synthesis 01 Network functions A network function is the ratio of two transformed quantities of a circuit with zero initial conditions. If both are taken at the same pair of terminals, it is a driving-point function: an impedance \\( Z(s) \\) or an admittance \\( Y(s) \\)."},{"t":"Functions of one real variable","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-variable-real","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 1 of 10 Functions of one real variable A function is a rule that assigns exactly one output to each input. With that idea you can describe the characteristic of a diode, the gain of an amplifier and any signal in time. Domain and range Composition Inverse Transformations Transfer 01 One input, exactly one output A function of one real variable is a rule that assigns to each number in a starting set one and only one number in a target set. That uniqueness is the whole definition, and it is what makes functions useful in engineering: if a circuit is a function of its i"},{"t":"Scalar functions of several variables","d":"Topic · Calculus II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-varias-variables","x":"Calculus II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 8 Scalar functions of several variables The temperature of a plate, the potential around two charges or the gain of an amplifier as a function of two components: all of them are functions of several variables. Domain Level curves Surfaces Limits Continuity 01 One output, several inputs The temperature of a plate depends on where it is measured: T(x, y). The power dissipated by a resistor depends on the voltage and the resistance: P(V, R). The gain of a stage depends on two components. All of these are scalar functions of several variables: two or more numbers g"},{"t":"Vector-valued functions of one real variable","d":"Topic · Calculus II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/funciones-vectoriales","x":"Calculus II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 8 Vector-valued functions of one real variable When a function returns a vector instead of a number, it describes a motion. Differentiating it gives the velocity; differentiating again gives the acceleration, and almost all of kinematics follows from there. Parametric curves Tangent vector Arc length Curvature Frenet 01 A function that returns a vector So far, functions have taken a number and returned a number. A vector-valued function of one real variable takes a number —typically time— and returns a vector: the position of something that is moving. \\[ \\vec{r"},{"t":"Synthesized signal generators","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/generadores-sintetizados","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 10 Synthesized signal generators For 1 MHz channels at 2.4 GHz, an integer-N synthesizer multiplies the phase noise of its comparator by 2440. A fractional one, by 49: its phase noise ends up 17 dB lower. PLL N divider Phase noise Fractional-N Spurs 01 From the DDS to the phase-locked loop Direct digital synthesis in the signal sources of Measurements I gives very fine resolution, but its output cannot exceed a fraction of the clock. To generate signals from hundreds of megahertz to several gigahertz with the accuracy of a crystal, RF generators"},{"t":"Environmental diagnosis and management instruments","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-ambiental","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 9 of 11 Environmental diagnosis and management instruments Replacing an office device that works with one that is 30% more efficient can take decades to offset the emissions of manufacturing it. In electronics, using things longer is usually the cleanest choice. Impact assessment Leopold matrix ISO 14001 Life cycle Carbon footprint 01 Measuring in order to manage Environmental management needs tools to answer concrete questions: what impact a project will have before it is built, whether a plant meets what it promised, how much a product pollutes o"},{"t":"Occupational risk management","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/gestion-riesgo-laboral","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 5 of 11 Occupational risk management In the matrix, dielectric gloves lower the electric shock risk at the bench from 15 to 10. An isolation transformer with a residual-current device lowers it to 4: it pays to act on the hazard. Hazard and risk Risk matrix HAZOP ALARP ISO 45001 01 Hazard and risk A hazard is a source of possible harm: a 220 V voltage, a flammable solvent, a ladder. Risk combines the likelihood that the harm occurs with the severity of its consequences. The same hazard can give very different risks: 220 V in a closed panel with an"},{"t":"Waveguides and modes","d":"Topic · Transmission Media","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/guias-onda","x":"Transmission Media · 96 h · Topic 3 of 8 Waveguides and modes A waveguide is a pipe that does not let low frequencies through: a WR-90 blocks everything below 6.56 GHz. The holes in the door of a microwave oven work the same way. TE10 mode Cutoff frequency Guide wavelength Group velocity Single-mode band 01 A pipe that carries waves Above a few gigahertz, a coaxial cable starts to lose a lot: the center conductor is thin, the skin effect concentrates the current on its surface and the dielectric absorbs. To carry microwave power with low losses, a hollow metal tube is used, the waveguide. It h"},{"t":"Industrial hygiene and safety","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/higiene-seguridad-industrial","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 1 of 11 Industrial hygiene and safety Fifteen minutes at 100 dBA use up the entire noise dose of an eight-hour working day. Every additional 3 dB cuts the permitted time in half. Risk agents Noise Daily dose Equivalent level Hierarchy of controls 01 Two complementary disciplines Safety at work deals with accidents: sudden events that cause injury, such as a fall, being caught in machinery or an electric shock. Hygiene deals with occupational diseases: harm that sets in slowly through repeated exposure to an agent, such as deafness from noise or lea"},{"t":"Origins and evolution of industrial organization","d":"Topic · Industrial Organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/historia-organizacion","x":"Industrial Organization · 48 h · Topic 1 of 7 Origins and evolution of industrial organization In 1936, T. P. Wright observed that every time the number of aircraft built doubled, the hours per aircraft dropped to 80 %. With that rule, unit one hundred takes less than a quarter of the time of the first. Scientific management Fayol Assembly line Toyota system Learning curve 01 The precursors Industrial organization was born with the factory. In The Wealth of Nations (1776), Adam Smith described how, in a pin factory, dividing the work into simple operations multiplied output: each worker gained"},{"t":"The social dimension and impact of engineering","d":"Topic · Engineering and Society","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impacto-social","x":"Engineering and Society · 48 h · Topic 3 of 5 The social dimension and impact of engineering Design decisions distribute costs, benefits and possibilities before anyone uses the product. That is why the designer is not a neutral executor. Winner First and second order Technology assessment Risk Accessibility 01 No artifact is socially neutral A comfortable idea says that technology is a neutral tool and that everything depends on how it is used. It is partly true and, taken literally, false: design decisions distribute possibilities. A device that can only be repaired at the official service c"},{"t":"System elements and implementation","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/implementacion-sistemas","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 29 of 34 System elements and implementation Adder, gain and memory: with those three pieces any system can be built. How they are arranged is not a matter of indifference, and that is why no high-order digital filter is implemented in direct form. Block diagram Direct form Biquad FIR IIR 01 Three pieces and nothing else Any LTI system, however complicated, is built from three elements. There is no fourth: Element What it does In continuous time In discrete time Adder \\( y = x_1 + x_2 \\) A node, or a summing amplifier An addition Gain \\( y = a\\,x \\) A resisti"},{"t":"Unit impulse and unit step","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/impulso-escalon","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 20 of 34 Unit impulse and unit step The impulse is not a function with a shape: it is the limit of any family of pulses that narrows while keeping area one. All you need to know about it is how it behaves inside an integral. Step Impulse Sifting Distribution Impulse response 01 The step, which holds no mystery Let us start with the easy one. The unit step is zero before and one after: \\[ u(t) = \\begin{cases} 1 & t \\gt 0 \\\\ 0 & t \\lt 0 \\end{cases} \\qquad\\qquad u[n] = \\begin{cases} 1 & n \\ge 0 \\\\ 0 & n \\lt 0 \\end{cases} \\] In continuous time the value at \\( t"},{"t":"Magnetic induction","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/induccion-magnetica","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 9 of 15 Magnetic induction Interrupting one ampere in one microsecond through a 10 mH coil generates ten thousand volts. The coil produces whatever voltage it takes so that its current does not change abruptly, and if nobody gives it a path, it opens one itself. Faraday Lenz Self-induction Transformer Eddy currents 01 Faraday’s law A magnetic field that changes in time creates an electric field. That is the discovery that joins electricity and magnetism into a single subject instead of two separate ones, and it is what makes it possible to generate electric power at"},{"t":"Inference and estimation","d":"Topic · Probability and Statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inferencia","x":"Probability and Statistics · 72 h · Topic 4 of 6 Inference and estimation “95 % confidence” does not mean there is a 95 % probability that the value lies in that interval. The 95 % is a property of the procedure, not of the interval you ended up with, and that distinction is not understood by reading about it: you have to watch it fail. Population and sample Standard error Confidence interval Student’s t Sample size 01 From the sample to the population You never measure everything. You measure a sample and want to say something about the entire population: the whole batch, all the parts the pr"},{"t":"Protection and sizing of installations","d":"Topic · Electrical Machines and Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/instalaciones-dimensionamiento","x":"Electrical Machines and Installations · 96 h · Topic 4 of 5 Protection and sizing of installations A 3 kW circuit 30 m from the panel carries the current with 1.5 mm² cables, but the voltage drops almost 6%. The length decides the cross-section, not the current. Thermal-magnetic breaker Residual-current device Current-carrying capacity Voltage drop Power factor 01 Two risks, two protections An electrical installation has to protect against two different things. Overcurrent —overload or short circuit— heats the conductors until the insulation is damaged and a fire starts. Electric contact makes"},{"t":"The definite integral","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integral-definida","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 7 of 10 The definite integral The definite integral is an area, and an area is a sum of infinitely many infinitely thin rectangles. From it come the mean value and the RMS value of any signal. Riemann sums Area Mean value RMS value Properties 01 An area built from rectangles How much charge did a varying current deliver? How much energy did a resistor dissipate? The two questions are the same: the area under a curve. And the area under an arbitrary curve has no formula, so it is constructed: the interval is split into n small pieces, a rectangle is raised over each o"},{"t":"Line integrals in the complex plane","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-complejas","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 6 of 34 Line integrals in the complex plane Between two points of the plane there are infinitely many paths, and the integral is defined along one of them. Each term of the sum is a vector, so the integral can be seen: it is a chain of little arrows whose end point is the result. Contour integral Parametrize 2πj ML bound Bromwich 01 Integrating along a path For a real variable, \\( \\int_a^b \\) has only one possible route: from the point \\( a \\) to the point \\( b \\) along the line. In the plane there are infinitely many paths between two points, and the integr"},{"t":"Line and surface integrals","d":"Topic · Calculus II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-linea-superficie","x":"Calculus II · 120 h · Topic 7 of 8 Line and surface integrals The work done by a force along a path and the flux of a field through a surface are the two integrals in which electromagnetism is written. Circulation Work Path independence Flux Surfaces 01 Integrating along a curve Until now we integrated over intervals and over regions. Now the domain is a curve. If what is being integrated is a scalar field —a density, for example—, the line integral gives the total quantity along the wire: \\[ \\int_C f \\, d s = \\int_a^b f (\\vec{r} (t)) \\left| {\\vec{r}}' (t) \\right| \\, d t \\] The factor |r′(t)|"},{"t":"Double and triple integrals","d":"Topic · Calculus II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/integrales-multiples","x":"Calculus II · 120 h · Topic 5 of 8 Double and triple integrals Integrating in more than one variable means summing over a region of the plane or of space: areas, volumes, masses and distributed charges. Double integral Order of integration Polar Cylindrical Center of mass 01 Summing over a region The single integral summed over an interval. The double integral sums over a region of the plane: the region is cut into small rectangles, the function is evaluated in each one, multiplied by its area, and all of them are added up. \\[ \\iint_D f (x, y) d A = \\lim_{n \\to \\infty} \\sum_{i = 1}^n f (x_i^*,"},{"t":"Heat transfer mechanisms","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercambio-calor","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 15 Heat transfer mechanisms The thermal chain of a transistor is solved exactly like a resistive divider: power plays the role of current, temperature that of voltage, and each interface that of a resistance. With that analogy, calculating a heat sink is a circuits exercise. Conduction Convection Radiation Thermal resistance Heat sinks 01 Three ways to move heat Heat always goes from the hot body to the cold one, and it arrives by three paths that act at the same time. In electronics all three are present in any piece of equipment, but they do not weigh the same"},{"t":"System intercomparison","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/intercomparacion-sistemas","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 11 of 11 System intercomparison No matter how much bandwidth is used, no system can transmit with less than −1.59 dB of energy per bit over the noise density. An uncoded QPSK needs 9.6 dB for one error in 100,000 bits. Power and bandwidth Spectral efficiency Shannon plane Shannon limit Coding gain 01 Two resources to share out Every communication system spends two scarce resources: power and bandwidth. Comparing systems means seeing how much of each they need to deliver a given quality, which in analog is the output signal-to-noise ratio and in digital the"},{"t":"Graphical user interfaces","d":"Topic · Computer Science II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interfaces-graficas","x":"Computer Science II · 120 h · Topic 5 of 8 Graphical user interfaces A graphical interface inverts control: the program no longer commands, it waits. That change of mindset —from the sequential program to the event loop— is what you have to understand first. Events Widgets Event loop Callbacks Design 01 The program stops being in charge A console program is in charge: it asks for a value, calculates, prints, ends. A graphical interface reverses that relationship. The program starts, draws the window and waits; the one who decides what happens is the user. Sequential program Event-driven progra"},{"t":"Interference and diffraction","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencia-difraccion","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 15 of 15 Interference and diffraction Diffraction does not allow drawing details much smaller than the wavelength used to draw them. That is why the chip industry moved from 436 nm to 13.5 nm, and each jump cost a fortune. Young Coherence Diffraction Diffraction grating Rayleigh criterion 01 Two slits Young’s experiment is one of the cleanest pieces of evidence that light is a wave. Two slits illuminated by the same source behave like two emitters in phase, and on the screen not two spots appear but a succession of fringes. \\[ d\\sin\\theta = m\\lambda \\quad\\text{maxima"},{"t":"Normal-mode and common-mode interference","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/interferencias-medicion","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 9 of 11 Normal-mode and common-mode interference An amplifier with 120 dB of common-mode rejection is useless if one of the lines has 10 kΩ more than the other: the imbalance converts the common mode into signal before it reaches the input. Common mode Normal mode Ground loops CMRR Integration 01 Two ways of getting into a measurement Interference is any voltage that adds to the one you want to measure. Depending on how it appears at the instrument’s terminals, two modes are distinguished: Normal mode (or differential): the interference is in series wi"},{"t":"Introduction to analog integrated circuit design","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/introduccion-ci-analogicos","x":"Applied Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 8 of 8 Introduction to analog integrated circuit design A 2 pF capacitor sets the bandwidth of an integrated op amp and creates a zero that steals phase from it. A 1/gm resistor in series makes it disappear. Design flow Two-stage op amp Miller compensation Miller zero Bandgap 01 From the specification sheet to silicon Designing an analog integrated circuit means sharing out a budget: with a limited current and area you have to obtain gain, bandwidth, phase margin, output swing, noise and offset. Applied Electronics I introduced the basics, matching, Pelgr"},{"t":"CMOS inverter","d":"Topic · Electronic Devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversor-cmos","x":"Electronic Devices · 120 h · Topic 6 of 9 CMOS inverter Two transistors, one always off: that is the reason a processor with billions of gates can operate without melting. Transfer curve Switching threshold Noise margins Dynamic power Latch-up 01 Two complementary transistors The CMOS inverter is a PMOS between the supply and the output and an NMOS between the output and ground, with both gates tied to the input. With the input at 0, the PMOS conducts and the NMOS is off: the output sits at \\( V_{DD} \\). With the input at \\( V_{DD} \\), the reverse. In either state one of the two transistors is"},{"t":"Inverters","d":"Topic · Power Electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/inversores","x":"Power Electronics · 96 h · Topic 5 of 8 Inverters With the same fundamental, unipolar modulation moves the harmonics to twice the carrier frequency and lowers the distortion from 146 % to 77 %: an inductive load filters them much better. H-bridge Sinusoidal PWM Modulation index Overmodulation Multilevel 01 The bridge inverter An inverter generates AC voltage from a DC source. The single-phase H-bridge has two legs of two switches; with the load connected diagonally, between the midpoints, it can apply \\( +V_{dc} \\), \\( -V_{dc} \\) or zero. The three-phase one has three legs, one per phase. Each"},{"t":"JFET: DC, small signal and switching","d":"Topic · Electronic Devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/jfet","x":"Electronic Devices · 120 h · Topic 4 of 9 JFET: DC, small signal and switching Two 2N5457s from the same bag, in the same circuit, can conduct 0.4 and 2.9 mA. The JFET is the lowest-noise transistor with high-impedance sources, and also the one with the greatest spread. Pinch-off Shockley equation Self-bias Spread Analog switch 01 A channel that pinches off The junction field-effect transistor (JFET) is the most direct idea for controlling a current with a voltage. An N-type silicon channel joins two terminals, the drain and the source, and on its sides there are P⁺ regions connected together"},{"t":"PN junction and diodes","d":"Topic · Electronic Devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/juntura-pn","x":"Electronic Devices · 120 h · Topic 2 of 9 PN junction and diodes A 1000 V diode and a 3 V Zener diode are the same PN junction with different doping. What decides how much voltage it withstands is the less doped side, not the more doped one. Depletion region Shockley equation Junction capacitance Breakdown Reverse recovery 01 The junction at equilibrium When a P region is joined to an N region, holes from the P side diffuse into the N side and electrons from the N side into the P side, following the concentration gradient described in the previous unit. Each carrier that crosses leaves behind"},{"t":"The Laplace transform","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/laplace","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 33 of 34 The Laplace transform Fourier leaves out everything that grows. Laplace adds a convergence factor, and with it the step, unstable systems and initial conditions come in. The price is that you always have to say where it converges. Laplace Region of convergence Unilateral Impedances Final value 01 What for, if we already have Fourier The Fourier transform requires the signal to be absolutely integrable, and that leaves out such common things as the step or a growing exponential. Laplace’s solution is to multiply by a convergence factor \\( e^{-\\sigma"},{"t":"Legislation: the law around engineering","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-introduccion","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 1 of 13 Legislation: the law around engineering Every signature an engineer puts down has legal consequences: a report, a contract, a drawing, an expert opinion. Law is not foreign to the profession; it is the framework in which the profession is practiced. Legal norm Technical standard Sources of law InfoLEG Professional practice 01 Why an engineer studies law Almost everything an engineer does at work has a legal side, even when it does not look like it. Accepting a quotation means entering into a contract. Signing the drawing of an installation means taking on a r"},{"t":"Legislation on works","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/legislacion-obras","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 13 of 13 Legislation on works A telecommunications installation in a public hospital is contracted, carried out and accepted under rules that have existed since 1947. Knowing them is part of the trade. Public works Tendering Direction of works Technical representative AEA regulations 01 Public works Law 13,064, of 1947, defines national public works as any construction, work or industrial service carried out with funds of the National Treasury. It remains in force, with its implementing regulations, and each province has its own law with a similar structure (in the p"},{"t":"Introduction to an advanced language","d":"Topic · Computer Science II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguaje-avanzado","x":"Computer Science II · 120 h · Topic 7 of 8 Introduction to an advanced language After C, a language with objects lets you think in pieces that fit together. It is not a fad: it is how a ten-thousand-line project stays understandable. Objects Classes Encapsulation Exceptions Libraries 01 Why functions are not enough In C a program grows as a list of functions that pass structures to each other. It works until the project reaches a few thousand lines and three problems appear: anyone can touch any field, the same piece of data is validated in twenty places, and adding a case forces you to hunt d"},{"t":"Hardware description languages","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lenguajes-hdl","x":"Digital Techniques I · 96 h · Topic 3 of 4 Hardware description languages In Verilog, changing a “ VHDL Verilog Concurrency Nonblocking assignment Synthesis 01 Describing hardware, not programming it A hardware description language (HDL) looks like a programming language and is not one. A C program is a list of instructions that a processor executes one at a time, in order. A description in VHDL or Verilog is the description of a circuit: each statement is a piece of hardware that exists and works at the same time as all the others. There is no “now this line runs”; there are gates and flip-fl"},{"t":"Limit, continuity and the complex derivative","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-derivada-compleja","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 3 of 34 Limit, continuity and the complex derivative The definition of the derivative is the same as in real calculus, but now the increment can arrive from infinitely many directions and the quotient has to give the same result in all of them. That single requirement is what makes complex variable so different. Limit Continuity Derivative Analytic Difference quotient 01 A limit with infinitely many paths On the real line, approaching a point allows only two paths: from the left and from the right. That is why the two one-sided limits are enough. In the comp"},{"t":"Limits of real functions","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/limite-funciones","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 2 of 10 Limits of real functions The limit asks what value a function approaches near a point, regardless of what happens exactly at that point. From that distinction come the derivative, the integral and steady-state behavior. Epsilon and delta One-sided Indeterminate forms Notable limits Asymptotes 01 What happens near the point, not at the point The limit answers a very precise question: what value does f(x) approach as x approaches a number a, without ever reaching it. What happens exactly at x = a plays no part; the function may not even be defined there. The ex"},{"t":"Transmission lines","d":"Topic · Transmission Media","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lineas-transmision","x":"Transmission Media · 96 h · Topic 4 of 8 Transmission lines A quarter-wave length of cable turns 100 Ω into 25 Ω without a single component. At those frequencies, the length of the cable is part of the circuit. Characteristic impedance Reflection coefficient SWR Quarter wave Reflectometry 01 When a cable stops being a cable In circuit theory, a cable is a node: the voltage is the same at both of its ends at the same instant. That holds as long as the signal takes much less time to travel along it than it takes to change. When the length of the cable approaches an appreciable fraction of the wa"},{"t":"Programmable logic devices","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/logica-programable","x":"Digital Techniques I · 96 h · Topic 4 of 4 Programmable logic devices Inside an FPGA there are no AND or OR gates: there are 16- or 64-bit memories that store truth tables. An AND and the most tangled six-variable function take up the same space. PAL and GAL CPLD FPGA LUT Bitstream 01 From fixed circuit to programmable With discrete 74-series gates, every design change means a new circuit board. A programmable logic device (PLD) reverses the order: the manufacturer sells an integrated circuit with many generic, unconnected logic resources, and the designer decides afterward, with a configurati"},{"t":"The root locus","d":"Topic · Control Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugar-raices","x":"Control Systems · 96 h · Topic 6 of 8 The root locus Three poles with no zeros leave two branches heading into the right half-plane at 60°. A well-placed zero turns them vertical, and the loop is stable for any gain. Angle condition Asymptotes Breakaway point Critical gain Lead network 01 The loop poles as a function of the gain The Routh criterion tells whether the loop is stable for one gain. The root locus, proposed by Walter Evans in 1948, shows how all the closed-loop poles move as the gain \\( K \\) goes from zero to infinity. They are the points of the s-plane that satisfy the characteris"},{"t":"Impedance and admittance loci","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/lugares-geometricos","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 5 of 13 Impedance and admittance loci An infinite straight line in the impedance plane becomes a finite circular arc in the admittance plane. With that single property, the behavior of a motor was predicted for decades with compass and straightedge, and the Smith chart comes out of it. Locus Complex inversion Admittance Circle diagram Smith chart 01 What a locus is The impedance of a circuit is a complex number: a point on the plane. If one of its parameters changes —the frequency, an adjustable resistor, the load on a motor—, that point moves and draws a curve"},{"t":"Macroeconomics","d":"Topic · Economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/macroeconomia","x":"Economics · 72 h · Topic 3 of 4 Macroeconomics With 3 % monthly inflation, in a year prices do not rise 36 % but 42.6 %. A fixed-term deposit at 40 % nominal annual interest earns barely 3.9 % in real terms, and one at 36 % earns nothing. GDP National accounts Price index Inflation Real rate 01 Variables and indicators Macroeconomics looks at the economy as a whole. Its main variables, and the indicators used to measure them, are: Variable Indicator Output Gross domestic product (GDP), nominal and real; its growth rate Prices Consumer price index (CPI), GDP deflator, inflation rate Employment"},{"t":"Magnetostatics","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/magnetostatica","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 8 of 15 Magnetostatics There are no magnetic charges, so the magnetic field is not defined by the force on a charge at rest —that one is zero— but on a moving one. Everything comes from there, from the motor to the Hall sensor. Lorentz force Biot–Savart Ampère's law Solenoid Hall effect 01 The magnetic field is defined by what it does There are no magnetic charges: there is no magnetic equivalent of an electron. That is why the magnetic field is not defined by the force on a charge at rest —that one is zero— but by the force on a charge in motion, and that force is p"},{"t":"Analytic functions and conformal mapping","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mapeo-conforme","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 5 of 34 Analytic functions and conformal mapping Near a point, every analytic function reduces to multiplication by a complex number: scale and rotate. That, and nothing else, is what makes angles preserved and what lets the Smith chart be drawn with a compass. Conformal Critical points Smith chart Nyquist Schwarz-Christoffel 01 Analytic, and what comes with it To recap: \\( f \\) is analytic on a domain if it is differentiable in the complex sense at all of its points, which by Cauchy-Riemann is equivalent to \\( u \\) and \\( v \\) being tied to each other. That"},{"t":"Transformers and AC machines","d":"Topic · Electrical Machines and Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-ca","x":"Electrical Machines and Installations · 96 h · Topic 1 of 5 Transformers and AC machines A four-pole induction motor never reaches 1500 rpm: at rated load it turns at 1448. That 3.5% difference, the slip, is what produces the torque. Transformer Rotating field Slip Torque-speed curve V/f drive 01 The real transformer The ideal transformer, seen in coupled circuits, relates voltages by the turns ratio and conserves power. The real one has losses and flux that does not link the two windings, and it is modeled with an equivalent circuit: the resistances of the windings and their leakage reactance"},{"t":"DC machines and brushless motors","d":"Topic · Electrical Machines and Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-cc","x":"Electrical Machines and Installations · 96 h · Topic 2 of 5 DC machines and brushless motors A DC motor is most efficient when only lightly loaded: the maximum efficiency of a small motor is at one tenth of its stall current, far from the maximum torque. Back EMF Excitation H-bridge Maximum efficiency BLDC 01 The easiest motor to control In a DC motor, the rotor conductors carry current inside the stator field and experience a force. A segmented commutator with brushes reverses the current in each coil just as it passes from one pole to the other, so that the torque always pushes in the same d"},{"t":"Sequential logic: state machines and timing","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maquinas-estado","x":"Digital Techniques I · 96 h · Topic 2 of 4 Sequential logic: state machines and timing A device that hangs once a day for no apparent reason may have a flip-flop reading an unsynchronized pushbutton. Adding a second flip-flop pushes that failure out to once every tens of thousands of years. Moore and Mealy Synchronous design Critical path Metastability Synchronizer 01 Synchronous design Flip-flops, counters and shift registers are covered in the page on sequential logic in the Electronics Technician program. This unit goes up one level: how any sequential system is designed and what limits it."},{"t":"Environmental legal framework","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-ambiental","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 10 of 11 Environmental legal framework The Nation sets a floor of environmental protection and the provinces may demand more, never less. A workshop that etches boards is already covered by the hazardous waste law. Article 41 Minimum standards Law 25,675 Hazardous waste International agreements 01 The environment in the Constitution The 1994 reform incorporated Article 41: all inhabitants have the right to a healthy, balanced environment fit for human development, and the duty to preserve it; environmental damage generates, as a priority, the oblig"},{"t":"Legal framework of occupational health and safety","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/marco-legal-hys","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 4 of 11 Legal framework of occupational health and safety A single workshop answers to a law of 1972, a decree of 1979, resolutions that change every few years and the inspection of its province. Finding your way means knowing at which level to look. Law 19,587 Decree 351/79 Law 24,557 SRT resolutions ILO Conventions 01 The rules in order The protection of health at work in Argentina has several levels. At the top is the Constitution: Article 14 bis guarantees dignified and equitable working conditions, and Article 75, paragraph 22, gives constitut"},{"t":"Electrical materials","d":"Topic · Electronic Technology","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/materiales-electricos-ing","x":"Electronic Technology · 120 h · Topic 2 of 9 Electrical materials A 1 mm copper wire has the same resistance at 50 Hz as at DC, but at 1 MHz it has four times more: the current crowds into a 66 μm layer next to the surface. Resistivity Temperature coefficient Dielectrics Thermal classes Skin effect 01 Conducting materials A conductor is chosen for its resistivity \\( \\rho \\), its temperature coefficient \\( \\alpha \\), its mechanical strength, its ease of soldering and its cost. The physics of conduction was covered in electrical properties of matter; here what matters is the material as an input"},{"t":"Magnetic materials","d":"Topic · Electronic Technology","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/materiales-magneticos","x":"Electronic Technology · 120 h · Topic 3 of 9 Magnetic materials Without an air gap, a 30-turn ferrite inductor has 2.8 mH but saturates at 0.31 A. With half a millimeter of air gap it drops to 0.2 mH and withstands 4.3 A: the energy is stored in the air. Hysteresis Saturation Ferrites Core losses Air gap 01 Magnetization and hysteresis A ferromagnetic material responds to the field \\( H \\) with a flux density \\( B \\) much greater than that of vacuum, but not linearly: the magnetization curve rises with a high slope, the permeability, and flattens out at saturation, where the material can no lo"},{"t":"Matrices","d":"Topic · Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/matrices","x":"Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry · 120 h · Topic 1 of 9 Matrices A matrix looks like a table of numbers, but it is the most compact way to write down an entire circuit, a rotation or the chaining of two amplifier stages. Matrix multiplication Inverse Rank Admittance matrix Two-port networks 01 A table that also does arithmetic A matrix is a rectangular array of numbers arranged in rows and columns. So far it is just a table. What turns it into a tool is that operations are defined on that array —addition, multiplication, inverse— and they stand for concrete things: solving a circuit, rotat"},{"t":"Maxwell's equations","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/maxwell","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 11 of 15 Maxwell's equations Maxwell combined two constants measured in electrostatics and magnetism experiments, with no connection to optics, and it gave him exactly the speed of light. That coincidence was the proof that light is an electromagnetic wave. The four equations Displacement current c Refractive index Transmission line 01 The four equations Everything that appeared in the previous units —Gauss, the absence of monopoles, Faraday and Ampère— fits into four equations. Maxwell did not discover all of them: he gathered them, and in doing so he noticed that o"},{"t":"Quantum mechanics: step, barrier and well","d":"Topic · Electronic Physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mecanica-cuantica","x":"Electronic Physics · 120 h · Topic 6 of 9 Quantum mechanics: step, barrier and well An electron can appear on the other side of a wall that, according to all of classical physics, it should not be able to cross. That result is not a laboratory curiosity: it is the reason a chip cannot be thinned beyond a certain limit. Schrödinger Tunneling Infinite well Harmonic oscillator MOSFET 01 The equation that replaces Newton If a particle is also a wave, there must be an equation that governs that wave, just as Newton's second law governs a trajectory. That equation was written by Schrödinger in 1926,"},{"t":"Classical and quantum statistical mechanics","d":"Topic · Electronic Physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mecanica-estadistica","x":"Electronic Physics · 120 h · Topic 8 of 9 Classical and quantum statistical mechanics The classical theory of electrons in a metal was off by almost two orders of magnitude, and it took twenty-seven years to learn why: Pauli forbids almost all of them from moving. The Fermi level that comes out of that is the central idea of the unit that closes this subject. Fermi-Dirac Fermi level Bose-Einstein Maxwell-Boltzmann Thermionic emission 01 Why a statistical physics is needed A cubic centimeter of copper has on the order of \\( 10^{23} \\) free electrons. Neither Newtonian mechanics nor the Schrödin"},{"t":"Amplifier measurements","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mediciones-amplificadores","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 5 of 10 Amplifier measurements An amplifier with a 1.5 dB noise figure and 10 dB of gain, measured with an ordinary analyzer, reads as if it had 10.5 dB. Without correcting for the second instrument, the measurement says nothing about the amplifier. 1 dB compression Intermodulation IP3 Y factor Noise figure 01 What is measured in an amplifier The specifications of an amplifier fall into four families, and each one has its instrument and its method: Family Quantities Instruments Small signal Gain, frequency response, input and output impedances, return"},{"t":"Voltage, current and power at low frequency","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mediciones-baja-frecuencia","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 2 of 11 Voltage, current and power at low frequency An analog voltmeter of 20 kΩ/V measuring a 100 kΩ divider reads 4 V where there are 5. It is not out of calibration: connecting it changed the circuit. Loading effect Burden voltage Wattmeter Aron method Clamp meter 01 Every instrument modifies what it measures To measure a voltage, the voltmeter is connected in parallel and draws some current; to measure a current, the ammeter is inserted in series and adds resistance. In both cases the circuit changes when the instrument is connected, and what is me"},{"t":"CMOS memories","d":"Topic · Electronic Devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-cmos","x":"Electronic Devices · 120 h · Topic 7 of 9 CMOS memories One bit of main memory is about 150,000 electrons on a leaky capacitor. If no one replenishes them, in less than a second the 1 is read as a 0. SRAM DRAM Flash Refresh Charge sharing 01 Three ways to store a bit All the memory of a present-day computer is made with MOS transistors, but in three very different ways, each with a different compromise between speed, density and permanence: SRAM DRAM Flash How it stores the bit Two cross-coupled inverters Charge on a capacitor Charge trapped in the gate of a transistor Transistors per bit 6 1"},{"t":"Electronic memories","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas","x":"Digital Techniques II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 6 Electronic memories A server memory module is 72 bits wide to store 64: the remaining eight correct any bit that a cosmic ray flips. SRAM and DRAM NOR and NAND flash Memory map Wear Hamming code 01 What distinguishes one memory from another A memory stores bits in cells, and each technology stores the bit in a different physical way: the state of a flip-flop, the charge of a capacitor, the charge trapped on an insulated gate, or the polarization of a material. All of its properties follow from that physical form: whether it loses the data when power"},{"t":"Mixers","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mezcladores","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 3 of 10 Mixers An FM radio tuned to 100 MHz also hears 121.4 MHz and 105.35 MHz if its input filter does not reject them. The mixer cannot tell which side the signal comes from. Intermediate frequency Image frequency Balanced mixer Gilbert cell Spurious responses 01 Translating a signal in frequency A mixer multiplies the input signal by that of a local oscillator. By the product-of-cosines identity, the sum and the difference of the frequencies appear at the output: \\[ \\cos\\omega_{RF}t \\cdot \\cos\\omega_{LO}t = \\tfrac12\\cos(\\omega_{RF} - \\omega_{LO})t \\]"},{"t":"Microeconomics","d":"Topic · Economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/microeconomia","x":"Economics · 72 h · Topic 2 of 4 Microeconomics A tax of ARS 90 per unit is paid two-thirds by buyers when demand is inelastic, and one-third when it is very elastic. Who bears a tax is not decided by the law, but by the elasticities. Production function Supply and demand Elasticity Equilibrium Types of market 01 The production function The production function relates the maximum quantity that can be produced to the quantities of factors employed. In the short run some factor is fixed —the plant, the machines— and adding labor increases output less and less: this is the law of diminishing retur"},{"t":"Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems","d":"Topic · Control Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelado-sistemas","x":"Control Systems · 96 h · Topic 2 of 8 Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems Neglecting the inductance of a small motor is almost always reasonable. With 10 mH and low inertia, the simplified model fails to see that the speed overshoots by 70 % before settling. Analogies DC motor State space Transfer function Runge-Kutta 01 From the physical system to the equations To design a controller you need a model of the plant: equations that describe how it responds to its inputs. It is obtained by applying the physical laws of each domain, Kirchhoff's laws in circuits, Newton's laws in mechanics,"},{"t":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","d":"Topic · Electronic Physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos","x":"Electronic Physics · 120 h · Topic 7 of 9 Atomic models and Schrödinger Classical electromagnetism predicts that no atom can last more than sixteen picoseconds. Resolving that contradiction led from Bohr to Schrödinger to Pauli, and ended up explaining why a crystal has energy bands instead of isolated levels. Bohr Schrödinger Quantum numbers Pauli Energy bands 01 The atom that classical physics could not sustain Rutherford's scattering experiment (1911) —alpha particles against a gold foil, with some bouncing straight back— brought down Thomson's “plum pudding” model and left an atom with alm"},{"t":"Lumped elements and time invariance","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 1 of 13 Lumped elements and time invariance Every circuit is, at bottom, an electromagnetic wave problem. The lumped-element model is the approximation that lets you ignore that, and knowing when it stops holding is as important as knowing how to use it. Lumped elements λ/10 rule Time invariance Self-resonance Ideal model 01 Every circuit is, at bottom, a wave problem Maxwell’s equations make no distinction between a wire and an antenna: any conductor carrying a varying current radiates and propagates fields at the speed of light. Solving a circuit that way —fi"},{"t":"Digital modulation","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-digital-ing","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 8 of 11 Digital modulation With minimum-bandwidth pulses, a 10 % error in the sampling instant closes the eye by 40 %. With twice the bandwidth, the same error closes it by less than 10 %. Symbols and bauds Nyquist criterion Raised cosine Eye diagram QAM and PSK 01 Bits, symbols and pulses A digital system groups bits \\( k \\) at a time and transmits one of \\( M = 2^k \\) possible symbols. Each symbol is a pulse of known shape whose amplitude, phase or frequency depends on the bits. Two rates must be distinguished: \\[ R_s = \\frac{R_b}{\\log_2 M} \\] \\( R_b \\) i"},{"t":"Exponential modulation: FM and PM","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-exponencial","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 5 of 11 Exponential modulation: FM and PM A commercial FM station occupies about 180 kHz to transmit 15 kHz audio. That bandwidth, twelve times larger, is the price of its quality. Modulation index Bessel functions Carson's rule Narrowband PLL 01 Modulating the angle In exponential or angle modulations, the message changes the phase or the frequency of the carrier and the amplitude stays constant. In phase modulation (PM), the phase follows the message; in frequency modulation (FM), the instantaneous frequency does: \\[ x_{PM}(t) = A_c \\cos\\big(2\\pi f_c t +"},{"t":"Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-lineal","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 4 of 11 Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB An AM station modulated at 100 % spends two thirds of its power on the carrier, which carries no information at all. In exchange, it can be received with a diode. Modulation index Efficiency Overmodulation DSB-SC SSB 01 Shifting the spectrum In linear modulations, the complex envelope is a linear function of the message. Its effect in frequency is simple: the spectrum of the message is shifted around the carrier, without changing shape. The variants differ in whether they transmit the carrier and in how many sideba"},{"t":"Pulse modulation and PCM","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-pulsos","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 6 of 11 Pulse modulation and PCM With 8 bits and μ-law, a voice 40 dB below the maximum sounds as good as with a 12-bit uniform converter. Digital telephony saves four bits per sample with a logarithmic curve. PAM, PWM and PPM PCM Quantization noise μ-law and A-law TDM and E1 01 Modulating a pulse train If the message is sampled at a frequency greater than twice its bandwidth, it is enough to transmit the samples. The carrier becomes a pulse train, and the message can change its amplitude, its duration or its position: Modulation What the sample carries Use"},{"t":"Modulators and demodulators","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/moduladores-demoduladores","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 7 of 10 Modulators and demodulators Half a decibel of difference between the two branches of an I/Q modulator and five degrees of quadrature error leave the image sideband barely 26 dB down. That is why transmitters are calibrated. I/Q modulator I/Q demodulator Costas loop Image rejection Carrier leakage 01 The I/Q modulator Any modulation can be written as a complex envelope \\( I(t) + jQ(t) \\), and every modern modulator generates it in the same way: two mixers fed with the local oscillator in phase and in quadrature, and a summer: \\[ s(t) = I(t)\\cos\\om"},{"t":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","d":"Topic · Electronic Devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado","x":"Electronic Devices · 120 h · Topic 5 of 9 MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling With a 30 nm channel, the textbook MOS formula predicts five times more current than the transistor delivers. And the supply voltage of processors does not go below 0.7 V because of a thermodynamic limit: 60 mV per decade. Threshold voltage Short channel Velocity saturation Dennard scaling FinFET 01 The MOS capacitor and inversion The MOS transistor is a capacitor with a semiconductor as one of its plates: a conducting gate, a very thin oxide and a P-type silicon substrate. A positive voltage on t"},{"t":"Stepper motors","d":"Topic · Electrical Machines and Installations","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/motores-paso","x":"Electrical Machines and Installations · 96 h · Topic 3 of 5 Stepper motors At 300 rpm, a stepper motor driven at its rated voltage keeps 20% of its torque. With a chopper driver at 24 V it keeps more than 80%: the motor is the same; what changes is how the current rises. Full step Microstepping L/R constant Chopper Step loss 01 Positioning without measuring A stepper motor turns in fixed increments, typically 1.8° (200 steps per revolution), each time the controller changes the current in its windings. If the load does not exceed its torque, the position is the number of steps sent: it is posi"},{"t":"Wave motion","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-ondulatorio","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 12 of 15 Wave motion A signal traveling along a cable is a wave, with its speed, its wavelength and its reflections, and it obeys the same equation as a shaken string. All of transmission-line theory comes from there. Wave equation Superposition Beats Standing wave SWR 01 What travels in a wave A wave carries energy and momentum, not matter. The particles of the medium oscillate around their position and stay where they were; what moves forward is the disturbance. This holds for sound, for a string and also for the signals in a cable. \\[ \\frac{\\partial^2 y}{\\partial"},{"t":"Oscillatory motion","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 6 of 10 Oscillatory motion The oscillating spring and the RLC circuit obey the same differential equation. Whoever understands one understands the other, and mechanical resonance is literally the same as that of a radio tuner. Simple harmonic Pendulum Damping Resonance Q factor 01 Simple harmonic motion Whenever a restoring force is proportional to the displacement and points in the opposite direction, the resulting motion is simple harmonic. Hooke’s law, F = −k·x, combined with the second law gives: \\[ m \\frac{d^2 x}{d t^2} + k x = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad x (t) = A \\c"},{"t":"The sampling theorem","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/muestreo-shannon","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 30 of 34 The sampling theorem Keeping the value every T seconds and throwing away the rest seems to lose information for good. And yet, if the signal is bandlimited and sampled fast enough, the samples contain everything. Nyquist Replicas Sinc interpolation Hold Antialiasing filter 01 The statement, and why it is not obvious Sampling means keeping the value of a signal every \\( T \\) seconds and throwing away everything that happened in between. It seems that information is lost forever, and yet: \\[ f_s \\gt 2\\,f_{\\text{max}} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; x(t) \\text{ ca"},{"t":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","d":"Topic · Electronic Devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas","x":"Electronic Devices · 120 h · Topic 8 of 9 Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs A microsecond pulse on the gate and the thyristor keeps conducting by itself until the next zero crossing. It is the same mechanism that, when no one wants it, burns out a CMOS circuit in latch-up. Thyristor TRIAC Phase control IGBT Latching 01 Four layers, three junctions A thyristor (or SCR, silicon-controlled rectifier) is a PNPN structure: a P anode, a wide, lightly doped N layer that withstands the voltage, a P layer that carries the gate terminal, and an N cathode. It has three junctions in series, and"},{"t":"Standards for representing circuits","d":"Topic · Computer-Aided Design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos","x":"Computer-Aided Design · 72 h · Topic 3 of 5 Standards for representing circuits A schematic is not a drawing of the circuit: it is a map of its connections. Symbols are standardized precisely so that the topology can be read at a glance. Symbols Designators Single-line Multi-line Schematics 01 A schematic is not a drawing of the circuit A photo of a board shows where each component is; the schematic shows how they are connected. They are two different documents and both are needed, but to understand what a circuit does the second one is what helps. Document What it shows What it is used for Fu"},{"t":"National and international standards","d":"Topic · Computer-Aided Design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo","x":"Computer-Aided Design · 72 h · Topic 2 of 5 National and international standards Standards keep everyone from drawing however they like. A standardized drawing is understood the same way in Rosario as in Shenzhen, and that is exactly what manufacturing requires. IRAM ISO Formats Scales Title block 01 What standardization is for A standard is a written agreement on how things are done. In technical drawing that means that the drawing that leaves an office in Rosario is manufactured in a workshop in Shenzhen without phone calls, because both read the same thing. Body Scope Examples IRAM Argentin"},{"t":"The subject matter of economics","d":"Topic · Economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/objeto-economia","x":"Economics · 72 h · Topic 1 of 4 The subject matter of economics Going from 60 to 80 thousand electronic units costs 40 thousand tonnes of food, and the cost of each additional unit rises from 1.5 to 2.7 tonnes: the resources that are moved are less and less suited to the new task. Scarcity Opportunity cost Factors of production Circular flow Production possibilities frontier 01 Scarcity and choice Economics studies how societies use scarce resources to satisfy needs that have no limit. Since there is not enough for everything, we have to choose, and every choice has an opportunity cost: the va"},{"t":"Wave, propagation and interference","d":"Topic · Electronic Physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/onda-interferencia","x":"Electronic Physics · 120 h · Topic 1 of 9 Wave, propagation and interference Michelson built the most sensitive instrument of his time to measure the motion of the Earth through the ether, and found nothing. That null result is the starting point of all twentieth-century physics. Interference Coherence Michelson Ether Null result 01 No coherence, no interference Two waves always superpose, but for the interference pattern to be visible the phase relationship between them has to hold steady during the observation time. That property is coherence, and it is what distinguishes a laser from a lamp"},{"t":"Electromagnetic waves","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-electromagneticas","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 13 of 15 Electromagnetic waves A charge at rest does not radiate, and neither does one moving at constant velocity. An accelerated charge does, and an alternating current in a conductor is exactly that: all of antenna engineering comes from there. Poynting Near field Friis Link budget Photon 01 What an electromagnetic wave looks like From Maxwell’s equations in vacuum comes a wave with a very rigid structure: the two fields are perpendicular to each other and both perpendicular to the direction of propagation. It is transverse, needs no medium and its two fields osci"},{"t":"Mechanical waves","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 7 of 10 Mechanical waves A wave carries energy without carrying matter. A string with one fixed end and a short-circuited transmission line produce exactly the same standing wave. Wave equation Standing waves Interference Sound Doppler 01 What a wave is A wave carries energy and momentum without carrying matter. Each particle of the medium oscillates about its equilibrium position, and what travels is the disturbance. Transverse: the particles oscillate perpendicular to the direction of propagation. A string, an electromagnetic wave. Longitudinal: they oscillate in th"},{"t":"Wave equations and plane waves","d":"Topic · Transmission Media","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas","x":"Transmission Media · 96 h · Topic 2 of 8 Wave equations and plane waves Seawater reflects almost all of a long-wave radio signal and swallows within a few meters whatever gets in. That is why a submerged submarine can only be reached on very low frequencies. Propagation constant Intrinsic impedance Attenuation Reflection Standing wave 01 From Maxwell to the wave equation Combining the two curl equations of the previous unit in a source-free region, the electric field is governed by a single equation, the Helmholtz equation: \\[ \\nabla^2 \\mathbf{E} - \\gamma^2 \\mathbf{E} = 0 \\] \\[ \\gamma = \\alpha"},{"t":"Geometrical optics","d":"Topic · Physics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica","x":"Physics I · 120 h · Topic 10 of 10 Geometrical optics When the wavelength is much smaller than the objects, light can be treated as rays that travel in straight lines. On that approximation the lens, the image sensor and the optical fiber are built. Reflection Snell's law Total reflection Lenses Optical fiber 01 When light can be treated as rays Light is an electromagnetic wave, but when the objects it meets are much larger than its wavelength —half a micrometer for visible light— it behaves as if it traveled in straight-line rays. That approximation is geometrical optics, and it is used to de"},{"t":"Photonics and optoelectronics","d":"Topic · Electronic Devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica","x":"Electronic Devices · 120 h · Topic 9 of 9 Photonics and optoelectronics Silicon detects light very well and hardly emits it: its band gap is indirect. That is why solar cells are made of silicon and LEDs of gallium. Photodiode Solar cell LED Laser Optocoupler 01 Photons and bands A photon with energy greater than the band gap can be absorbed and create an electron-hole pair; a pair that recombines can emit a photon with the energy of the band gap. All of optoelectronics comes from those two sentences and from the relationship between energy and wavelength: \\[ E = \\frac{h\\,c}{\\lambda} \\qquad \\l"},{"t":"RF oscillators","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciladores-rf","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 1 of 10 RF oscillators A 10 MHz crystal specified for an 18 pF load oscillates 41 ppm higher if the circuit gives it 16 pF. That is why the crystal's datasheet and the board's have to agree. Oscillation condition Colpitts Phase noise Quartz crystal Load capacitance 01 The oscillation condition at radio frequency An oscillator is a feedback amplifier that deliberately satisfies the Barkhausen criterion: loop gain of magnitude 1 and zero phase at a single frequency. At low frequency the network that selects the frequency is RC, like the Wien bridge in stab"},{"t":"The analog oscilloscope","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 6 of 11 The analog oscilloscope A badly compensated 10:1 probe makes the calibrator’s square wave start at 3.7 V instead of 5 V. The probe trimmer is not a detail: it decides whether the measured amplitude is correct. Cathode-ray tube Bandwidth Rise time Trigger Compensated probe 01 Seeing the shape of a signal The oscilloscope plots voltage as a function of time. No other instrument shows at a glance the shape, the peaks, the oscillations, the rise times and the transients. Today almost all oscilloscopes are digital, but their vertical channel, their"},{"t":"The advanced digital oscilloscope","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-avanzado","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 2 of 10 The advanced digital oscilloscope A 5 GS/s oscilloscope with 10,000 points of memory samples at 1 MS/s when it is set to 1 ms per division. The memory, not the converter, decides what you see. Memory depth Sampling rate Advanced trigger Segmented memory High resolution 01 Three numbers that define a digital oscilloscope The digital oscilloscope of Measurements I was described by its bandwidth and its sampling rate. An advanced instrument is judged by three numbers that interact: the analog front-end bandwidth, the maximum sampling rate of the"},{"t":"The digital oscilloscope","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 7 of 11 The digital oscilloscope A 1 GS/s oscilloscope with 1000 points of memory, at 10 µs/div, samples at only 10 MS/s. A 10.1 MHz signal then appears as a clean 100 kHz sine wave that does not exist. Sampling rate Memory Aliasing Peak detect Vertical resolution 01 From the beam to the memory A digital storage oscilloscope (DSO) keeps the vertical channel of the analog scope —attenuator, amplifier, bandwidth— but instead of deflecting a beam, it digitizes the signal with a fast ADC and stores the samples in an acquisition memory. The trigger decides"},{"t":"Other devices used in electronics","d":"Topic · Electronic Technology","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/otros-dispositivos","x":"Electronic Technology · 120 h · Topic 5 of 9 Other devices used in electronics A 10 kΩ NTC thermistor in a divider with another 10 kΩ resistor departs almost 8 °C from a straight line between 0 and 70 °C. With 5.1 kΩ the departure drops to 2.2 °C: the fixed resistor is also part of the sensor. NTC thermistor β equation Varistor TVS suppressor Relay 01 Thermistors A thermistor is a semiconductor resistor, made of sintered metal oxides, whose resistance changes strongly with temperature. NTCs lower their resistance when heated, by about 4 % per degree at room temperature, ten times more than a m"},{"t":"Measuring device parameters","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parametros-dispositivos","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 10 Measuring device parameters A 10 μF electrolytic capacitor measured at 1 MHz is no longer a capacitor: it is above its self-resonance and the LCR meter sees it as an inductance. LCR meter Series and parallel model Dissipation factor Self-resonance Curve tracer 01 The LCR meter The bridges of Electronic Measurements I are balanced by hand. A modern LCR meter does the same automatically with a self-balancing bridge: it applies a sinusoidal voltage of known frequency to the component, takes the other terminal to a virtual ground with an amplifier"},{"t":"Even and odd signals","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pares-impares","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 15 of 34 Even and odd signals Every signal splits into an even part and an odd part, in exactly one way. That split decides which harmonics it can have and explains why a push-pull stage cancels the even ones. Even and odd Decomposition Hermitian symmetry Harmonics Push-pull 01 Two symmetries A signal is even if mirroring it in time does not change it, and odd if mirroring it is equivalent to changing its sign: \\[ \\text{even: } x(-t) = x(t) \\qquad\\qquad \\text{odd: } x(-t) = -x(t) \\] In discrete time it is the same, with \\( n \\) in place of \\( t \\). The cosin"},{"t":"Parseval’s relation","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parseval","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 26 of 34 Parseval’s relation The energy of a signal is the same whether computed in time or in frequency. From that equality come harmonic distortion, noise calculation and the idea that makes JPEG and MP3 possible. Parseval Power THD Noise Compression 01 Energy does not depend on where you look at it A signal has a certain energy. That energy can be computed by summing its square over time, or by summing the square of its spectrum over frequency, and the result is the same. That is Parseval’s relation: \\[ \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} |x(t)|^2\\,dt = \\frac{1}{2\\pi"},{"t":"General-purpose passive components","d":"Topic · Electronic Technology","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general","x":"Electronic Technology · 120 h · Topic 4 of 9 General-purpose passive components A divider of two 1 % resistors can be off by up to 50 mV on 5 V, but in 4000 simulated dividers the standard deviation is 20 mV. The worst case is safe; the statistics are realistic. E series Tolerance Temperature coefficient Ceramic dielectrics Electrolytic life 01 Resistors The ideal model of passive components was covered in passive components, in Circuit Theory I; here what matters is real components and how they are chosen. In a resistor what matters is the value, the tolerance, the temperature coefficient (TC"},{"t":"Planning and inventories","d":"Topic · Industrial Organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios","x":"Industrial Organization · 48 h · Topic 4 of 7 Planning and inventories Ordering lots of 400 or 1500 units instead of the optimal 775 costs the same: 23 % more per year. Near the optimum, the total cost curve is flat, and a 20 % error in the lot size adds only 2.5 %. Master schedule MRP ABC classification Economic order quantity Safety stock 01 The planning hierarchy Planning production means deciding what, how much and when to manufacture with the available resources. It is done in levels, from the general to the particular: Level Horizon What it decides Aggregate plan Months to a year Total o"},{"t":"The complex plane and its regions","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 1 of 34 The complex plane and its regions Complex numbers are not a trick: they are the natural tool for everything that oscillates. A phasor, an impedance and a pole are points of this plane, and the regions drawn in it tell you whether a circuit is stable. Complex plane Polar form Euler Regions Phasors 01 Why an electronics engineer needs complex numbers The word “imaginary” is a historical misfortune: it suggests that they do not exist. In electronics, the complex plane is the place where the most concrete things of the whole degree live. Object in the co"},{"t":"Polarization","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 14 of 15 Polarization Two crossed polarizers let nothing through. Put a third one between them and 12.5% gets through, and adding a filter increases what passes because a polarizer does not only filter: it reorients. Malus's law Circular polarization Brewster Birefringence LCD 01 The direction in which the field oscillates The fact that the electromagnetic wave is transverse leaves a freedom that longitudinal waves do not have: the electric field can oscillate in any direction in the plane perpendicular to the propagation. That direction is the polarization, and soun"},{"t":"Policies for national and regional development","d":"Topic · Engineering and Society","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo","x":"Engineering and Society · 48 h · Topic 4 of 5 Policies for national and regional development Whether a country designs or merely assembles is not a natural consequence of anything: it is the result of policy decisions that can be studied. Sábato’s triangle Innovation Local industry Funding The Argentine case 01 Why there is technology policy No country buys development off a supermarket shelf. What can be bought are products; the capacity to design, maintain and improve them is built, and that building takes decades and decisions. Technology policy is the set of public decisions about what get"},{"t":"Poles and zeros","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 9 of 34 Poles and zeros A constant and a handful of points of the plane contain the whole circuit: the frequency response, the step response and the stability all come from reading where those points are. Poles Zeros Stability Q Bode 01 The two numbers that define a circuit A lumped-parameter transfer function is always a quotient of polynomials, and every polynomial can be factored. So: \\[ H(s) = K\\,\\frac{(s - z_1)(s - z_2)\\cdots(s - z_m)}{(s - p_1)(s - p_2)\\cdots(s - p_n)} \\] The \\( z_i \\) are the zeros: there \\( H \\) is zero. The \\( p_k \\) are the poles:"},{"t":"Power in transmission lines","d":"Topic · Transmission Media","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas","x":"Transmission Media · 96 h · Topic 5 of 8 Power in transmission lines An antenna with SWR 3 at the end of 20 m of RG-58 looks like SWR 1.5 from the transmitter. The equipment is satisfied, but less than a third of the power reaches the antenna. Incident and reflected power Attenuation Mismatch loss SWR at the transmitter Power rating 01 The power that travels along the line In a transmission line the power travels in two waves: the incident one, which goes from the generator to the load, and the reflected one, which comes back. Since the characteristic impedance is real in a low-loss line, each"},{"t":"RF power measurement","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 8 of 10 RF power measurement A source with an SWR of 1.5 and a sensor with an SWR of 1.2 leave an uncertainty of ±0.16 dB that no meter can correct: more than the error of many power meters. Diode sensor Thermocouple Square-law region Calibration factor Mismatch 01 Why power is measured and not voltage At radio frequency the voltage changes along a line with standing waves, and it depends on where it is measured. The power traveling toward the load, on the other hand, is well defined. That is why RF instruments measure power, in watts or in dBm, with"},{"t":"Techniques for preventing accidents","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 2 of 11 Techniques for preventing accidents For every serious injury there are hundreds of incidents that hurt no one. They are the cheapest information for prevention, and the one that is almost never recorded. Bird’s pyramid Swiss cheese Causal tree Frequency rate Severity rate 01 Accidents have causes Prevention starts from a simple idea: an accident is not bad luck but the result of causes that can be identified and eliminated. Three models shaped the way we think about it: Model Central idea Consequence for prevention Heinrich’s domino (1931)"},{"t":"First and Second Laws","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 15 First and Second Laws Erasing a bit has a minimum energy cost that does not depend on the technology: it is kT·ln2 joules, and it comes from the Second Law. Thermodynamics puts a floor under the power consumption of a computer. First Law Entropy Carnot Peltier Landauer 01 The First Law: energy is conserved The internal energy of a system changes in two ways, and only two: the heat that enters and the work that is done. Written like that it seems little, and it is the most general conservation principle there is. \\[ \\Delta U = Q - W \\] With the usual conventio"},{"t":"Probability","d":"Topic · Probability and Statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/probabilidad","x":"Probability and Statistics · 72 h · Topic 2 of 6 Probability A test that is right 99 % of the time, applied to a batch with 1 % defective parts, gives one good part for every bad part among those it flags as defective. It is not a flaw in the test: it is Bayes. Sample space Conditional Independence Bayes False positives 01 Three axioms and nothing more The whole theory is built on three rules. A random experiment has a set of possible outcomes —the sample space \\( S \\)—, and every subset of that space, called an event, is assigned a number between 0 and 1: \\[ 0 \\le P(A) \\le 1 \\qquad P(S) = 1 \\"},{"t":"Environmental problems and their evolution","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/problemas-ambientales","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 8 of 11 Environmental problems and their evolution The electronics industry cleaned its boards with the same compounds that punched a hole in the ozone layer. The Montreal Protocol phased them out, and it is considered the most successful environmental agreement. Scales Ozone layer Radiative forcing Climate sensitivity Planetary boundaries 01 From the smokestack to the planet Environmental problems have changed scale as human activity has grown. The first ones were local and visible; today’s are global, slow, and unevenly distributed between those"},{"t":"The profession in Argentina and professional ethics","d":"Topic · Engineering and Society","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/profesion-etica","x":"Engineering and Society · 48 h · Topic 5 of 5 The profession in Argentina and professional ethics Signing a drawing or a report commits the personal responsibility of the person who signs. Everything else —the license, the scope of practice, the code of ethics— is understood starting from there. Scope of practice Professional license Liability Code of ethics Gender 01 What the degree authorizes, and what it does not A university degree certifies an education. What entitles you to practice is the professional license (matrícula), which is granted and overseen by the professional councils. In el"},{"t":"Advanced programming in C","d":"Topic · Computer Science II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-avanzada","x":"Computer Science II · 120 h · Topic 1 of 8 Advanced programming in C In C you program knowing where things are. The pointer is not an oddity of the language: it is the memory address, and understanding it is the difference between fighting the compiler and driving the hardware. Pointers Addresses Arrays malloc Modularization 01 Every variable has an address A variable is a name for a place in memory. That place has an address, and C lets you work with it directly, something most languages hide. Operator Reads as What it gives &x address of x Where x is stored *p contents of p The value at the"},{"t":"Structured programming language","d":"Topic · Computer Science I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/programacion-estructurada","x":"Computer Science I · 120 h · Topic 4 of 6 Structured programming language Three control structures, functions that can be understood separately and a compiler that translates all of it into machine instructions. C is the language where you can see both at once. C Compilation Functions Call stack Modules 01 From jumps to blocks The first programs were written with jumps: “if such-and-such happens, go to line 340.” They worked, but beyond a certain size nobody could follow the thread, because any line could be reached from any other. In 1968 Edsger Dijkstra published a letter with a title that b"},{"t":"Electrical properties of matter","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-electricas","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 6 of 15 Electrical properties of matter Pure silicon and doped silicon are the same substance, and their resistivity differs by a factor of almost a million. The only thing that changed was one phosphorus atom for every ten million atoms. Resistivity Temperature coefficient Constantan Polarization Superconductivity 01 Twenty-four orders of magnitude No other physical property spans such a large range. Between silver and quartz there is a factor of \\( 10^{24} \\), and in the middle is silicon, which can be placed almost wherever you want. \\[ \\rho = \\frac{RA}{L} \\qquad\\"},{"t":"Properties of the transform","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-fourier","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 24 of 34 Properties of the transform Each property says what happens to the spectrum when you touch the signal. With them, problems are solved without integrating again, and out comes the rise-time rule used every day in the lab. Properties Shifting Scaling Modulation Duality 01 The table you need to know how to read The properties of the transform are not curiosities to memorize: each one says what happens to the spectrum when you modify the signal, and vice versa. They are what lets you solve problems without ever integrating again. In time In frequency Wh"},{"t":"Magnetic properties of matter","d":"Topic · Physics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/propiedades-magneticas","x":"Physics II · 120 h · Topic 10 of 15 Magnetic properties of matter The area of the hysteresis loop is energy lost on every cycle. Multiplied by the frequency, it gives the watts that heat the core, and from that number comes the choice of material for every transformer. Permeability Domains Hysteresis Saturation Curie 01 Three responses to the field Every material responds to a magnetic field, even though in almost all of them the response is imperceptible. The quantity that measures it is the relative permeability, and according to its value materials split into three very unequal groups. \\[ \\"},{"t":"Protection and heat removal","d":"Topic · Power Electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protecciones-potencia","x":"Power Electronics · 96 h · Topic 8 of 8 Protection and heat removal A transistor that dissipates 200 W in 1 ms pulses with a 10 % duty cycle peaks at 71 °C; with 100 ms pulses and the same average, at 128 °C. The average power is not enough for sizing. Snubber Desaturation Ultrafast fuse Thermal resistance Thermal impedance 01 Overvoltages When a switch carrying current opens, the parasitic inductance of the circuit —traces, cables, package— generates an overvoltage \\( L\\,di/dt \\) that adds to that of the bus. With the edges of tens of nanoseconds of modern devices, a few nanohenries are enoug"},{"t":"Serial communications and protocols","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/protocolos-seriales","x":"Digital Techniques II · 120 h · Topic 5 of 6 Serial communications and protocols A CAN frame carries at most 8 data bytes and 47 control bits. That is why, even at 1 Mbit/s, a car’s bus carries no more than about 72 kB/s of useful data. UART SPI I²C CAN Line coding 01 Why almost everything is serial For decades data traveled in parallel: eight, sixteen or thirty-two lines at a time. At high speed that stops working: the bits of a single data word arrive with timing differences because of the different lengths of the traces, and every line that switches adds noise to its neighbors. A single dif"},{"t":"Hypothesis tests","d":"Topic · Probability and Statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pruebas-hipotesis","x":"Probability and Statistics · 72 h · Topic 5 of 6 Hypothesis tests Moving the decision threshold splits the risk between the two possible errors, but does not reduce it. The only thing that lowers both at once is measuring more, and which of the two hurts more is an engineering decision. Null hypothesis Type I and II error Power p-value Significance 01 How a test is set up A hypothesis test decides between two statements using data. The structure is always the same and is deliberately asymmetric: you start by assuming that nothing is going on and you only abandon that assumption if the data mak"},{"t":"Bridges and component measurement","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/puentes-qmetro","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 5 of 11 Bridges and component measurement At bridge balance no current flows through the detector, so the result does not depend on the detector or on the source: only on the reference components. Wheatstone Kelvin Maxwell Q meter Four terminals 01 Measuring by comparison A bridge measures a component by comparing it with other known ones. Instead of reading a voltage on an instrument, the known components are adjusted until a detector reads zero. At this balance no current flows through the detector and the result does not depend on its accuracy or on"},{"t":"Environmental chemistry","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/quimica-ambiente","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 12 of 12 Environmental chemistry One kilogram of sulfur hexafluoride escaped from an etch chamber warms as much as twenty-three and a half metric tons of carbon dioxide. It is inert, it is not toxic and it does not touch the ozone layer: the problem is that it never goes away. Ozone Greenhouse effect GWP RoHS and WEEE Effluents 01 The atmosphere as a reactor Environmental chemistry studies reactions that take place in systems that nobody designed and that nobody can opt out of. Two of them get confused all the time and it pays to separate them from the start,"},{"t":"Electromagnetic radiation","d":"Topic · Transmission Media","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electromagnetica","x":"Transmission Media · 96 h · Topic 7 of 8 Electromagnetic radiation A car's one-meter antenna has, in the AM band, a radiation resistance of thousandths of an ohm. A half-wave dipole has 73 Ω and connects straight to a coaxial cable. Elementary dipole Radiation resistance Directivity Radiation pattern Near field 01 Why a conductor radiates A charge at rest creates an electric field; a charge in uniform motion also creates a magnetic one. But only an accelerated charge sheds fields that move away on their own and carry energy off to infinity: that is radiation. In a conductor carrying alternatin"},{"t":"Radiation, the electron and X-rays","d":"Topic · Electronic Physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/radiacion-electron","x":"Electronic Physics · 120 h · Topic 2 of 9 Radiation, the electron and X-rays Classical physics predicted that a hot body would radiate infinite energy in the ultraviolet. To avoid that absurdity, Planck assumed that energy came in packets, and he himself considered the idea a mathematical trick. Blackbody Ultraviolet catastrophe Planck Electron X-rays 01 The ultraviolet catastrophe Every hot body radiates. Nineteenth-century physics could calculate how: the radiation inside a cavity was treated as a set of modes, each one was assigned the average energy dictated by classical thermodynamics, an"},{"t":"Receivers","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/receptores","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 9 of 10 Receivers A 200 kHz receiver with a 6 dB noise figure hears down to −105 dBm, but two neighbors at −45 dBm already fill its channel with intermodulation. The dynamic range lies between those two numbers. Superheterodyne Sensitivity Noise floor SFDR Blocking 01 The superheterodyne receiver and its successors A receiver has to find a weak signal among others that are much stronger. The superheterodyne, invented by Edwin Armstrong in 1918, solves it with a mixer that translates each channel to a fixed intermediate frequency, where a selective filter"},{"t":"Lines and planes","d":"Topic · Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recta-y-plano","x":"Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry · 120 h · Topic 5 of 9 Lines and planes A point and a direction define a line; a point and a normal define a plane. Everything else follows from those two ideas. Vector equation Normal vector Distances Angles Relative positions 01 Two pieces of data are enough All the analytic geometry of space rests on two simple ideas: A line It is defined by a point and a direction. The direction is given by a vector, called the direction vector. A plane It is defined by a point and a perpendicular. That perpendicular is given by the normal vector. There are other ways t"},{"t":"Power rectifiers","d":"Topic · Power Electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/rectificadores-potencia","x":"Power Electronics · 96 h · Topic 2 of 8 Power rectifiers With a 30° delay, a three-phase thyristor bridge connected to 400 V delivers 468 V instead of 540, and its power factor drops from 0.95 to 0.83. Regulating the voltage with the firing angle consumes reactive power. Six-pulse bridge Firing angle Inverter mode Commutation Harmonics 01 Uncontrolled rectifiers A diode rectifier delivers a fixed DC voltage, which depends only on the line. With a highly inductive load, the output current is nearly constant and each diode conducts for a portion of the cycle: Configuration Average voltage Pulses"},{"t":"Human resources and labor relations","d":"Topic · Industrial Organization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/recursos-humanos","x":"Industrial Organization · 48 h · Topic 7 of 7 Human resources and labor relations Evaluated by points, the maintenance technician position is worth more than the product engineer position, but it pays less: it sits 17 index points below the salary line. Job evaluation brings such internal inequities to light. Selection Training Job evaluation Motivation Collective agreement 01 The human resources functions People are the resource that decides how all the others are used. The human resources area takes care of their complete cycle in the company: Function What it does Planning Estimates how man"},{"t":"Data networks and protocols","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/redes-datos","x":"Digital Techniques III · 120 h · Topic 2 of 3 Data networks and protocols With the original 64 KB window and a 100 ms round trip, a TCP connection cannot exceed 5.2 Mbit/s even if the link is one gigabit. The limit is not in the cable. TCP/IP stack Ethernet IP addressing TCP window MQTT 01 Layers and encapsulation A data network solves many problems at once: transmitting bits over a medium, sharing the medium among stations, finding the path between networks, delivering the data complete and in order, and giving it meaning. So that each problem can be solved separately, protocols are organized"},{"t":"Reflectometry","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/reflectometria","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 7 of 10 Reflectometry A step that returns 303 ns later says that the cable has a fault at 30 m. The shape of the step says whether it is an open, a short or a badly assembled connector. TDR Velocity factor Reflection coefficient Reactive loads OTDR 01 A radar for cables Time-domain reflectometry (TDR) injects a fast step into a transmission line and observes the voltage at the input. While the step travels along a uniform line, the input sees the characteristic impedance and the voltage stays at a fixed value. When the wavefront meets a discontinuity,"},{"t":"Steady state and spectra","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-espectros","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 7 of 13 Steady state and spectra A filter does not deform a signal in some mysterious way: it changes the proportion among its harmonics. With that single idea, everything learned for sinusoids is useful for any periodic signal. Fourier series Spectrum Harmonics Parseval THD 01 Any periodic signal is a sum of sinusoids Everything learned about sinusoidal steady state would seem to be useful only for pure sinusoidal signals, which in practice hardly exist. Fourier solved the problem in 1807: every reasonable periodic signal is a sum of sinusoids whose frequencie"},{"t":"Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regimen-sinusoidal","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 4 of 13 Sinusoidal steady state and the s-plane The gain of a filter at any frequency can be measured with a ruler on a drawing: it is the distance between a point on the imaginary axis and the pole. The s-plane turns the frequency response into geometry. s-plane Poles and zeros Bode Decibels Power factor 01 The complex frequency \\( s \\) The previous topic used \\( j\\omega \\) to turn derivatives into products. That \\( j\\omega \\) is a particular case of something more general: the complex frequency \\( s = \\sigma + j\\omega \\). A signal of the form \\( e^{st} \\) cov"},{"t":"Regression analysis","d":"Topic · Probability and Statistics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/regresion","x":"Probability and Statistics · 72 h · Topic 6 of 6 Regression analysis Four data sets can have the same line, the same R² and the same mean, and look nothing alike. That is the Anscombe quartet, and it is the reason a residual plot is not optional. Least squares Calibration R² Residuals Anscombe 01 The line of best fit Measuring a sensor’s output at several known temperatures gives a cloud of points, not a line. Regression looks for the line that best represents them, and “best” has a precise definition: the one that minimizes the sum of the squares of the vertical distances. \\[ \\hat{y} = b_0 +"},{"t":"Special relativity","d":"Topic · Electronic Physics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/relatividad","x":"Electronic Physics · 120 h · Topic 3 of 9 Special relativity In a television cathode-ray tube the electrons travel at 30 % of the speed of light, and GPS would drift out of sync by eleven kilometers per day if it did not correct for relativistic effects. Relativity is not an exotic topic: it is inside the device. Postulates Time dilation Lorentz E = mc² GPS 01 Two postulates and everything else Einstein did not add complications: he removed an assumption. Faced with the null result of Michelson-Morley, instead of inventing mechanisms to save the ether, he declared it unnecessary and postulated"},{"t":"Representation of signals and systems","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/representacion-sistemas","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 28 of 34 Representation of signals and systems Four ways of describing the same thing, none of them truer than the others. Each problem is easy to solve from one of them, and a good part of the craft consists of choosing where to look from. Differential equation Transfer function State variables Difference equation z-plane 01 The same system, four descriptions An LTI system can be described in four different ways, and all four say exactly the same thing. They are not alternatives you have to choose between: they are points of view, and each problem is easier"},{"t":"Residues applied to real integrals","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos-aplicaciones","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 12 of 34 Residues applied to real integrals There are real integrals with no elementary antiderivative that come out in two lines if you take them off the real axis and close them in the complex plane. Among other things, that is where the noise bandwidth of a filter comes from. Residues Jordan’s lemma Principal value Noise bandwidth Fourier 01 A real integral, solved from the outside Some real integrals have no elementary antiderivative and yet come out in two lines if you take them off the real axis and close them in the complex plane. The maneuver is alwa"},{"t":"The residue theorem","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/residuos","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 11 of 34 The residue theorem An integral over an entire curve is reduced to adding up a few numbers, one per singularity. And you already knew those numbers: they are the coefficients of the partial fraction expansion. Residues Laurent Partial fractions Inverse transform Bromwich 01 It all comes down to one coefficient When the Laurent series is integrated term by term over a closed contour, all the terms give zero except one: the \\( 1/(z - z_0) \\) term, which gives \\( 2\\pi j \\). That coefficient, the \\( b_1 \\) of the expansion, is the residue, and it is the"},{"t":"Systematic circuit analysis","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resolucion-sistematica","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 9 of 13 Systematic circuit analysis A single diagonal resistor is enough for a circuit to stop being reducible by series and parallel. Nodal analysis solves it just the same, and it is exactly what any circuit simulator does on the inside. Nodal Mesh Conductance matrix Wheatstone bridge SPICE 01 When series and parallel are not enough As long as a circuit can be reduced by combining resistors in series and in parallel, that is enough. But it takes just one element connecting two branches “diagonally” for there to be no pair of resistors strictly in series or in"},{"t":"Resonance","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/resonancia","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 6 of 13 Resonance With a 10 V source, a thousand volts can appear across the capacitor of a resonant circuit. The same phenomenon tunes a radio, sets the time of a quartz clock and powers a SUBE card with no battery. Resonance Quality factor Bandwidth Tuning Quartz 01 Series resonance In a series RLC circuit the inductive reactance \\( \\omega L \\) grows with frequency and the capacitive reactance \\( 1/\\omega C \\) falls. There is a single frequency at which the two are equal and cancel: the resonant frequency. There the impedance is reduced to \\( R \\), its minimu"},{"t":"Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/responsabilidad-profesional","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 12 of 13 Professional liability: civil, administrative and criminal A single accident can end with the engineer paying compensation, suspended from the register and prosecuted. These are three independent routes. Civil liability Basis of liability Disciplinary liability Negligent crime Statute of limitations 01 Three independent liabilities When something goes wrong in work signed by an engineer, the engineer can be held liable through three channels at once, each with its own purpose, rules and tribunal: Civil Administrative and disciplinary Criminal Purpose Repairi"},{"t":"Frequency response of amplifiers","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-amplificadores","x":"Applied Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 3 of 8 Frequency response of amplifiers A 2 pF capacitance between base and collector looks like almost 400 pF from the input. The Miller effect is what limits the bandwidth of a common emitter. Midband Hybrid-π Transition frequency Miller effect Time constants 01 Three frequency regions The gain of a transistor amplifier is constant only over a band, the midband. Below it the gain falls because of the coupling and bypass capacitors, which stop being short circuits; above it, because of the internal capacitances of the transistor, which stop being open ci"},{"t":"Frequency response in control","d":"Topic · Control Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-control","x":"Control Systems · 96 h · Topic 7 of 8 Frequency response in control A delay does not change the gain of a loop, but it adds unbounded phase: its Nyquist plot is a spiral. That is why processes with delay are controlled with low gains. Bode plot Nyquist criterion Gain margin Phase margin Delay 01 Designing by looking at the loop gain in frequency The root locus needs a model with poles and zeros. The frequency response works with what can be measured: the loop gain \\( G(j\\omega) \\), with a generator and an analyzer, even if the plant has delays or parts that cannot be modeled. Its two represent"},{"t":"Frequency response of feedback amplifiers","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia-realimentados","x":"Applied Electronics II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 8 Frequency response of feedback amplifiers The same amplifier that is stable with a gain of 100 oscillates as a follower. The lower the feedback gain, the harder it is to stabilize. Gain–bandwidth Complex poles Quality factor Phase margin Overshoot 01 One pole: the gain–bandwidth product If the amplifier has a single pole, \\( A(s) = A_0/(1 + s/\\omega_1) \\), feedback shifts it. Substituting into \\( A/(1 + A\\beta) \\): \\[ A_f(s) = \\frac{A_0/(1+T_0)}{1 + s/\\big[\\omega_1 (1 + T_0)\\big]} \\] \\( T_0 = A_0\\beta \\) is the DC loop gain. The gain goes down by \\"},{"t":"Frequency response","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-frecuencia","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 27 of 34 Frequency response An LTI system does not mix frequencies: each harmonic comes out scaled and phase-shifted, unaware of the others. But looking only at the magnitude is not enough, and an all-pass shows why. Bode Asymptotes Group delay Minimum phase Phase margin 01 What the system does to each frequency If a complex exponential enters an LTI system and comes out multiplied by \\( H(s) \\), then evaluating on the imaginary axis gives what happens to each real frequency: \\[ Y(j\\omega) = H(j\\omega)\\,X(j\\omega) \\] The output spectrum is the input spectrum"},{"t":"Transient response","d":"Topic · Control Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-transitoria-control","x":"Control Systems · 96 h · Topic 3 of 8 Transient response The overshoot of a second-order system depends only on the damping ratio: 16 % with 0.5 and under 5 % with 0.7. A nearby third pole breaks the rule. First order Damping Overshoot Settling time Dominant poles 01 First order: the time constant The step response of a first-order system, \\( G(s) = K/(\\tau s + 1) \\), is an exponential that reaches 63 % of the final value in one time constant \\( \\tau \\), 95 % in \\( 3\\tau \\) and 98 % in \\( 4\\tau \\). It has no overshoot. It is the model of the oven and of the simplified motor in the previous uni"},{"t":"Transient response and residues","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/respuesta-transitoria","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 8 of 13 Transient response and residues Before doing a single calculation, the location of the poles already says whether a circuit will oscillate, how long it will take to settle and whether it will overshoot. The residues only supply the numbers. Laplace Residues Poles Damping Overshoot 01 The whole circuit, transformed into \\( s \\) Steady state was solved with phasors. The transient —what happens from the moment a switch closes until the circuit settles— is solved with the Laplace transform, which does for derivatives the same thing that phasors do, but with"},{"t":"Technological risk and environmental risk","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/riesgo-tecnologico","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 6 of 11 Technological risk and environmental risk Two independent barriers that each fail once in a hundred fillings let one overflow through every ten thousand. If they share the sensor, one in a hundred: the redundancy disappears. Major accidents Natech Fault tree Common cause Bow-tie diagram 01 The risk that leaves the establishment Occupational risk affects those who work. Technological risk is broader: it is the possibility that an industrial installation, a transport system or an infrastructure causes harm to the population and the environmen"},{"t":"Real-time operating systems","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/rtos","x":"Digital Techniques II · 120 h · Topic 6 of 6 Real-time operating systems With a utilization of 95 %, three tasks can miss a deadline with fixed priorities and meet them all with EDF. The difference is not the power of the processor but the order in which they are served. Tasks RM scheduling EDF Response time Priority inversion 01 Real time does not mean fast A system is real-time when the correctness of a result also depends on when it is delivered. An airbag controller that computes perfectly but 50 ms late has failed. What matters is not the average speed but the worst case: the guarantee th"},{"t":"Noise in analog modulations","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ruido-modulaciones-analogicas","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 7 of 11 Noise in analog modulations Above the threshold, an FM with β = 5 outperforms the best linear modulation by 16 dB. About 5 dB below the threshold, it is already worse than DSB. Signal-to-noise ratio DSB, SSB and AM FM improvement FM threshold Pre-emphasis 01 A common reference To compare modulations we need a measure that does not depend on them. The reference taken is the transmission of the message in baseband, unmodulated, with the same received power \\( S_i \\) and white noise of density \\( N_0/2 \\) over the bandwidth \\( W \\) of the message: \\[ \\"},{"t":"Noise in digital modulations","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ruido-modulaciones-digitales","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 9 of 11 Noise in digital modulations For one error per million bits, a QPSK needs 10.5 dB of Eb/N0 and a 64-QAM, 18.8 dB. Tripling the bits per symbol costs almost seven times more energy per bit. Matched filter Q function Eb/N0 Error rate M-QAM 01 The optimum receiver In a digital system noise does not degrade quality gradually: it produces decision errors. The performance measure is the bit error probability, or BER. The receiver that minimizes it with white Gaussian noise has two parts: a matched filter for the pulse, which maximizes the signal-to-noise"},{"t":"Signals in circuit theory","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/senales-circuitos","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 2 of 13 Signals in circuit theory An ordinary multimeter measures almost any signal other than a pure sinusoid wrongly, and most signals in power electronics are not pure sinusoids. The difference between average value and RMS value explains why. Signal classification Elementary signals RMS value Form factor Energy and power signals 01 Classifying a signal before analyzing it Before solving any circuit you need to know what kind of signal is exciting it, because each kind allows different tools. Four classifications organize almost everything that follows in th"},{"t":"Continuous-time and discrete-time signals","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/senales-continuas-discretas","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 13 of 34 Continuous-time and discrete-time signals The only thing that changes between the two worlds of the course is where the independent variable lives. But that difference has consequences: in discrete time not every sinusoid is periodic, and frequency is confined to an interval. Discrete time Digital Periodicity Energy Power 01 Two independent variables, two worlds A signal is a function that carries information. The only thing that changes between the two worlds of this subject is the set where the independent variable lives: \\[ x(t),\\ t \\in \\mathbb{R"},{"t":"Digital signals, logic analyzers and electromagnetic compatibility","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/senales-digitales-emc","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 9 of 10 Digital signals, logic analyzers and electromagnetic compatibility Two identical 25 MHz clocks emit very differently at 300 MHz if one has 1 ns edges and the other 5 ns. What decides the high emissions is the rise time, not the frequency. Logic analyzer Electromagnetic compatibility CISPR 32 Trapezoidal spectrum Rise time 01 The logic analyzer A digital system has dozens of lines changing at once. The oscilloscope shows a few with analog detail; the logic analyzer shows many, 16, 32 or more, but only as zeros and ones, comparing each input wit"},{"t":"Signals and signal sources","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/senales-fuentes","x":"Applied Electronics I · 120 h · Topic 1 of 7 Signals and signal sources A 600 Ω microphone connected to a 600 Ω input loses half of its signal before the amplifier does anything. Choosing an amplifier starts with looking at the source. Thévenin and Norton Decibels Loading effect Input impedance Amplifier types 01 The signal and its source An amplifier never works alone: it takes the signal from a source —a microphone, a sensor, an antenna, the output of another circuit— and delivers it to a load. How well or badly it performs depends as much on those two ends as on the amplifier itself. That i"},{"t":"Measuring non-sinusoidal signals","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/senales-no-senoidales","x":"Electronic Measurements I · 120 h · Topic 4 of 11 Measuring non-sinusoidal signals A multimeter that is not true RMS reads a square wave 11% too high and a 10% pulse train one third too low. With the sine wave, the only shape it is calibrated for, it gets it right. RMS value Form factor Crest factor True RMS Bandwidth 01 Three values for the same signal Several things can be measured from a periodic voltage, and each answers a different question: \\[ V_{avg} = \\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T v\\, dt \\] \\[ V_{rect} = \\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T |v|\\, dt \\] \\[ V_{rms} = \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T v^2\\, dt} \\] The aver"},{"t":"Periodic signals","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/senales-periodicas","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 22 of 34 Periodic signals Four numbers matter in a periodic signal, and two of them give the form factor: the 1.111 that a cheap multimeter applies blindly, and that makes it wrong for any wave that is not sinusoidal. Period RMS value Form factor Crest factor Multimeter 01 Repeating exactly A signal is periodic if there exists a positive \\( T \\) that maps it back onto itself: \\[ x(t + T) = x(t) \\quad\\text{for all } t \\qquad\\qquad x[n + N] = x[n] \\quad\\text{for all } n \\] The smallest \\( T \\) that satisfies this is the fundamental period. In discrete time, \\("},{"t":"Discrete-time signals","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/senales-tiempo-discreto","x":"Circuit Theory II · 120 h · Topic 6 of 8 Discrete-time signals A 7 kHz sinusoid sampled at 8 kHz gives exactly the same samples as a 1 kHz one. In discrete time, frequency only makes sense between zero and half the sampling frequency. Sequences Normalized frequency Difference equation z-plane DFT 01 From signal to sequence When a converter samples a signal every \\( T \\) seconds, what is left is a sequence of numbers \\( x[n] = x(nT) \\), defined only for integer \\( n \\). What happens between samples is lost, and by the sampling theorem that does not matter if the signal had no frequencies above"},{"t":"Sensors for automation and control","d":"Topic · Electronic Technology","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sensores-automatizacion","x":"Electronic Technology · 120 h · Topic 6 of 9 Sensors for automation and control A single gauge in a bridge mistakes 20 °C of heating for 100 microstrain. With two or four gauges in adjacent arms, the temperature cancels and the signal doubles or quadruples. Proximity sensor Encoder Strain gauge Wheatstone bridge 4–20 mA loop 01 Presence and position sensors An automation system needs to know where the parts and mechanisms are. Presence sensors give a binary output; position sensors, a value: Sensor Principle Detects Limit switch Mechanical contact Any object that actuates it Inductive An oscil"},{"t":"Fourier series and integrals","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/series-fourier","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 23 of 34 Fourier series and integrals Every reasonable periodic signal is a sum of sinusoids whose frequencies are multiples of the fundamental. And since each one goes through an LTI system without changing shape, it is enough to know what happens to each harmonic. Fourier series Orthogonality Dirichlet Gibbs Spectrum 01 Building any periodic signal out of sinusoids The result that opens the second half of the subject is this one: every reasonable periodic signal can be written as a sum of harmonically related complex exponentials, that is, of sinusoids who"},{"t":"Series","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/series","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 10 of 10 Series Adding infinitely many terms can give a finite number. That idea makes it possible to represent functions as infinite polynomials and signals as sums of sinusoids. Convergence Tests Power series Taylor Fourier 01 First, sequences A sequence is an infinite ordered list of numbers: a₁, a₂, a₃, … All that matters about it is its long-run behavior: whether it approaches a number — converges — or not. Sequence Behavior Where it appears a n = 1/n Converges to 0 Error of a numerical method a n = r n Converges to 0 if |r| < 1; diverges if |r| > 1 Amplitude of"},{"t":"Singularities","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/singularidades","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 10 of 34 Singularities There are exactly three ways for a function to be undefined at an isolated point, and the Laurent series tells them apart at a glance. The third, the essential one, is the reason a pure delay cannot be written with poles and zeros. Laurent Removable Pole of order m Essential Branching 01 Three ways of being undefined A point \\( z_0 \\) is an isolated singularity of \\( f \\) if the function is analytic on a neighborhood of \\( z_0 \\) except at \\( z_0 \\) itself. The classification is made by looking at what happens as you approach, and ther"},{"t":"Frequency synthesis","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sintesis-frecuencia","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 2 of 10 Frequency synthesis With 1 kΩ in the filter, a PLL takes 645 μs to change channel; with 3.3 kΩ, 205 μs. A single resistor decides whether the loop oscillates before locking. Direct synthesis Charge-pump PLL Loop filter Damping Lock time 01 How a frequency is synthesized A transmitter or a receiver needs local oscillators that change channel with the accuracy of a crystal. There are three families of synthesizers, which are combined in real equipment: Technique Idea Advantage Limit Analog direct synthesis Mix, multiply and divide reference frequen"},{"t":"Structure of a computer system","d":"Topic · Computer Science I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistema-computacional","x":"Computer Science I · 120 h · Topic 1 of 6 Structure of a computer system Before writing a line of code, it pays to know what machine you are writing for: what parts it has, how it fetches and executes each instruction, where it keeps its data and what makes it fast. Von Neumann Instruction cycle Memory Buses Performance 01 A computer is a stack of machines Nobody programs a computer “as a whole.” You program the machine that the layer below offers. Someone writing in C sees a machine that understands functions and variables; the compiler translates it into the machine that understands instruct"},{"t":"The Argentine legal system","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistema-normativo","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 4 of 13 The Argentine legal system A municipal ordinance cannot contradict a national law, and an IRAM standard binds no one until a legal norm makes it mandatory. Knowing what prevails over what avoids many mistakes. Normative pyramid Laws and decrees Entry into force Provinces and municipalities IRAM standards 01 The pyramid of norms Legal rules are not all at the same level. They form a tiered order, which the jurist Hans Kelsen described as a pyramid: each rule derives its validity from a higher one and cannot contradict it. When two rules collide, the higher-ran"},{"t":"Introduction to communication systems","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistemas-comunicacion","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 1 of 11 Introduction to communication systems The same low-noise amplifier improves a receiver's sensitivity by 5 dB if it is placed at the antenna rather than after the cable. In a receiver, the order of the stages matters. Decibel and dBm Thermal noise Noise figure Friis formula Sensitivity 01 Carrying information from one place to another Every communication system has the same blocks: a source that produces the message, a transmitter that adapts it to the medium (encodes, modulates, amplifies), a channel that carries it and degrades it (attenuates it, d"},{"t":"Systems of linear equations","d":"Topic · Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistemas-ecuaciones","x":"Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry · 120 h · Topic 3 of 9 Systems of linear equations Solving a three-mesh circuit, fitting a calibration curve or simulating an entire network are the same problem: A·x = b. Only the size changes. Gauss-Jordan Rouché-Frobenius Homogeneous systems Pivoting Mesh analysis 01 Where linear systems come from Almost every engineering problem ends up, at some point, as a system of linear equations. Kirchhoff’s laws applied to a circuit of n meshes give n equations in n unknown currents. A truss, a material balance, a least-squares fit of a calibration curve and the s"},{"t":"Embedded systems hardware and software","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistemas-embebidos","x":"Digital Techniques II · 120 h · Topic 4 of 6 Embedded systems hardware and software A sensor that sleeps at 2 µA lasts more than two years on a CR2032 cell. If a peripheral is left on and the sleep current rises to 50 µA, the same cell lasts four months. Microcontroller Boot code Interrupts Low power Watchdog 01 A computer nobody sees An embedded system is a computer dedicated to one task inside a product: the control of a washing machine, an airbag unit, a gas meter, a pacemaker. It does not look like a computer and is almost never reprogrammed, but it has a processor, memory, peripherals and"},{"t":"Linear time-invariant systems","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistemas-lti","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 17 of 34 Linear time-invariant systems Two properties, and with them a single measurement is enough to know the whole system. They also explain why, to translate a signal in frequency, one of the two has to be broken. Linearity Invariance LTI Impulse response Mixer 01 Linearity: superposition A system is a box that receives a signal and returns another. It is linear if it responds to a combination of inputs with the same combination of their outputs: \\[ T\\{a\\,x_1(t) + b\\,x_2(t)\\} = a\\,T\\{x_1(t)\\} + b\\,T\\{x_2(t)\\} \\] With \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) arbitrary constan"},{"t":"Material systems","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistemas-materiales","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 1 of 12 Material systems A transistor requires silicon with fewer than one foreign atom per billion. Reaching that purity is a problem of separating mixtures, and it is solved with a technique you can understand from what is covered in this unit. Pure substance Mixtures Phases Separation Zone refining 01 How what is there gets classified Everything you can touch is a material system, and the first question is whether it has a single composition or several. The whole classification follows from that: System Phases Separated Example from the workshop Simple pure"},{"t":"Number systems and binary arithmetic","d":"Topic · Computer Science I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistemas-numeracion","x":"Computer Science I · 120 h · Topic 2 of 6 Number systems and binary arithmetic Inside the machine everything is bits. What changes is how they are read: the same byte can be an unsigned number, a negative one, a character or an instruction. Binary Hexadecimal Two’s complement Overflow IEEE 754 01 A number is not its notation Thirteen sheep are thirteen sheep, whether written 13, XIII, 1101 or D. The number is the quantity; what changes is the system used to write it down. The systems used in computing are positional: each digit weighs according to the place it occupies, and the weight of each"},{"t":"Operating system fundamentals","d":"Topic · Computer Science II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistemas-operativos","x":"Computer Science II · 120 h · Topic 8 of 8 Operating system fundamentals The operating system is what shares out the processor, the memory and the peripherals among programs that believe they own the machine. Understanding how it does so explains almost every odd behavior of a program. Processes Threads Scheduling Virtual memory Real time 01 The one that shares out the machine A program is written as if it owned the processor, the memory and the peripherals. It does not: dozens are running at once. The operating system maintains that illusion and shares out what there is. Resource What the pro"},{"t":"Representation systems","d":"Topic · Computer-Aided Design","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sistemas-representacion","x":"Computer-Aided Design · 72 h · Topic 1 of 5 Representation systems A drawing is a language: it turns a three-dimensional object into flat figures that any workshop in the world can turn back into the object. The first thing to learn is how to project. Orthographic projection Views First-angle projection Third-angle projection Isometric 01 Projecting Representing a three-dimensional object on paper requires choosing how to project it. The two families that matter in engineering are: System How it projects What it is for Orthographic projection (views) Parallel rays, perpendicular to the plane M"},{"t":"The operating system and the hardware","d":"Topic · Digital Techniques III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/so-hardware","x":"Digital Techniques III · 120 h · Topic 3 of 3 The operating system and the hardware With the FIFO algorithm, giving a program more memory can make it fault more. This is Belady’s anomaly, and it shows up with twelve references. Privilege modes Interrupts and DMA Virtual memory TLB Page replacement 01 A general-purpose operating system A real-time operating system promises deadlines. A general-purpose one, such as Linux, Windows or macOS, promises other things: that many programs share the machine fairly, that none can harm the others or the system, and that each sees more memory than there is."},{"t":"Companies","d":"Topic · Legislation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/sociedades-comerciales","x":"Legislation · 48 h · Topic 5 of 13 Companies Two engineers who start a business without incorporating it are personally liable, with their own assets, for the debts of the business. Choosing the type of company is choosing how much to put at risk. General Companies Law SRL SA SAS Liability of the partners 01 What a company is Under Article 1 of the Ley General de Sociedades (General Companies Law, Law 19,550), a company exists when one or more persons, organized according to one of the types the law provides for, undertake to make contributions in order to produce or exchange goods or services"},{"t":"Soldering in electronics","d":"Topic · Electronic Technology","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/soldadura","x":"Electronic Technology · 120 h · Topic 8 of 9 Soldering in electronics The same oven that melts the paste well on a light board leaves a heavy board 11 °C colder and barely 43 s above the melting point. The thermal mass of the board is also part of the profile. Soft soldering SAC305 Reflow Thermal profile IPC-A-610 01 Soft soldering In electronics, soft soldering is used: an alloy that melts below 450 °C joins the parts without melting them. The solder wets the copper and reacts with it, forming a thin layer of intermetallic compounds, which provides the electrical and mechanical bond; if that"},{"t":"Solutions","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/soluciones","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 7 of 12 Solutions The purest water that can be produced measures 18.2 MΩ·cm, and that number is not a purification goal but a physical limit: water ionizes itself, and those intrinsic ions set the floor. Concentration Solubility Electrolytes Kohlrausch Colligative 01 How much of what A solution is a homogeneous mixture: the solute is dispersed in the solvent at the molecular scale, with no separating surface. The only thing to decide is how to report how much there is, and there half a dozen units coexist that are not interchangeable. Unit Definition When it i"},{"t":"Technology, environment and society","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tecnologia-ambiente-sociedad","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 7 of 11 Technology, environment and society If the population grows 0.8% per year and output per person 2%, halving emissions in 25 years requires energy and carbon per unit of output to fall by more than 5% per year. IPAT Kaya identity Rebound effect Decoupling Precautionary principle 01 A two-way relationship Technology is not neutral with respect to the environment: every technique changes which resources are extracted, what energy is used and what waste is generated. But it does not determine it alone either: the impact depends on how many peop"},{"t":"Construction technology","d":"Topic · Electronic Technology","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tecnologia-constructiva","x":"Electronic Technology · 120 h · Topic 7 of 9 Construction technology An external trace of 35 μm copper carrying 3 A with a 10 °C rise needs 1.37 mm of width; on an inner layer, 3.56 mm. Inside the board the heat gets out worse. Printed circuit board FR-4 Trace width IPC-2221 IP rating 01 The printed circuit board The printed circuit board is at once the mechanical support and the wiring of a device. It is made on a laminate: the most common is FR-4, glass-fiber cloth with epoxy resin, which is specified by its glass transition temperature (T g ), its permittivity and its thickness, typically 1"},{"t":"Microelectronic technology","d":"Topic · Electronic Technology","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tecnologia-microelectronica","x":"Electronic Technology · 120 h · Topic 9 of 9 Microelectronic technology Quadrupling the area of a chip, from 100 to 400 mm², multiplies its cost by six: four times fewer fit on the wafer and, besides, a smaller proportion comes out good. Wafer Photolithography CMOS process Packaging Yield 01 From silicon to the wafer An integrated circuit is fabricated on a monocrystalline silicon wafer, sliced from an ingot grown from molten silicon by the Czochralski method. Current wafers are 200 or 300 mm in diameter and less than a millimeter thick, and are polished until the surface is flat on the atomic"},{"t":"Cauchy’s integral theorem","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/teorema-cauchy","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 7 of 34 Cauchy’s integral theorem If the function is analytic inside, the integral over any closed curve is zero. From that comes what is used most: a contour can be deformed at will as long as it does not cross a singularity. Cauchy-Goursat Green Deforming contours Simply connected Nyquist 01 The statement It is the result that organizes the whole second half of complex variable, and it can be said in one line. \\[ f \\text{ analytic on a simply connected domain } D \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; \\oint_C f(z)\\,dz = 0 \\] For every closed curve \\( C \\) contained in \\( D \\"},{"t":"The fundamental theorem of calculus","d":"Topic · Calculus I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/teorema-fundamental","x":"Calculus I · 120 h · Topic 8 of 10 The fundamental theorem of calculus Differentiation and integration are inverse operations. That result, which seems obvious, is the theorem that unifies all of calculus and lets you compute areas without adding infinitely many terms. Area function Newton–Leibniz rule Antiderivative Derivative and integral Applications 01 The area function Up to here the definite integral gave a number. But if you hold the lower endpoint fixed and move the upper one, a function appears: the one that accumulates area as it advances. \\[ A (x) = \\int_a^x f (t) d t \\] The variabl"},{"t":"The theorems of vector calculus","d":"Topic · Calculus II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/teoremas-calculo-vectorial","x":"Calculus II · 120 h · Topic 8 of 8 The theorems of vector calculus Green, Gauss and Stokes say the same thing in different dimensions: what happens inside a region is determined by what happens on its boundary. Maxwell’s equations come from there. Green Divergence Stokes Maxwell Integral form 01 One idea, three statements The three theorems that close vector calculus say the same thing in different guises: what happens on the boundary is determined by what happens inside. It is the same structure as the Fundamental Theorem of Calculus, but with one more dimension each time. Theorem Boundary In"},{"t":"Circuit theorems","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/teoremas-circuitos","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 10 of 13 Circuit theorems Seen from two terminals, any linear circuit is a source with a series resistance, and no experiment can tell them apart. Why an antenna is matched at 50 Ω and a loudspeaker is not comes from there. Superposition Thévenin Norton Maximum power Matching 01 Superposition In a linear circuit with several independent sources, any voltage or current is the sum of those that each source would produce acting alone. To “turn off” a source it is replaced by what it is when it delivers zero: a voltage source by a short circuit; a current source by"},{"t":"Information theory","d":"Topic · Communication Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/teoria-informacion","x":"Communication Systems · 96 h · Topic 10 of 11 Information theory A 3100 Hz telephone channel with 35 dB of signal-to-noise ratio supports at most about 36 kbit/s. The last voice modems reached 33.6: theory set the ceiling before they existed. Entropy Huffman code Channel capacity Shannon–Hartley Channel coding 01 Measuring information Claude Shannon proposed in 1948 to measure the information of a message by how improbable it is: a message that was expected tells us nothing. The information of a symbol of probability \\( p \\), and the average over all the symbols of a source, the entropy, are:"},{"t":"ICT and the environment","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tic-medio-ambiente","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 11 of 11 ICT and the environment In 2022 the world generated 62 million tonnes of electronic waste and recycled only 22% of it in a documented way. What is decided at the design stage determines what happens to it. Data centers PUE WEEE RoHS Ecodesign 01 Two faces of ICT Information and communication technologies have a double relationship with the environment. On the one hand, they generate impacts: they consume energy in data centers, networks and devices; they require minerals whose extraction has environmental and social costs, and they produce"},{"t":"Time and frequency measurements","d":"Topic · Electronic Measurements II","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/tiempo-frecuencia","x":"Electronic Measurements II · 120 h · Topic 6 of 10 Time and frequency measurements A direct-counting counter with a 1 s gate measures 10 Hz with a 10% error. A reciprocal one, with one part in ten billion: the difference lies in what is counted. Frequency standards Allan deviation Direct counting Reciprocal counter Timebase 01 The best-measured quantity Time is the quantity that is measured with the greatest accuracy. Since 1967, the second of the International System has been defined by the radiation of cesium 133: 9,192,631,770 periods of a transition of its atom. The cesium atomic clocks of"},{"t":"Safe work procedures","d":"Topic · Safety, Health and Environment","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/trabajo-seguro","x":"Safety, Health and Environment · 48 h · Topic 3 of 11 Safe work procedures In an installation with a ground rod, a ground fault leaves the enclosure of a device at more than 100 V without the circuit breaker noticing. What saves the person who touches it is the residual-current device. Job safety analysis Work permit Five golden rules TT system Residual-current device 01 What a safe work procedure is A safe work procedure describes step by step how to carry out a risky task: what has to be prepared, what hazards appear at each step, what controls are applied and who gives authorization. It is"},{"t":"Linear transformations","d":"Topic · Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-lineales","x":"Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry · 120 h · Topic 8 of 9 Linear transformations A function that respects addition and scalar multiplication is determined by what it does to a basis, and that fits in a matrix. Associated matrix Kernel Image Rank-nullity theorem Composition 01 Functions that respect the structure A linear transformation is a function between vector spaces that plays well with the two operations: \\[ T (\\vec{u} + \\vec{v}) = T (\\vec{u}) + T (\\vec{v}) \\quad T (k \\vec{u}) = k \\, T (\\vec{u}) \\] The two conditions together boil down to one: T(a·u + b·v) = a·T(u) + b·T(v). It is the"},{"t":"Transformations of the independent variable","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformaciones-variable","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 14 of 34 Transformations of the independent variable Three operations and nothing more: shift, mirror and stretch. The trouble starts when they are combined, because the shift of x(at+b) is not b but b/a, and everyone gets that wrong at some point. Shifting Reflection Scaling Key points Decimation 01 Three operations and nothing more Everything that can be done to the time axis of a signal is built from three moves: Operation Expression What it does Shifting \\( x(t - t_0) \\) With \\( t_0 \\gt 0 \\) it moves to the right: it is a delay Reflection \\( x(-t) \\) Mir"},{"t":"The Z-transform and notions of filtering","d":"Topic · Signals and Systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transformada-z","x":"Signals and Systems · 144 h · Topic 34 of 34 The Z-transform and notions of filtering The same as Laplace, for sequences. The imaginary axis becomes the unit circle, and everything you knew about stability carries over by replacing “left of the axis” with “inside the circle”. Z-transform Unit circle Poles and zeros Notch Digital filters 01 Laplace, for sequences The Z transform is to discrete signals what Laplace is to continuous ones. The definition replaces the integral with a sum and the exponential with a power: \\[ X(z) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} x[n]\\,z^{-n}, \\qquad z \\in \\mathbb{C} \\] E"},{"t":"Transmitters","d":"Topic · Applied Electronics III","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transmisores","x":"Applied Electronics III · 120 h · Topic 8 of 10 Transmitters To deliver 10 W average with a signal of 10 dB peak-to-average ratio, a class B amplifier draws 40 W from the supply. Three quarters of it becomes heat. Direct conversion EIRP Spectral mask ACLR Peak-to-average ratio 01 Transmitter architectures A transmitter generates the modulated signal, brings it to the channel frequency and gives it power. The architectures differ in where each step is done: Architecture How it works Advantage and limit Heterodyne Modulates at a fixed intermediate frequency and then mixes up to the channel Easy"},{"t":"Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport","d":"Topic · Electronic Devices","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/transporte-portadores","x":"Electronic Devices · 120 h · Topic 1 of 9 Basic semiconductor theory: carrier transport A silicon wafer with one phosphorus atom per ten million conducts more than a hundred thousand times better than pure silicon. Every device is born from controlling how many carriers there are and how they move. Drift Mobility Diffusion Einstein relation Recombination 01 From material to device The page on semiconductor physics established how many carriers there are: the intrinsic concentration \\( n_i \\), on the order of \\( 10^{10} \\) cm⁻³ in silicon at room temperature, the law of mass action \\( n\\,p = n_"},{"t":"Chemical bonding","d":"Topic · General Chemistry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/uniones-quimicas","x":"General Chemistry · 120 h · Topic 4 of 12 Chemical bonding Whether a material conducts, insulates or lies in between depends not on its atoms but on how they bond. Energy bands come from that, and from the bands comes every solid-state device. Electronegativity Ionic and covalent Bands Band gap Impurity levels 01 Three ways of sharing electrons Atoms bond because doing so lowers their energy, and they do it in three ways depending on how hard they contest the electrons. What decides it is electronegativity: the tendency of each atom to pull the bond's electrons toward itself. Bond Difference \\"},{"t":"Solving circuits with state variables","d":"Topic · Circuit Theory I","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/variables-estado","x":"Circuit Theory I · 144 h · Topic 13 of 13 Solving circuits with state variables A simulator can show a circuit that oscillates and grows without limit when the real circuit settles quietly. The state form explains how a simulator simulates, and why it is sometimes wrong. State variables Matrix A Phase plane Euler Runge-Kutta 01 What the state of a circuit is To predict what a circuit will do from some instant onward you do not need to know its entire history: it is enough to know how much energy each element that can store energy holds. That minimal information is the state, and in an electric"},{"t":"Vectors in R² and R³","d":"Topic · Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/vectores-r2-r3","x":"Linear Algebra and Analytic Geometry · 120 h · Topic 4 of 9 Vectors in R² and R³ Forces, fields, velocities and phasors have something in common: one number is not enough to describe them. You need a direction, and with it comes an algebra of its own. Components Dot product Cross product Projection Triple product 01 When one number is not enough The temperature of a room, the resistance of a resistor or the energy of a battery can be described with a number and a unit: they are scalars. The force on an electron, the electric field at a point or the velocity of a moving object also need a direc"},{"t":"MC1496","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/mc1496-modulador","x":"The MC1496 as a product modulator: DSB-SC versus AM, the chip inside, and the Figure 27 circuit from its datasheet with interactive oscilloscopes and spectra."},{"t":"🚦 Traffic Light with Logic Gates","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/semaforo-logico","x":"The intersection Street A runs from left to right and street B from top to bottom. The cars obey what the traffic lights show: they stop at red and at yellow, unless they are already so close to the line that they cannot stop in time. Drag to rotate the view and use the wheel to zoom in. Timing diagram of a full cycle The sixteen counter states, one per clock pulse. The blue band marks where the circuit is right now. Street A Street B The counter State 0 F = Q3 · Q2 0 Right of way Street A Controls ⏸ Pause Clock pulse Reset Simulation speed ×1 ×2 ×4 NE555 R2 68 kΩ Clock period 1.01 s Green · y"},{"t":"📐 Electronic and Electrical Symbols","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/simbologia","x":"The symbols of electronics and electrical engineering drawn to the standard: passive and active components, wiring and measurement, sensors, blocks, waveforms, an installation floor plan and a control panel. With a search box, a card for each symbol and a self-assessment. Tap a symbol to see its card. Practice with Correct 0 of 0 Another symbol →"},{"t":"LM555 Simulator","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/simulador-555","x":"Simulator of the LM555 timer in astable and monostable modes: choose R and C, see how the capacitor charges and discharges and where the times come from, with inverse design and the internal diagram."},{"t":"🔬 Instrument Simulator","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/simulador-instrumental","x":"← catto.ar Drag from a BNC connector to pull a cable and drop it on another one to connect it. Knobs are turned by dragging or with the little arrows at the side. Slide the bench sideways to reach everything. DSB-SC AM Upper SSB Lower SSB All four together Reading aids Initial state Remove the cables What they are measuring The same thing anyone would do at the instrument: count divisions on the oscilloscope and read the lines on the analyzer. Oscilloscope A No signal on screen. Spectrum analyzer No signal at the input. Guided practice 1 · Bring in a signal and measure it 2 · Zero, DC and coup"},{"t":"Q Code and Morse","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/telecomunicaciones","x":"The Q code used by radio amateurs, the international phonetic alphabet, the Morse code tree and a key trainer to practice with."},{"t":"🐢 The Logo Turtle","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/tortuga","x":"Program; A square: four times forward and turn 90° repeat 4 [forward 150 right 90] ▶ Draw ■ Stop Clear screen speed The Draw button clears the screen and runs the program. The command center continues from where the turtle was left and knows the procedures in the program. x 0 y 0 heading 0° pen down Worked examples Ordered from the simplest to the hardest. Each one explains the new idea that appears; the button loads it into the program and draws it. Afterwards, try changing the numbers and see what happens: that is how you learn Logo. The commands The screen is 600 steps wide, with the origin"},{"t":"Bipolar transistors: an interactive course","d":"Tool","u":"/en/publicaciones/transistores-bipolares","x":"Interactive course on bipolar transistors: how they work, characteristic curves, gain, load line, the transistor as a switch and as an amplifier, biasing, regulated power supplies, common emitter and common collector, and the Darlington pair."},{"t":"The workshop","d":"Page","u":"/en/taller","x":"The workshop What came out while I was building the rest. None of this is electronics and all of it is used right here, in the browser. ♟️ Chess 🔢 Calculator ✒️ Cursive 🎸 Music \"Omnes sumus in canale, pauci autem ad astra vident\" ✍️ Cursive writing From print to cursive: the basic strokes, the alphabet with animated stroke order, connections and words to trace with your finger or the mouse. With free-form lines and printable templates. Interactive Teaching Open card → Play with a classmate 2 players Challenge the person next to you: you both play on this same screen, taking turns. Pick the clo"}];
